      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>BRIGHT AUTUMN FLOWER.</head>
        <p>A BRIGHT AUTUMN FLOWER.</p>
        <p>Rudbeckia Purpurea, one of the finest and, most distinct perennials in the autumn is au once recognised by its rose-purple flowers on sturdy stems. The plant will grow freely in any ordinary good garden soil, and should b° grouped to get the best effect vom the bold, handsome flowers. It is all Hie more valuable in the autumn, as so many plants season have yellow flowers, which get monoton- ous when they are planted freely. at that</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>ROOT PRUNING.</head>
        <p>ROOT PRUNING.</p>
        <p>How often does one hear growers conaplaining that their fruit trees grow too strongly and 4° not bear any fruit. The only remedy for thl grossness of growth is root pruning in the case °` established trees and lifting in the case of young ones. Now is a good time to carry tihis out, ag if the root pruning and lifting are done while the leaves are still on the trees the formation of new fibres is encouraged, and the trees are able to get a hold of the soil before very severe weather is with us. Opinions vary as to tha value of root pruning, but my experience is thav there is no better and simpler way of checking the exuberant growth one often sees in fraiV trees. I have distinct recollections of havinig, as a lad seen my father toot pruning a via' tree of Marie Louise pear. This had hees planted several years and made very growth, but no fruit was borne. It was then decided to have it root pruned, one side of the tree being operated on one year and the other side the next. The result of this treatment will that in two years the tree began to bear, al!ti heavy crops of fine fruit were forthcoming 2.11 succeeding years. Instances are not wanting 11 my experience in various parts of England ao:', Scotland of the value of toot pruning both w 51,, grown trees and trees in the open.—" Wick," in "Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
