      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>WHAT, THE GENERALS OF ALLIES THINK OF ESPERANTO.</head>
        <p>WHAT, THE GENERALS OF ALLIES THINK OF ESPERANTO.</p>
        <p>General Prion: "The use of Esperanto by the various Red Cross Societies is so essential that we may be sure in a few years the little green star of Esperanto will appear as a symbol of hope beside the distinguishing emblem of the Red Cross."</p>
        <p>General Matton: "Esperanto should be spoken as a second language in the homes of the people." General Langlois : I urn an enthusiastic adherent of Esperanto." General De Lacroix: "The utility ot Bspei- anto is indisputable." General Sebert, himself a fluent Esperantist, advocates it.</p>
        <p>General Baron Oku : "The Esperanto Red Cross guides will be very useful in military circles."</p>
        <p>Count Hayashi: "I should like Esperanto to become the international tongue." General Marquis de Palaviega, of the Spanish Red Cross; General Bailloud and Vice-Admiral Bayle might also be quoted. Colonel Kiffin-Taylor "Every progressive man should know Esperanto." Major Straut (a neutral), of America, says: "Esperanto is entirely suitable as an inter- national language, and will play an important role in future."</p>
        <p>The address of the Secretary of the Warring- ton Esperanto Society is 14, Bewsey-road.</p>
        <p>Try Cockle's Pills next time. Buy a box to-day and have them handy.</p>
      </div>
