      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD</p>
        <p>JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>The Warrington White Heather Committee's advertisement in last week's issue reminded me of 2'</p>
        <p>•.:ch I fancied I detected the</p>
        <p>late</p>
        <p>It occurred in a short</p>
        <p>story whicn A° _wrote for the "Windsor Maga- zine." The scene was laid in the Highlands in February, and in his description of the environ- ment he told of "White heather in bloom." it struck me that for once the careful observer and skilful writer must in this have been mistaken. If it is I who am in error I shall be glad of correction.</p>
        <p>Slips in Literature. The fact is that these are numerous. It is notorious that Defoe made many in "Robinson Crusoe," and that even' Shakespeare, Scott, and Dickens were betrayed into a few. To be sure, the most amazing of these were trifling in com- parison with those of a certain author whom De Quincy criticised as being "wrong as to the time when, wrong as to the place where, wrong as to the manner how, wrong as to the person by whom, and pre-eminently wrong as to the thing which." Of course, Thackeray, Wilkie Collins, Rider Haggard, Mrs. Humphrey Ward, Kingsley, and smaller fry who committed really ludicrous blunders in the way of anachronisms and so forth could none of them fairly -be charged so seriously as that; but such solecisms as I have noted in my. reading are very amusing. I once collected sufficient for an evening with Et Literary Society, and since then I have dis- covered as many more. Readers will often notice such slips in' the Press. For example, only the other week it was pointed out that the erudite and scrupulously exact Mr. Archer, who pretty frequently contributes to the "Daily News," had in one article attributed to our Lord a statement actually made by John the Baptist. As I have hinted, I have material for the almost 'indefinite multiplication of such instances; but there is a proverb warning the dweller in a glass house against stone-throwing. As the good Bishop observed, we are none of us in- fallible, not even the youngest. Historical Accuracy.</p>
        <p>I know the objection to the sort of thing above indicated, and I hasten to assure all and sundry that I have not searched the classics with an eye to trivial errors, though I have found it interesting to "when found, make a pote of." This has its justifications and its uses, moreover. In a short introduction to a new publication of Richardson.'s "Pamela," Professor Saintsbury has a poke at "illiterate people" who 14fer to " Clarissa " as "Clarissa Harlowe "•, nevertheless, the bibliography following this 'gives the name of the second of Ricliardson's works as " Clarissa Harlowe "! The organisers of the last great military tour- nament claimed to have pursued "historical research for accurate detail." Surely that im- plied a challenge, and Professor Haverfield promptly and good-naturedly directed their attention to the indisputable fact that they had introduced into their scene of the Triumph of the Emperor ClaudiuS one person who never existed. another who was dead before Claudius invaded Britain. and a Sixth Legion which did not come to Britain until 80 years after Claudius.</p>
        <p>Prepared to Stay (?) At last week's meeting of the trade union delegates, Mr. Elwell, who had previously deprecated such prolongation of the proceed- ings, eventually announced that he would stay all night if necessary. The incident brought to my mind a somewhat similar scene which it was my lot to report of in the long bygones. There was a saeme for the introduction of horse-racing into the town in which I then lived. This touched the Nonconformist con- science, and a public meeting for indignant pro- test was called in the largest hall. The enemy attended in force, and pretty well packed the place, and the promoters of the gathering were hopelessly beaten on the ground which they had chosen. The latter's champion was a renownad Doctor of Divinity accustomed to' respectful hearing, but his powerful voice was drowned in the howls of the rowdies. Ultimately he pulled out his watch, and, taking Advantage of a momentary lull, declared his intention of say- ing what he had to say even though he had to stand there all night. Upon that the uproar broke out afresh, and, as I have intimated, the meeting had to be abandoned without any motion being submitted: in fact, the Platform was stormed and speedily cleared. However, the whirligig of time brought its revenge, for .not many years after their establishment the races had to be given up. the speculation having Proved a ,disastrous failure, involving wealthy local supporters in heavy financial loss. Weary Indeed! I suppose most of us have met preachers (and of, course others) who with all their enviable gifts did not happen to be endowed with humour. I think of one who called upon the choir to sing the anthem, "Let the earth rejoice," and then inflicted upon his long-suffering hearers a 55 minutes' sermon which set at least one wonder- ing why we had been exhorted to " rejoice " by anticipation—though his good-humour was quickly restored as the innocent occupant of the pulpit solemnly and unsuspectingly an- nounced as the closing hymn—" Art thou weary?" That was many years ago, and the recurrence of the harvest anthem in a local paragraph in last week's issue reminded me of it. Nicknames. •</p>
        <p>The death of " Owd Toss" set me ruminating again on this subject, and I ran over the lists of nicknames which I have compiled in Ow various districts in which I have lived. They are fear- ful and wonderful indeed. The owners of some of them never answered to any but their nick- names. Others there were, of course, who strongly resented the liberty; but these were a small minority. In many cases it was impos- sible to distinguish individuals without refer- ence to bynames. I knew of one community that consisted almost wholly of Greenwoods; in a neighbouring place the inhabitants were mostly Barkers and Sutcliffes; and so we had " Jooa-o'-better-Sam's " and " Jooa-o'-war- Sam's "—and, by-the-bye (so much for heredity) the latter was a " holy terror." An ordinary, detent sort of nickname Was accepted as neces- sary and unavoidable; but when naughty boys cried "Billy Jackass" after one in .the street—well, needless to add, the person so identified didn't like it, and there were others who also did not think it was nice.</p>
        <p>" Good Health." • At the annual dinner the tenants of the Walton Estate drank Sir Gilbert Greenall's health. The origin of the custom is obscure. Camden wrote—" The English in their long wars in the Netherlands, first learned to drown them- selves with immoderate drinking and by drink- ing others' healths to impair their own." Another old writer says that when the Danes ruled the land they used to take occasion of an Englishman's drinking to stab him. Hence an Englishman about to drink would ask his neighbour at table to be his surety or pledge. Hence our custom of pledging one another, which, begun in necessity, has grown to be a general compliment. Then and Now.</p>
        <p>I have just been reading the extract in the Farm column showing that, according to an ex- pert, most farm work can be done by women; and we all know what women are doing ireother callings, including the making of munitions. Imagine, therefore, how I gasped and rubbed my eyes when, turning up an old copy of the "Book Monthly," I read therein—" A man's work and a woman's are quite aistinct and different. It is so from the cradle to the grave, and no amount of codeducation can alter it." Howe'er, that be, it seems to me the war has completely altered it.</p>
        <p>Want Holidays Observed On Saturday. Another reform is under way in America. It is that of the Saturday Full Holiday National Movement, which has just opened headquarters in Newark, N.J., and which has for its object the celebration of every legal holiday, except Christmas and New Year's, on a Saturday. The advocates of the movement would have such days as Washington's Birthday, Memorial Day, Independence Day and Labour Day cele- brated on the Saturday nearest to the date on which they fall. The scheme also provides for eight Saturday full holidays throughout the country during the summer months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 21, 1916. MAYORAL EVENING.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 21, 1916. MAYORAL EVENING.</p>
        <p>Silver Centre-Piece For Alder- man And Mrs. Peacock.</p>
        <p>MILITARY TRIBUNALS.</p>
        <p>COMBING OUT THE SINGLE</p>
        <p>BABY DAUGHTER LOOKS I Successful Applications By The ON SWEETLY. Military Representative. • Balanced in the arms of her nurse seated in The principal feature of Tuesday's sitt of</p>
        <p>the Council Chamber on Tuesday evening, Marion, with the eyes of wonder becoming a child a few months old, saw her father and mother, the Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs. Peter Peacock) receive in commemora- tion of her advent into the ,world, during their term of Office, a handsome combination silver centre piece, the gift of the Town Councillors and officials of the Corporaticin. It had been whispered that, as was custom- ary on such occasions, the presentation would take the form of a silver cradle, but, the Mayor, in a felicitous speech, explained the reason for the change. ' It is a happy and singular co- incidence that two of the four children born during their parents' mayoralty, arrived , next door to each other—Alderman Peacock's at 47, Wilson Patten-street, and Alderman Wright's at No 45. But for the hall wall the youngster in No. 47 could have the perpetual delight of observing the shine of the silver cradle in No. I 45' It was a pleasant presentation ceremony, celebrated after the Council business had been transacted, and the usual resolution of testi- mony and singularly high appreciations of the services of the Mayor and Mayoress had been accorded. When the Mayor took the chair be presided over an ususual assembly, the Coun- cillors having a surround of ladies interested in Me proceedings. Conspicuously stationed so as to miss nothing was the baby, and on two occasions when it began to whimper, after the manner of all children, it was ejected from the Chamber, the nurse ushering it out once and the Mayoress the second time. That it shouid have to submit to this indignity was essential to the dignity of the Council meeting. The baby and Alderman Tinnion cried together. Miss Marion was tearful and the Alderman cried, " Order ! Order !" The Mayor thumped his bell as in the days of pandemonium when the rest of the world was at peace. Miss Marion still , protested and was carried out, and the dreadful- ness of her conduct having been explained to her thoroughly, she was readmitted, and throughout the presentation ceremony remained perfectly decorous; in fact, she frequently smiled sweetly.</p>
        <p>the local Tribunal was the number of cases in which the military representative (Captain Mathams) objected to any further exemptions, and applied for review of the circumstances. Dr. Joseph (Deputy Mayor) and Messrs. F. A. Frost and W. Bradbury formed the Tribunal. A Baker's Son. Captain Mathams asked the Tribunal to review the case of a baker's son, aged 21, single, who had been granted exemption, on the ground that he is no longer employed in a certified occupation. The young man's father stated that they were doing five men's work between them. There were originally five rounds each morning, and when their men were taken, the horses were sold, a motor van bought, and the rounds split up into two. He and his son had also to turn in with the men in the bakehouse. They worked until eight or nine o'clock at night, and also on Sunday. Two of the bakers are. exempted men, and a third is a man over mili- tary age.—The Chairman said the Tribunal could not get over the fact of this young man's age-21, single. He • had already had a long exemption', and they would be straining a point by allowing until the end of November. Partner In House Furnishing Business. A partner in a house furnishing firm, having two places of business, and who is 33 years of age and single, appeared to answer the asser- tion of the military representative that it was now possible to be carried .on in his absence. He had been granted exemption in March. His- case was that 'he -does all the business of, one f the shops, the rent of which is £lOO year; with a lease of five years to run. He has to do the buying, serve the customers and even deliver the goods lie sells. He had been passed for garrison duty, and at present gave three nights each week as a Special Constable.—One month's exemption was granted. Short Exemption For Business Partner. Mr. T. S. Steel appeared for a single man, aged 30, partner in a firm of milliners and drapers, who had, been provisionally exempted in January last, and whose case was brought up for review. The evidence was heard in prixate, and the Tribunal granted one month's exemp- tion.</p>
        <p>Timber Merchants' Appeal.</p>
        <p>Messrs. It. A. Naylor, Ltd., timber mer-, chants, sought exemption for eleven employes and a representative of the firm gave evidence as to the character of the work and the useful- ness of the men employed.—The Tribunal granted conditional exemption in the cases •)f seven men, and dismissed the remaining four. One of the latter was, however, granted exemption on a personal appeal. Brushmaker's Indispensability.</p>
        <p>Regarding exAmption for a brushmaker, the employer stated, in reply to Captain 3,,tathams' objection, that the, particular class of work on which the man was employed could not be done by women, who could only turn out the cheaper class of goods. Acting on the advice of the Board of Trade and in order to capture German trade, the, business had been extended; but if this man were taken the work could not be done.—Mr. Frost asked if blind soldiers were not being taught to make brushes, and appli- cant said that a blind soldier could not bore the brush or trim it. A man with one leg would be of more use than a blind man.—Conditional exemption was allowed. Women Window Cleaners' Wages.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Window Cleaning Company appealed on behalf of a married man, aged 35, who had been granted exemption to the 31st August in order that the firm should try end get someone else in his place. A representative of the firm stated that he had made all arrangements to engage a man .who had been rejected on medical examination. Since then the man had keeßimsed for service. Applicant Said that he had 'price lost two men and a boy, and all the girls , who had been employed. The latter prove a- unsatisfactory. Captain Mathams: How? , They did not turn 1113 to the work; they just L.. pleased themselves. Did you offer them the same wages as men?— No.</p>
        <p>, Then what could you expect?—You could not iF hive th the same as men. They can't do the work. takes two months when they have to be taug t.</p>
        <p>In reply to further questions by Captain Mathams, the representative of the firm said they paid the girls 15s. fr,wyek to start, and the</p>
        <p>_ men were paid so much per hour. The latter could make anything up to £2 a week, and there was overtime. At present they were paying two boys who were not seventeen years " old, 275. 6d. a week.</p>
        <p>The application was refused. " Tater " Pies' Chef.</p>
        <p>The proprietor of a bakery and eating house in Market-street, appealed for exemption for his son, aged 31, and unmarried. He stated that he carried on the business of a caterer and confectioner, and carried on trade amongst the working classes. They made ," tater pies" and supplied dinners to workpeople throughout the day. Applicant ,had not done any baking him- self for three years, and produced a medical certificate showing that he was unfit for work at the present time.—The Chairman said the Tribunal thought that these pies could be made by women, but granted a month's exemption in order that applicant could mitke other arrange- ments.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>PICTURESQUE OCCASION.</head>
        <p>A PICTURESQUE OCCASION.</p>
        <p>The speech of the Deputy Mayor (Councillor Joseph), in whose hands the act of presentlng the silver lay, was worthy of the- occasion. lie said it was refreshing in the midst of this national gloom, to have a momentary glimpse of a bright family episode, particularly when it occurred in the home circle of the chief citizen and his wife, and it was but fitting, after the strenuous manner in which the Mayor had de- voted himself to the interests of the town, that he should have some little compensation in the shape of that presentation. He could truthfully say that the presentation of -that beautiful emblem of good will and respect to the Mayor and Mayoress proceeded from the hearts of the übscribers. (Hear, hear.) To the little one, the innocent cause of that picturesque function, he would say that it was given to very few to be born haying for their parents the chief citizen and his wife. He fervently hoped that ,the future progress and usefulness in life of their daughter would coincide to the utmost with the auspiciousness of her birth. The gift was indi- cative of the feelings, not only of the Council and officials, but of the general- public of the borough. (Applause.) -in returning thanks, the Mayor remarked uppn the happy thought which led the sub- scribers to make a departure from the usual fbrm of gift—a silver cradle. The Mayoress and he were delighted that something had been chosen which would not only be a source of pride and gratification to them and to their daughter when she grew up, but which mould be in almost, daily use. Remarking on the pre- sence of the ladies, his Worship smilingly obServed that he ltoptd the time was not' far distant when they would have lady councillors— (Alderman Tinnion: "No, no ")—to help the present members in their important duties, (Laughter and applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>THANKS TO THE MAYOR</head>
        <p>THANKS TO THE MAYOR</p>
        <p>Who, In Reply, Alludes to Anonymous</p>
        <p>Letters.</p>
        <p>The duty of proposing the adoption: of a resolution of thanks to the Mayor and Mayoress for their services during the year was entrusted to Alderman Bennett, whose speech was con- tinually marked by expressions of approbation by Councillors. Alderman Bennett said that seldom, if ever, in the history_of Warrington, had the import- ant and responsible duties which attached 1- the position of chief magistrate been per- formed in a manner so entirely satisfactory tc the inhabitants of the town, and never before had those duties been so exacting or so onerous. (Hear, hear.) Apart from the usual extraneous duties of the position, which seemed to grow from year to year, the war had imposed a multi- tude of added buraens, and, if any man in War- rington could say that he had "done his bit," it was the present Mayor. (Applause.) It was a constant source of wonder how he had been able to stand the strain, and he had borne it in the shadow of the knowledge that one of his sons had been in constant peril at the front. Moreover, in the stress and tumult of it all his courtesy and tact had been invariable, and he had set an example of impartiality which was specially to be appreciated at a time when we were forgetting our old feuds and workiw strenuously together for the common good. His term of office had been unique in many ways. He was, for example, the only Mayor who had ever been asked to accept the post for four years in succession. That was a precedent which could only be justified upon the strongest grounds. It was a very exceptional honour, but the exceptional honour involved exceptional responsibility, exceptional sacrifice, and excep- tional hard work. (Hear, hear.) In all that he bad said he wished to include the Mayoress, who had won the affection of every member of the Council. (Applause.) Alderman Tinnion, in seconding, said the Mayor and Mayoress had risen to the 'occasion and had fulfilled their duties with credit to themselves and honour to the town. (Ap- plause.)</p>
        <p>Councillor Pemberton expressed the opinion that Warrington had never had a Mayor who had given so much time for the benefit of the town. It was a constant wonder to all of them how he had managed to get through all the work which had to be done. The Mayoress also had done her duty nobly and had worked very hard in assisting her husband. He hoped that before another year of office was over peace would have been declared, and that they would all be in at the flag-waving. (Applause.) Councillor Henshall declared that the Mayor had not only given his time and sympathy to the public good, but himself. Where he might have been content to send a letter, he had sent himself, and his consideration and generosity had always been appreciated. (Applause.) The resolution having been carried with acclamation, the Mayor replied that when he accepted the office he did not under-rate the responsibilities and obligations which would devolve upon him. The past two years had been an extraordinarily anxious time for him— as, indeed, for everybody—but he had done his utm:tst to please Cle inhabitants. Whilst he migur it t. ii .-..eeded in every direction, he ;1:.: sdy that he had straightfor- c• wardly lowe I t policy which his conscience told him was light. Of course, he could not profess to satisfy everybody, but he made it his purpose to give satisfaction to the greatest num- ber possible.</p>
        <p>He had had letters from time to time stating that he had not satisfied everybody, and thought it would be well, in passing, to state that the other day he received an anonymous lettbi.. Ile generally treated such communications with the contempt they deserved, but just to show the difficulties of anyone who held that position, in the present circumstances, he would just refer to this one. It would perhaps be as well for the public to know that there was no great salary attaching to the position. Alderman Tinnion : I hofie there -vwer -ill 1)-.</p>
        <p>The Mayor added that when Lady Jelliene came to Warrington on Friday last, he thought, almost at the last moment, it would perhaps be</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>PATRIOTISM BEFORE CONVENIENCE.</head>
        <p>PATRIOTISM BEFORE CONVENIENCE.</p>
        <p>Crosfield's Not Appealing For Single Men.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd., having .refrained from appealing for four single men, the National Union of Dock Labourers pressed the Tribunal at the Town Hall on WedneSday morning, for exemption, but unavailingly. Mr. Fred Hodson, of the Union, explained that the men were aged 35, 26, 25 and 24 year, and were engaged in the transport section, mainly on the boats. Two of them had been originally rejected, but now passed; another was a time-expired man. "Do Crosfield's support the claim?" inquired Alderman Tinnion.--" No," replied Mr. Hodson. Aldffman Tinnion: Surely Crosfield's would feel the shortage if they went and .therefore, if they wanted the men they would appeal for them. • •</p>
        <p>Mr. Hodson: The firm have refused to appeal on patriotic grounds. They won't appeal for any single men. Our instructions are to appeal for these men as the others claim that without them they cannot get through the work, as so many men are required for each boat. Captain Mathams: As the firm which directly employs them does not appeal, I submit that they can be dispensed with. Appeals refused. Deputy Mayor, and the Amazons.</p>
        <p>In an application for the exemption of three assistants in a provision shop, the representa.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>MAYORAL EVENlNG—continued.</head>
        <p>MAYORAL EVENlNG—continued.</p>
        <p>acceptable if he provided light refreshments at the Town Hall for 200 or 300 people. The letter he had referred •to came from an apparently educated person, whoa pointed out that he (the Mayor) had "his own particular friends,and the Bumbledom of Warrington" on the Town Hall steps, and that he ought to have arranged for everybody outside to be there, instead of reserv- ing that favoured position for a few. (Laugh- ter.) He rather pitied the unfortunate in- dividual who thought it proper to complain on such an occasion, and about such a trivial mat- ter. On the other hand he had received hun- dreds of letters thanking him for the services he had rendered to the town. -(Applause.) There- fore, he hoped that, while he endeavoured to tive satisfaction to the greatest number, any of those who felt inclined to be dissatisfied would remember that he always followed the policy which he was persuaded was in the best interests of the town. . (Applause.) The proceedings aptly terminated with the singing of "God save the King."</p>
        <p>THE LATCHFORD CADET CORPS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <p>tive remarked upon the difficulties of carrying on the business. "We want to get through the work," he said, and the Chairman (Dr. Joseph) broke in with: "Not through the war !"</p>
        <p>The Representative: We want to carry on as well as win the war. Re added that one of the men, who was single, had had two brothers killed, and had another brother in the Navy. It. was suggested that women might do the work of one of the men, and the representative replied that they could not do it. The Chairman: There are some strong women going about. The Regresentative: You want brains As well as muscle in the provision trade. T4e single man was granted three months and the others conditional exemption. "See what you can do in the finding of mus- cular ladies," was the advice of the Chairman to the representative. Expert Seedsmen.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Gartons, seed merchants, applied for two single men, and Mr. Pearson, to illustrate the expertness of the work, produced various species of seeds which were apparently of the same kind for all laymen could tell. If the seeds got mixed it would mean a loss of hun- dreds or pounds to the firm, and would be very disastrous to the farmers because of the losses they would sustain. One of the men had been in the Territorials and discharged as medically unfit in October of 1914, but had since been re- examined and passed for 81. The Tribunal decided to inspect the work of the men before coming to a decision.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>Paint-maker.</head>
        <p>Paint-maker.</p>
        <p>Three months' exemption was given to the foreman of the Warrington branch of Messrs. A. H. Davies, Ltd., paint manufacturers. He is single and aged 38 years. Most of the paint is used on iron and steel.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>Tribunal's Astonishment.</head>
        <p>Tribunal's Astonishment.</p>
        <p>"What, 22 years old, single, and bodged!" ex- claimed the Chairman, when a man employed at Messrs. Rylands Bros., Ltd., made a domestic appeal.</p>
        <p>-Questions Were asked the young man about the nature of his employment, and it was Alderman Tinnion's turn to exclaim, "Twenty- two, and badged for shifting wire about!" The Chairman: It is downright mockery. The domestic claim was then considered. It appears that his mother is a widow, a brother is in France, a sister is earning 12s. a week, and there are three children dependent. His wages. averaged from 245. to 30s. weekly. The Clerk (Mr. A. T.- Hallaway): How much do you pay your mother?</p>
        <p>The young man replied that the amount varied according to the SUM he arned, and was then asked how much he generally kept for him- self. Silent for a longer time than usual, the applicant was again plied with the question. He asked whether the question was necessary. The Chairman (sharply) : No unnecessary questions are put here.</p>
        <p>Applicant: I usually keep Bs. a week. It was pointed ouf that as a brother had made an allotment to the mother he could make one to a sister.</p>
        <p>The Chairman 'said: "With regard to your work at Rylands, the Tribunal are very much surprised to find that a young unmarried man of 22 years is badged when we have to send to the Front married mefi who have families. Tour personal claim is refused. We cannot touch the badge—at present." Declaring that only young men could do the work 'because of their having to stand in water, the Firth Company applied for the exemption of a single man aged 20 years. Old men could not stand the water, said the representative. The Chairman: They stand whisky and water better. (Laughter.) The representative explained that the man had been rejected before and, therefore, they did not apply for a badge; otherwise, they would have got one. The Chairman: We really cannot allow this man to pass.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>Two More Singles.</head>
        <p>Two More Singles.</p>
        <p>The appeal of a single man aged 19 years on the ground that his father could not work, was dismiwd. He has. a brother, aged 28 years? "single, "Mid badged. An adjournment was ordered in the case of a single man, aged 24 years, who had been un- badged at Messrs. Rylands Bros., Ltd. He sup- ports his mother and brother, who are both in delicate health.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>FARM HANDS EXEMPTED.</head>
        <p>FARM HANDS EXEMPTED.</p>
        <p>Tannery Firm Complimented For Assisting Recruiting.</p>
        <p>The decision of, the Army Council as to the calling up of agricultural labourers was re- ferred to in the first case which came before the Warrington Rural Tribunal on Wednesday, Mr. George Mather presiding. It was an ap- plication on behalf of a young man who had been granted exemption to the end of October. He had now been called, up by the military authorities, and the question arose as to whe- ther the Tribunal should grant leave for a further appeal. The members were under the impression that no agricultural worker would be called up before the Ist of January. Communication having been made with the recruiting officials at the Police Station by the military representative, the Tribunal was in- formed that if the "calling-up" papers were brought to the office they would be cancelled until the 31st December.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. S. Steel represented a Martinscroft farmer who appealed for exemption until the end of December on behalf of his son, a single man, aged 32 years. The farm is one of 49 acres and has been kept in constant cultiva- tion.—The Military Representative (Mr. Fur- ness) pointed out that in addition to the farmer himself, who is 61 years of age, there are three men on the farm, one a married man, aged 39, with four children; another son, aged 36, mar- ried and with three children; and the son who was the subject of the present application. The military thought that the unmarried son should go.—Temporary exemption was granted to 31st December.</p>
        <p>In several other cases where farmers applied for exemption for men employed on the land time was granted until the end of the year.</p>
        <p>Tannery Employes.</p>
        <p>The Tribunal took up a list of cases touching employes of the Orford Tannery and which had been adjourned for further consideration. Mr. Gough, one of the directors of the firm, attended and explained that nineteen men had been de-badged. He had applied for a re-issue of badges, but had only got a reply to the effect that the matter is in hand. The firm had always done all they could to help forward recruiting, and while they did not ask the Tribunal to postpone the matter any longer, they asked for a certain amount of time for some of the men, and that Mr. Furness (the military representative) would let them have substitutes for a certain number of other men. Mr. Ball hoped that the substitutes would he drawn from •returned soldiers, and that they would not be taken from the farms. Mr. Gough: You ,cannot call us offenders in that respect.</p>
        <p>Mr. Furness expressed the appreciation of the military authorities for the very generous way in which Mr. Gough had met them. An inspector who had been visiting the tanneries throughout the country told him (Mr. Furness) that no tannery had done so much as the Orford Tannery in releasing men for service, or who were employing so many girls. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>Mr. Gough: On beklf of myself and my co-directors I thank you for the very generous way you look at it.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: We are not a bad lot, you know. (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>, In the cases of two men conditibnal exemp- tion was granted, three were given until the end of November, and fourteen to the end of December.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>ORDERS.</head>
        <p>ORDERS.</p>
        <p>Friday.—Fall-in at 7.30 prompt. Uniform must be worn. Ambulance *Section to parade under orders of Corporal E. Lea; bandaging, &amp;c. Semaphore Class, 8 to 9 p.m. Orderlies.—Lance-Corporal N. Garlick and Cadet T. :Yorke. • J. B. STARKEY, Commanding Officer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>FARM AND GARDEN.</head>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>MORE HOME WHEAT NEEDED.</p>
        <p>The Royal Commission on Wheat Supplies has issued the following statement:—</p>
        <p>Having received numerous inquiries on the subject the Royal Commission on Wheat Sup- plies desire to inform, farmers generally throughout the United 'Kingdom that they will best serve the national interest by sowing as many acres as possible of wheat. The " Times " agricultural correspondent says that this intimation of what is required will be welcomed by farmers, and adds: "There are horses enough, and steam tackle and oil tractors are being employed to the limit of the equipment available, but man-power remains the dominating factor in determining the area cultivated. The Army Council, co-operating with the Board of Agriculture, have recognised this by extending the exe.nption of skilled farm hands till the end of the year. The pur- pose of this ,concession is clear. The duty de- volves upon farmers of turning to the best national account the advantages this respite confers, and the Wheat Commission declare that this consists in growing as large an area of wheat as possible." ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>PRIZE CHEESE PRICE.</head>
        <p>PRIZE CHEESE PRICE.</p>
        <p>At the cheese market held gt Chester last week at the annual show of the Cheshire Dairy Farmers' Association, the first prize cheese was sold for the " record " price.of 150 s. a cwt.; the second prize lots fetched from 1265: to 1355. This is £1 a cwt. above the rates current •last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>GOOD LAW, TOO.</head>
        <p>A GOOD LAW, TOO.</p>
        <p>There is a law in Norway which prohibits any person from cutting down a tree unless he plants three saplings in its place. And a good law too it would be, says "Farm and Home," for many parts of our isles,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>PRICE OF MILK.</head>
        <p>THE PRICE OF MILK.</p>
        <p>The Council of the Cheshire Milk Producers' Association have considered very carefully the following, recommendations :--That it is highly important in the national interests that milk should be sold at as reasonable a price as pos- sible during the coming winter, consistent with both producer and distributor making a fair profit on their business. In order to ensure this, dairy farmers generally are urged not to allow the number of -their milking cows to. de- crease, but to use their best endeavours to pro- duce as much milk as usual, especially in the months of November, December, January and February, as an ample supply is a vital neces- sity, while a shortage in any period would inevitably result in greatly increased prices. There are abundant reasons—in the .very high price of dairy cows, the greatly increased price of feeding stuffs, and the difficulty of obtaining the necessary labotir at even a much higher rate than a year ago—for farmers demanding a price considerably in advance of last winter. The details of increased cost of production have been carefully gone into by persons well quali- fied to deal with the question. The Council have considered the results of these investiga- tions, and are of opinion that in justice to pro- ducers of milk they cannot recommend a less price than is. sd. per Imperial gallon, delivered to Manchester, Liverpool, and adjacent towns, for six months commencing Ist October, 1916, and ending 31st March, 1917. For London, the prike should be Id. ,per gallon more to cover extra cost of carriage. They are of opinion, however, that it would be more business-like for the above-mentioned prices to be varied as follows per Imperial gallon :—October, 19M, is. 3d.; November, 1916, is. 5d.; December, 1916, is. 6d.; Aanuary, 1917, is. 6d.; February, 1917, is. 6d.; March, 1917, is. sd. The Council fur- ther recommend that wherever possible shorter contracts be made, say for two months at a time, as the whole situation is one of consider- able uncertainty. The special circumstances which prevail in some districts, where even quantities are delivered day by day, where the milk has to be delivered very early, and where the rate of wages is exceptionally high, may make it necessary for Is. 6d. per gallon for six months to be charged, but the Council venture to hope that such eases will be rare.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>CHESHIRE'S AID FOR ALLIES.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE'S AID FOR ALLIES.</p>
        <p>A cheque for £2,500, being a first instalment of the proceeds of the recent gift sale at Chester organised by the Cheshire Agricultural Society, has been received at the headquarters of the Agricultural Relief of Allies Fund (16, Bedford- square, London, W.C.). The sale, which was opened by Katharine, Duchess of Westminster, is expected to realise fully £3,000 by the time the accounts are completed. Lancashire arid Cheshire farmers have jointly raised £15,000 for the fund for helping to replace the farmers 4 f our allies On their lands. Kent fanners are second on the list with £8,094.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>PLANTING OUT CARNATIONS.</p>
        <p>There is no better time for planting out than from the middle of September to the same time in October, as then the layers have ample time to make some fresh root-growth- while the ground is still warm and before the heavy and cold rains of November come on. Of colfrse, if the weather is open, planting can be done later than the period mentioned above, but there is a risk, as should very severe weather, with frost and thaws, set in after planting has been completed the plants are loosened before they have got a hold of the soil, and ultimately perish unless their condition is recognised and remedied by treading the soil round them, and making them firm again. In districts where the soil is very heavy and cold, and the layers%adly rooted, it is advisable to put them into pets, those three inches in ,diameter being the best, using a composition of pure loam and sand. Stand them in a frame which should be freely ventilated when the weather is mild. Be care- ful with the watering during the winter and cover with mats during severe frost. Plant out at the end of March or early in April.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>HELPING THE FRUIT TREES.</head>
        <p>HELPING THE FRUIT TREES.</p>
        <p>This is the time to lay the foundation of a good crop of fruit next season by giving the roots of the trees something to feed on so long as they remain active, and for them to find. ready directly they begin to- move in the spring. It is foolish to expect the trees to bear fine fruit year after year unless the roots are helped .in some way. There is not the least. doubt that the diseased and worn-out barren trees one so often sees are due to starvation. The strain of heavy cropping is very great, and unless some assistance in the way of rich top dressings, or applying liquid manure, is given, a falling off, not only in the quantity. but also in the quality of the produce will inevitably follow.</p>
        <p>ing kinds early in the year and plant them out in May have good reason to congratulate them- selves on the 'fine display of flowers. At one time growers used to complain that there 19119 a lack of bright colours, but this has bee° remedied, and we now have several early sortS, the flowers of which are quite as brilliant as in the later flowering varieties. , When the dahlias have been blackened by the frost the flowers of these will be valuable as a sslikht frost seems to do them but little if any harm.-- " Wick," in Farm and Home."</p>
