      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>EARLY CHRYSANTHEMUMS.</head>
        <p>EARLY CHRYSANTHEMUMS.</p>
        <p>Now that the dull October days when the beauty of the garden is on the wane, are with us, it is pleasing to turn to the early flowering chrysanthemums, many of which are now in bloom in the open. In order ,to see these at their best they should be grown naturally with- out any disbudding. In this way sprays contain- ing five or Six blooms may be had. Another valuable characteristic of these October flower.: ing chrysanthemums is their lasting properties when cut. Those readers who were fortunate enougl</p>
        <p>to secure plants of the best early flower-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>Itttus toot abitor</head>
        <p>Itttus toot abitor</p>
        <p>DICKENS'S POPULARITY.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Your contributor, "An Old Journalist," a sound and staunch Dickensian, is perhaps mistaken in suggesting that Dickens "has many more admirers among old fellows like myself than he has in the later generation." If ,we are to believe the publishers, the demand for the novelist's works continues undiminished—and that demand certainly does not emanate from the (fine old) readers who have already possessed copies half a -century. As to those which are passing through the Camps Library, their name is Legion; and they are being read by our fighting men everywhere. Somewhat similar statistics recently prepared by our Russian friends demonstrate that of English writers, Dickens takes the lead, closely pursued by H. G. Wells (who ivith him, of course, has many points in common) ; while The Dickens Fellow- ship, whose branches extend to every part of the English-speaking world, finds its driving forces largely in those upon the "right " side of middle age—although it, is to be admitted many of its counsellors are associated with the past great literary generation. I trust your contributor will welcome this dissension. SYDNEY JEFFERY, London.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>LATE SWALLOW.</head>
        <p>A LATE SWALLOW.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l write to let you know that at dusk on October 16th I picked up a swallow that had just alighted. The bird must have 'flown a 'consider- able distance, as it was quite exhausted, and allowed me to place my hand over it, without trouble. I took it home, and liberated it early the following morning, when it departed swiftly, seemingly none the worse for its previous day's experience. I should like to have your opinion as to whether it is rather an unusual occurrence for the swallow to helound in this country so late in the year.</p>
        <p>J. R. SCOTT 14, Samnel-street, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>PADGATE COTTAGE HOAIES.</head>
        <p>PADGATE COTTAGE HOAIES.</p>
        <p>An Excellent Report Of A Satisfactor</p>
        <p>Year.</p>
        <p>• Mrs.- A. E. Lowrance, the superintendent of the Padgate Cottage Homes, in her annual report, which was submitted to the Warrington 'Board of Guardians on Wednesday, stated that the work and the industrial training in the school had been favourably reported upon; and the majority of children who had left the insti- tution were known to be doing well in their -situations. There were 107 old boys on the roll of honour, some of whom had given their lives for their - country. During the winter Months the children spent happy evenings in the school- room, and in the summer the sports were .greatly enjoyed. The training of the children in trades, household work, gardening and hus- 'bandry had gone on successfully. The health of the children had -been excellent; the hospital 'had not been called into use. Mrs. Lowrance concluded with a word of praise for her staff. 3.4.5 s Broadbent moved the adoption of the report with an expression of _appreciation of the services of the superintendent. She wis very pleased with the way in which the work was going on. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell seconded, and said the state of things at the homes was most satis- factory. The resolution was adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>WORKHOUSE MILK CONTRACT. Farmer On Increasing Demand On Rising Market.</head>
        <p>WORKHOUSE MILK CONTRACT. Farmer On Increasing Demand On Rising Market.</p>
        <p>Mr. Broom;, farmer, Preston Brook, the con- tractor for the supply of milk to the Warring- ton Union Workhouse, has written to the Guar4iAns with reference to the present cost of production, and suggesting a revision of the Price. When he took the contract, he explained, the estimated quantity he had to supply was 9,200 dozen gallons. He had already supplied 4,888, and if the present rate were maintained he would be called upon to supply something like 12,250 gallons in the year. In the first thirteen • weeks, when milk was plentiful, he had to supply only 160 dozen; now it was scarze he was supplying at the rate of 260 dozen. The Increase in the cost of producing milk had been so*great that he. was suffering financial loss. During the last few weeks he had had to spend a considerable amount on dairy cows to keep up the supply, .and he would. have to spend 'a -further £l,OOO for,the same purpose. Cows now cost £45, and the cost of feeding stuffs had in- creased tremendously. "It iA a serious thing for me," he concluded. The Clerk (Mr. A. Bottomley) • having read the letter, suggested that it be sent to the Finance Committee for their consideration. Mr. Geo. Mather; With the terms of the con- tract.</p>
        <p>The Chairman (Mr. A. J. Jolley): I think it ought to be considered Mr. Mather: I don't think so—and I am n farmer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>POOR GAS.</head>
        <p>THE POOR GAS.</p>
        <p>Reasons Why Citizens Should Not Grumble.</p>
        <p>Complaints as to the poor lighting obtained from gas in the borough were made .by Coun- cillors Broadhurst and Gough at a meeting of the Town Council on Tuesday evening, a„nd interesting and sufficing explanation was given by Alderman Tinnion.</p>
        <p>Two of Councillor Gough's remarks were that there should be a fair saleable gas, and a t the present time that was not the case; and through the bad lighting it was almost a danger to walk along the streets. Alderman Tinnion replied that at one time the Corporation was compelled to supply gas at 16 candle power, but the Ministry of Munitions had stipulated that Certain ingredients should be taken out for the purpose of making high explosives. He hoped, therefore, that those who stayed at home would be willing to give up some , little thing, so that they would end the war As ' quickly as possible and sate the lives of those at the Front. He admitted, however, that the light was poor, being only of about 11 candle power. But the gas, when used for heating purposes, was as good as any to be /seen else- where. With regard to the lighting of the streets, he would rather walk into a lamp-post than have the town lighted in a manner which would attract Zeppelins. - (Hear, hear.) Councillor George Dale remarked that seeing the purpose for which the absent ingredients of illuminative gas were being employed, if would not matter if people had to sit in semi-darkness. (Hear, hear.) They would all be willing to do it. He recommended the use of incandescent mantles in order to get good results at the pre-- Sent time.</p>
        <p>Councilor Pemberton thought they ought gladly to submit to the matter.</p>
        <p>And all the Councillors agreed, excepting Councillors Broadhurst and Gough, who raised their hands in support of a technical amend; ment. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>OLD FOLKS.</head>
        <p>THE OLD FOLKS.</p>
        <p>Worst Payers Of Rates In The Borough.</p>
        <p>Commenting upon the collection of the borough rate for the half-year, Mr. W. H. Woodcock states that "the very small amount of loss and the greatly reduced 'arrears bear , evidence to the fact that the trade of the town ' has been good; and that all classes of the com- munity have been apparently prosperous and in receipt of adequate incomes to discharge their obligations to the rate collector without undue strain upon their resources. There are, of course, certain exceptions to this rule, and I have found more difficulty with elderly private residents than with any other class of ratepayer. Some of the works are carrying out extensive building operations, notably Rylands Bros., Ltd.; Pearson and Knowles, Ltd., ' and the Whitecross Co., Ltd. Apart from these, there is practically no building going on, and the scarcity of dwelling-houses continues. The operation of the 'lncrease of Rent and Mort- gage Interest Act, 1915,' has almost entirely pre- vented any increase in rents during the half- year, and very little cottage property is, there- fore, changing hands. It is interesting to noce here that, for the first time in my experience, there has not been a single new dwelling-house added to the rate."</p>
        <p>The amount collected, he adds, was £80,705 15s. 2d., and 'the arrears carried 'forward are £77 Is. Bd., of which only £9 4s. sd. is in respect of this half-year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>ROPE ENOS.</head>
        <p>ROPE ENOS.</p>
        <p>Subject Of Legislation At The County</p>
        <p>Court.</p>
        <p>An interesting case as to a consignment .of "rope ends," which, it was stated, can be used in the manufacture of paper, was heard on Thursday before Judge Barnard Lailey, at the County Court.</p>
        <p>Messrs. John H. Fleming and Co. rope and twine makers, of Arpley-street, Warrington, sued Messrs. Butterworth and Co., marine store, dealers, of Waterloo-road, Liverpool, for the Sum of £9 9s. 9d., value of oiled jute rope ends, alleged to be sold to the latter by agreement. Mr. Arthur Browne, solicitor, appeared for the plaintiffs, and Mr. Batham (Messrs. Robert Davies and Co.),represented the defendant firm. The defence was a repudiation of the contract on the ground that the goods, as supplied, were not in accordance with the sample submitted. Mr. Browne said his clients had about four tons of oiled jute rope ends in hand, and that Mr. Fleming, jun., went to Liverpool and gave the defendant firm's manager, Mr. Holt, a sample, and told him that they ivanted £3 a ton for them. The manager promised to report to his superior and let Mr. Fleming have a reply by letter. Such letter came electing to buy and delivery to be made at the Cheshire Lines Rail- way, Warrington, to defendant's order. The goods were so delivered, but it was found that the defendants had described the goods as "old bagging," and the Cheshire Lines Railway refused to accept them. Telephonic communication took place, with the result that the defendants sent a post- card stating that the goods were oiled jute rope ends. The invoice in which they were described as oiled jute rope ends had never been returned or 'rejected. Correspondence ensued showing that the sub-purchasers from the defendants had refused to receive the goods. Evidence having been called, Mr. Batham submitted the ease for the de- fendants, and argued that the goods, as ordered, had not been supplied according to sample. A quantity of the product was put in evidence and, having reviewed the case, his Honour gave judgment in favour of the defend- ants.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>WOMAN AT HOME.</head>
        <p>WOMAN AT HOME.</p>
        <p>"A woman matured in stupidity is the most intractable material that 'could be found." Thus writes Mary Macarthur in the " Woman at Home and Girl's Realm" for November, in •an article dealing with the problem of the woman of forty. The subject is one that is exciting much attention just now, and Miss Macarthur's pronouncements will be read by all women with the greatest interest. Another striking article is that by Miss Rebecca West, entitled "How. to be Good in War-Time." Those who look for light fare will enjoy the stories by Flora Sidney Woolf, Netta Syrett, and Mrs. Neish, while Annie S. Swan's "Let- ters to a War Bride" strike an entirely new note, giving much useful housekeeping infor- mation in the happiest manner. This number also contains an illustrated article on Lady Beatty, • useful Crochet and Knitting articles, and the usual features beautifully illustrated. There is no better sixpennyworth on the mar- ket than this magazine. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <p>' , l'', I ''.: •:,. ': , ~ • , , .‘• • ' 7 ' - : •' I N '4' 7-T . ~. (Reed. Trade Mark.) COFFEE IMZENCIE The Ce.ce Maid, of Symington's, says: "MY way of making delicious coffee is the f: Symington way. A teaspoonful in a cup of boiling water, ang there you are." A THOS. SYMINGTON 0 CO., 4dia4orgh onal kingdom.</p>
