      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <p>4. 4. . 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 21, 1916.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>WOMAN.9S WORLD 4+ 4 c. _ •</head>
        <p>WOMAN.9S WORLD 4+ 4 c. _ •</p>
        <p>and other rich materials. Several tints of bottle green are likewise favoured. Through. out the summer, scarves of green tulle were almost as popular as black and white, whilst shot green and white silk stockings were quite a feature of summer hose, for wearing with green tailor-mades. Green, too, is the prevailing colour of the popular velour beaver hat for this autumn. The favourite back comb is also green, and has graduated blobs, of jade set in enamel. Next tp green comes brown in every shade, starting with fawn, and including, of course, the still popular tete de negre. Grey and ,biege .and all dark neutral tints will be worn during the winter; black, too, has rather gained than lost in popularity, and black and white is still the favourite combination. To Preserve Plums Whole.</p>
        <p>,To every pound of fruit allow three-quarters of a pound of loaf sugar. To make a thin syrup allow a quarter of a pound of loaf sugar to every pint of water. Select large ripe plums; slightly prick 'them to prevent them bursting, and simmer gently for 20 minutes in a syrup made with the above proportion of sugar and water. Put them carefully into a pan, let the syrup cool, pour it over the plums, and allow them to remain for two days. Having pre- viously weighed the other syrup, dip the plums quickly into water and put them into a pre- serving-pan with no more sugar than hangs about them, and boil the water to a syrup, care- fully skimming it. Drain the plums from the first syrup, put them into the fresh syrup, and simmer for half an hour, or until they are clear. Lift them out singly into pots, pour the syrup over, and when cold cover down to exclude the air. Preserved thus, plums are excellent for dessert. • A!cohol and Motherhood.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Society for the Study of Inebriety, held in the rooms of the Medical Society of London, a paper was read by Dr. J. W. Ballantyne, physician; Edinburgh koyal Maternity Hospital, on the effect of alcohol in relation to antenatal life. Alcohol, he said, falls into line with other poisons, and with toxines infective organisms, and physical agencies as one of the causes of antenatal mor- bid effects. Alcohol produced effects upon the unborn infant 'similar to, and not less unplea- sant than, those arising from lead, from the typhoid germ, or the tubercle bacillus. It was abundantly clear that alcohol was a menace to antenatal life. The expectant mother should refrain from alcohol, or if unable to refrain should be•protected from it for the sake not only of herself but of her offspring. All intend- ing parentsshould remember that they carry in their bodies thd most precious of all earthly things, germ cells; and they should protect these .from all evil influences as they would their own lives, for by doing so they could give a great gift Vi future generations.</p>
        <p>Spinach. _ To cook spinach in the French manner, wash clean and boil half a peck of spinach; when well drained pound it in a mortar, rub through a wire sieve and having placed the pulp in a small stewpan, with a pat of butter, a bit of glaze, a little pepper and salt, and a pinch of sugar, stir all together over the fire until _quite hot, and serve with fried croutons. This is the regulai; French puree; but, a simpler method, equally good, is, after boiling the spinach, to drain it , very thoroughly, and then chop it quite fine on a board, put it in a stewpan with a little milk (or a piece of butter), with pepper and salt, and stir A over the fire for at least five minutes to dry it. "Spinach should be carefully picked over, and all decayed leaves and stalks taken off.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>CHAT and GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>The Lady Private Secretary. Women are still debarred from the higher departments of the Civil Service, and this rather narrows the field for possible careers for girls who have carried off honours at Girton and Newnham. Many of them seek private secretaryships to Members pf Parliament, and some fill very important posts in the political world. Mr. Lloyd George has had a lady secre- tary—Miss F. L. Stevenson—for a number of years, indeed during all his offices as Chancellor of the Exchequer, Minister 04, Munitions, and Secretary for War. Although quite young, Miss Stevenson has ,proved an able organiser and administrator. Perhaps her success may be in part due to her parentage, a Scoitish father and French mother, and that she inherits the best characteristics of the two nationalities. - Hand-made Lace.</p>
        <p>Two unfortunate Queen Consorts were associated—one quite intimately—with the hand- made lace industry of this country. Katherine of Arragon, the first wife of Henry VIII. solaced the weariness of her retirement from Court after her divorce, by teaching the poor women of Kimbolton to make a kind of Spanish lace. The art in time almost died out, but before the war efforts were made to revive it. Although a great deal of lace was made in Spanish con- vents during the Middle Ages, it was not dis- tinctive, but rather copies of Venetian needle- made :work. Princess Henry of Battenberg is a great admirer of lace, and is endeavouring to revive the making of the kind worn by Marie Antoinette of France. The industry was intro- duced in the Isle of Wight, like the making of silk and wool by French refugees, who settled there during the French Revolution. For many years it was a flourishing industry, killed, however, by the introduction of machinery-made lace. The Princess possesses some very fine specimens of this work. Velvet Autumn Suits.</p>
        <p>Velvet and its humble sister, velveteen, are 'to be very much worn this autumn and winter. Some very handsome models are shown which have basques reaching the knees, and in several instances these are even longer, and all have high waists, which naturally accentuate their length, anti the majority are trimmed with fur. Every season coats must differ, and this autumn it has been decrad that changes in the length of basques, the height of collars, and the shape and size of pockets, with the introduction of toilches of rich gold or silver embroidery, are to be the distinguishing features of velvet suits. The metal embroideries are seen on the flaps of pockets and across the fronts of coats, or take the. form of tabs on the shoulders, or again the narrow waistbelt is the chosen place for this handsome decoration. Frocks of fine woollen materials and cloths are largely trimmed and combined with velvet and velveteen, as well as being embellished with touches of fur. Serge and velveteen also combine excellently, and a new waxed braid finds favour on both these fabrics. Fashionable Colours.</p>
        <p>Fashion-mongers evidently believe in the old adage that "luck in green is always seen," for this colour was never more fashionable than at present. Some uncommon shades such as lichen, verdigris, and jade, are seen in velvets</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>.:. +++ + + ++++++++ + + + + + + + + •:. + + + I . A i + Children 8 corner.; •, . •</head>
        <p>.:. +++ + + ++++++++ + + + + + + + + •:. + + + I . A i + Children 8 corner.; •, . •</p>
        <p>+ + Conducted by UNCLE JACK. + 4. 4.4.</p>
        <p>A FINE DRAWING COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>My Dear Children,— We have had a fine drawing competition this week, about seventy drawings having been submitted for me to judge. What an army of young artists we have in Warrington, to be sure! Many of the drawings were of high merit, and I have been compelled to give three prizes, which I feel will meet the case better than one prize. The Corner is now so large and the competitors so many that one prize is lost, and it makes it very difficult for me to say which that one shall be. The winners of prizes are:— Nephew Harold Whittle,</p>
        <p>123, Cartwright-street, who attends St. Bar- nabas' School, has won twice previously, and sent a splendid drawing of John Travers Cornwell;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>Nephew Frank Overend,</head>
        <p>Nephew Frank Overend,</p>
        <p>74, Lythgoes-lane, Hamilton-street School, who sent a drawing of Admiral Jellicoe; and Niece Amy Berry, 83, Algernon-street, Fairfield Girls' School, who sent an accurate drawing of a box., I am pleased to say our membership is bound- ing up still. Would those who send in their names or friends' names be careful to state the names in full, the address, the age and the school attended? I like to have all particulars concerning my new nephews and nieces. Niece Ida Whitby writes me a very nice letter of thanks for the prize she received' last week. This week we will return to essay writing, and I will ask you to write me A Short Account of Trafalgar Day. —that is, how we celebrate it, and why. Your loving -UNCLE JACK.</p>
      </div>
