      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>LOCAL INTELLI ENCE.</head>
        <p>LOCAL INTELLI ENCE.</p>
        <p>Lights For Coming Week. Light up Obscure Vehicles. Lights. Saturday Sunday Monday Tuesday Wednesday Thursday Friday Saturday % God and the War.</p>
        <p>A series of sermons by the Rev. T. T. James on "God and, the War" sis to be commenced at Wycliffe Church to-morrow evening.</p>
        <p>More Births and Fewer Deaths.</p>
        <p>Last week there were 35 births and 13 deaths as comparedtwith 25 and 17 respectively for the corresponding week last year.</p>
        <p>Warrington Market Prices.</p>
        <p>At Wednesday's Warrington Market the price of wheat was Bs. 6d. to Bs. 9d. per 701 b., and 4s. 6d. to 4s. 9d. per 451 b. was realised for oats. Flour realised 475. 6d. to 525. 6d. per 2401 b, thirds 2s. sd. to 2s. 6d. per 201 b., while bran was is. 9d. to is. 10d. per 201 b.</p>
        <p>NValking Sticks for Wounded Soldiers.</p>
        <p>Mr. Stewart Royston, Sunnyside, Paddington, desires "The Examiner" to acknowledge with grateful thanks the receipt of £4 10s. Treasury Notes "In memory of J. 8.5.," to help to re- plenish his wounded soldiersl walking stick fund.</p>
        <p>Dukes' Successor Accepted. The Town Council on Tuesday unanimously approved the appointment of Mr. J. Hamblett as a member of the War Pensions Committee in place of Private Charles Dukes, whom they re- fused to accept under circumstances with which everyone is familiar.</p>
        <p>TholWaillilbys Pound Day.</p>
        <p>Pound Day. wjlLx be held at Thelwall Heys Military Convalescent Hospital on Thursday. Special needs are notepaper and groceries.</p>
        <p>SaliL.By Auction.</p>
        <p>Mr. Frank Hickman- will sell by auction next. Thursday at 47. Cawdor-street, Stockton Heath, the household,furniture of the late Mrs. Emma Dunn.</p>
        <p>Fearnheail Man Mentioned in Dispatches. General Sir. Percy Lake, in a dispatch pub- lished yesterday, "mentions" Sergeant A. IL, HugheA, Fearnheacl-lane, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>Teachers Want More Money.</head>
        <p>Teachers Want More Money.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Teachers' Association having applied for increases of • salary to all teachers on the permanent staffs, the Education Com- mittee have decided to meet a deputation. For a long time the watchwords of the teachers have been, "Smaller classes, higher leaving age, and more money."</p>
        <p>Two Gallant Teachers Congratulated.</p>
        <p>To Mr. David M. James and Mr.—Jack White- house, two teachers, who have recently- been promoted to second-lieutenancies for services the field; the congratulations of the Education Committee Were extended, on Monday evening, the, Rev. Canon Morley Stevenson voicing the sentiments of the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>Heating of a School.</head>
        <p>Heating of a School.</p>
        <p>It was reported to the Education Committee, on Monday, that the managers at Heathside School had found it necessary to replace, the. stoves throughout the school. The committee agreed with the managers' proposals, and' the arrangements made for the replacement. but consideration of the apportionment of the cosii was deferred.</p>
        <p>Harvest Festival at Christ Churek</p>
        <p>Padgate. Harvest thanksgiving services will be held at Christ Church, Padgate, to-morrow.. The preacher in the morning will be the Rev. Canon M. Stevenson, and in the evening the Rev: W. H. Campbell Baugh, vicar of St. Bride', Liver- pool. A children's serVice will be herd in the afternoon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>Rev. SF. Collier.</head>
        <p>The Rev. SF. Collier.</p>
        <p>The fourth in ,a series of special services and meetings in connection. with -Bold-street Wes- leyan Church was held on Thursday evening, when an address was delivered'• by ,the Rey. S. F. ,Collier' (of the Manchester Mission), who took as his subject "The Churches' Responsi- bility in the Present Day.'" Mr. T. B. Duck- worth presided. To-morrow- a popular service will be held in the church, when an attractive musical programme will be given at 6.30. The Rev. Percy meson will preach,</p>
        <p>Latchford Baptist Autumnal Sermons. Large congregations assembled in the Latch- ford Baptist Church on Sunday, on the occasion of the autumnal services. The special preacher for the day was the Rev. W. Scrago., of Wigan. In the morning an anthem, "Thrustr. in thy sickle and reap" was beautifully rendered by the choir, the anthem in the evening being "Father of Mercies." A very large congrega- tion was present in the afternoon, when the choir rendered a cantata, "From sowing to reaping." Collections, taken on behalf of church funds, realised £9.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>No Women Police Yet.</head>
        <p>No Women Police Yet.</p>
        <p>In Warrington there is at least one door closed to enterprising ladies, and policemen are there to keep watch. A letter has been received by the Watch Committee of the Town Council from the</p>
        <p>Warrington and Latchford Branches of the British Women's Temperance Association for-</p>
        <p>warding resolution requesting the committee lo consider the advisability of appointing women police, and it has been, resolved that the 'association be informed that the committee are of opinion that women police are unnecessary pt the present time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>Visits to Clinics.</head>
        <p>Visits to Clinics.</p>
        <p>The Medical Inspection Committee of the Education Committee have been on'a voyage of discovery. • Mr. Holman Kingdon mentioned to the Education Committee on• Monday, that the Sub7committee, of which he is the chair- man, had been round to look at the clinics, and those in attendance seemed very much surprised to see them. •Whether that surprise Was due to the fact that they had deigned to go, or that they had gone, he did not know. (Laughter.) However, he thought it a pity that those visits were not more. frequent. Councillor Henshall remarked that when they went those in charge looked their surprise; they did not express it. (Laughter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>Importan I. Regulations lor Street Collections.</head>
        <p>Importan I. Regulations lor Street Collections.</p>
        <p>It was decided at Tuesday's meeting of the Town Council that before there can be any collection of money or, sale for the benefit of charitable objects, application for a permit is to be made to the Chief Constable not later than one month before the date on which it is pro- posed to make the collection or sale. The police are given powers as to the conditions of sale, and no person under the age of 16 years shall act as a collector or vendor. No collector or vendor, is to "importune any person to the annoyance of such person." Within one month after the date of any collection or sale the per- son or organisation responsible therefor must forward to the Town Clerk for the information of the police authority a statement, with vouchers showing in detail the amount col- lected and the expenses incurred, and shall if required by the police authority satisfy them as to the due and proper application of the pro- ceeds of the collection or sale. A penalty not exceeding forty shillings, or, in the case •of a second or subsequent offence, not exceeding five pounds, is providetl for contravention of the regulations.</p>
        <p>Mr. Edward Wallington and Arpley</p>
        <p>Street School.</p>
        <p>It was reported to a meeting of the Educa- tion Committee on Monday 'evening, that the Director (Mr. J. Moore Murray) had, at the request of the managers of Arpley-street School, communicated with the Board of Education with reference to the approaching retirement of the headmaster (Mr. Edward Wallington) and the present difficulties of staffing. The Board having agreed to allow Mr. Wallington's ser- vices to continue until Bth December of next year, the School Management Committee now made that recommendation. Alderman Bennett remarked that in this time of national emer- gency they were very glad that Mr. Wallington was staying on another year. He (the speaker) hoped it would be a happy year, and arrange- ment might possibly last more than one. Mr. C. Jackson Holmes expressed the pleasure of the managers that Mr. Wallington was willing to continue another year end, Mr. Wallington very much appreciated the compliment. The Education Committee unanimously approved the recommendation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>Taxi Rides to Cost More.</head>
        <p>Taxi Rides to Cost More.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Taxi Owners'dAssociation have decided to increase the regulation taxi. fares. The, new prices will be found in our advertisement columns.</p>
        <p>CostioPShaving Increased. • The Warrington and District Hairdressers' Association give notice that on and after Mein* day the price of shaving will be increased to 2d.; also,- on -and from the same date, the shops will be cldsed from 11.30 to 12.30 for dinner.</p>
        <p>, Bread Again Advanced. The_Wrington Master Bakers' and Confec— tioners',Association announce that owing toithe • continued high cost of flour, &amp;c., they are com— pelled tot advance the price of bread one penny,. per 41b.,10af.</p>
        <p>Gaplain Malhams Injured. While walking along Sankey-street in • the. dark :on Wednesday evening Captain Mathams,. the military representative on the TribUnaL. slipped off the footpath and fractured a leg. We. are happy to say that he is progressing nicel;y.</p>
        <p>School Managers. Mr. John Eckersley, of Padgate-lane, has, beappointed a representative manager,of St.. Benedietts.R.f. School. The Rev. A. B. Ilayes. as. fbundattibsa manager of St. Mary's Sclsool, takes the place of the Rev.. Rasill Mtiwson.</p>
        <p>Harvest Festival at Elmwood Avenue:. : Harvest: festival services will be condiinthdi by. the Rev.. J. Ross Murray, of Manchester, on, Siandby .at Elmwood-avenue, Congseg_ational. 'Church, In the afternoon a service will be. conducted by the Rev. T. T. James,.. pastor of ,W&amp;cliffea, church. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>Volunteer Officers.</head>
        <p>Volunteer Officers.</p>
        <p>_ The, following appointments in, the, tqlieshirer !Volunteer Regiment were, among others,. .an- 'nouneed in the "London Gazette "'of, Mondaar : Battalion.—TO be Temporary, Lieutenants 1;17,th October, 1916: Frank Warburtion,,. Etaineis I:Alyilmer Frost. .</p>
        <p>"Constantinope ; Its History and Art."</p>
        <p>The Warrington Museuih Committee announce Ithat Mr. J. E. Phythian will deliver Lantern lee- Itures, commencing Saturday, November 4th, and finishing Saturday, November. 25th, in the Co- operative 'Hall, Cairo-street,',. On "Constanti- nople: Its History and Art.":</p>
        <p>• Early Closing, ot Shops. On Monday, October 30th, there wiU come into force an Order for the closing of. all shops at seven o'clock in the evening, es,ceptiug on Sat- urdays, when two hours' extension will be per- mitted. It is also infenred that the Order will apply to the Market, as it would be manifestly unfair for the stalls to: remain open after the shops had been compelled; to dose. Street trad- ing is also to cease at seven o'clock.</p>
        <p>Wood's College. Typewriting Successes</p>
        <p>The Typists' Section of the N.S.A., Junior Grade, certificato has been granted, after ex- amination, to the under-mentioned students J. Gioarke, L„ Jr Whorton, D. Rathbolle. James, E. B. Lester,, G. Prosser; D. Newton, A. Pasquill, I. Rooth, Walter Hughes, A. Burrows, A. M. Lloyd., M., Wright, G. H. Green, K. Parr, L. Lee, E. BORtoc.k,, B. Capper; N. Williamson, L. Hunt, D. Richards; M. A. Melling, F. Ileaton, T. Taylor, A. Pye. C. Tunstall, G. Mather, F. G. Atherton, N. Henshaw.</p>
        <p>The ev. Irvine Lister's Addresses. The series of extremely interesting discourses on "great Confessions of Faith" announced to be given by the ,Rev. Irvine Lister at the Sunday morning services was slightly altered last week. Mr. Lister was to have spoken on "The Autobiography of Mark Rutherford," but had to postpone his address on that sub- ject until to-morrow. Last Sunday he dealt with the life and spiritual development of Tolstoi, the Russian novelist. Mr. Lister's series of discourses are being followed with increasing interest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>Sale of Work at Golborne Street Baptist Church.</head>
        <p>Sale of Work at Golborne Street Baptist Church.</p>
        <p>Next Thursday- and Saturday a sale of Irak will be held in connection with the Baptist Church, Golborne-street. On the first day the sale will be opened by Mrs. Ogden, wife of the Pastor, and the chairman will be Mr. J. Taylor. On the Saturday the sale will be opened by the children, when one of the boys from the &amp;Ind** school will preside and one of the girls will per- form the opening ceremony. Other children will render a chorus. A great variety of useful and ornamental articles will be arranged on stalls. At these times the sale of work is ren- dered necessary by the fact that there is a build- ing debt contracted before the war of .nOO, and it is hoped that this will be reduced by at lealt £lOO. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>Soldier-Organist at Bewsey Road Wesleyan Church.</head>
        <p>Soldier-Organist at Bewsey Road Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>At Bewsey-road Wesleyan ,Church on Sunday special services were held, the, preacher, morn- ing and evening, being the Rev. Tom Dring, of Wallasey. In the morning the anthem wa6 " 0 how amiable." The afternoon was devoted to an organ recital by Private J. Worslor Harrop, organist and choirmaster, of Hazel Grove Wesleyan Church, Stockport. Mr. T. S. Steel presided. Two solos, "There's a Land" and "The Home Flag," were well sung by Miss E. Harrop, the organist's sister. In the evening the choir rendered the anthems " Jesu, priceless treasure," and the soloists, were Miss A. Winstanley and Mr. Albert Baker.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>Wood's College Successes.</head>
        <p>Wood's College Successes.</p>
        <p>The National Shorthand Association have granted certificates for efficiency in shorthand. at their respective stages to the under-mentioned students :—Ninety words per minute: Marion Smethurst (distinction). Seventy words Per minute: Ivy M. Lyon, Edith Withenshavr• Sixty words per. minute : L. J. Higgs (distinc-• tion), Ada Dykes (distinction), Amy Burrows (distinction), Dorothy Rathbone (distinction), Maud Forster (distinction), Nellie Griffiths, Betty Parsonage, A. Roughsedge, Gwendoline Prosser, Hilda Merrington, Edith D. Richarag, I. Booth, I. Bibby, A. Pa.squill, Marion Wright, Rose teach, J. Atherton, G. Mather, E. B. Les-.* ter. Fifty words per minute: Nora Williamson' Gladys James, Walter Hughes, F. G. Atherton, Gladys H. Green, K. Parr, Annie Pye, M.A. Melling, C. Tunstall.</p>
        <p>Death of Mr. Joseph Adams. A well-known figure in Friendly societ9 circles passed from amongst us on TuesdaY, the 10th inst., in the person of Mr. Joseph Adams,: of 18, Jockey-street. He reached the age of 6' years, and the funeral took place last Mond° at Hill Cliffe burial ground, being conducted by the Rev. W. T. Derry. Mr. Adams leaves widow, one son and six daughters. He receive' his early education in the Warrington Blue Coat School and graduated at the early age °f, nine years in the world of labour. He employed at Messrs. J.. Naylor's wire war' and became an indentured apprentice to T. Hackett, engineering tool manufacturer. In subsequently worked with Messrs. Pearson and Knowles, and for the past 24 years with Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co., Ltd. He married 3059 M. A. 'Foden. He was a prominent and active member of the Royal Order of Ancient OW herds and passed through the offices of the "Olive Branch" Lodge.</p>
