      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <p>BtRTHS, MARRIAGES, DEATHS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>CHRISTMAS IN THE TRENCHES.</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS IN THE TRENCHES.</p>
        <p>We would earnestly direct the attention of our readers to .an appeal which is made in to day's "Examiner " for donations, large or small, to the fund by which it is hoped to raise sufficient money to send a plum pudding to every British soldier on the various Fronts on Christmas Day. What we have to bear in mind is that the War Office 'does not guar- antee the delivery of separate puddings sent to' individuals or units. That. task would be too stupendous, and the War Office has insti- tuted .an Army Christmas Pudding Fund which is being run by two great daily news- papers, with assistance from other news- papers,, "The Warrington Examiner" in- cluded. Under this scheme the puddings, made under Government inspection, will be sent in bulk to the Front, and the battalions will be informed, when the money irepresent- ing -their supply has been raised in their own' district, that the puddings are a gift from their own home. It is a nice and thoughtful arrangement, and we hope to get a response that' will send a present from this district4to at least three local battalions. But it is a question of " Bis dat qui cito dat "—he gives twice who gives quickly. Christmas comes on , apace, and presentation plum pudding would lose much of its charm if it did not arrive in time. So the Editor respectfully asks his readers to bring or send their contribntions of a shilling, or any number of shillings, with all speed, and thus assist to set the makers to work in order that "the boys" may have a Christmas as happy as pit, m pudding may make it. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>MAYOR.</head>
        <p>THE MAYOR.</p>
        <p>• The MAYOR concluded 'his third year of office on Tuesday night, and had to undergo the ordeal of listening to a perfect hurricane of praise from all partr3 of the Council Cham- ber. There can be no doubt that Alderman PEACOCK has been an ideal Mayor for the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 21, 19161</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 21, 19161</p>
        <p>XMAS PUDDINGS FOR OUR BOYS.</p>
        <p>Sc he me.</p>
        <p>NEED FOR PROMPT ACTION.</p>
        <p>Our " boys " will be spending Christmas Day in the Field once again—the Field that is almost as wide as the world itself. Think of it—realise it, if you cant Three millions of our fathers, our sons, our 'brothers, watching over 'the interests of their native land, protecting us, its people, from the horrors that Belgium, Servia, Northern France and Poland have experienced. Three millions of men, peace-loving men, who, a few months ago, never dreamt of being. fighting men, but seethed destined to enjoy all their Christmases at home in happy family surroundings. This Christmas they will be away from home—some in waterlogged Bel- gium, others in the chalk-mud of the Valley of the Somme, others in the Balkans; Egypt, Mesopotamia, East Africa, with their un- familiar deserts and trying climates; while others will be keeping watch at such outposts of Empire as Malta and Gibraltar. ' ,Wherever they are their eyes will be turned towards England and their minds will wander, lovingly and longingly, homewards. Wher- ever they are, our thoughts will go out to them with a yearning that cannot be satisfied, .a desire inexpressible because so vain, to have them with us, as of old, upon this particular Ileaven-blessed festive occasion.</p>
        <p>If we cannot meet, however, we can join hands by suitable acts of remembrance. Our citizen-soldiers, who are famed for the man- ner in which they overcome the disadvantages of their environment ; will celebrate Christmas.. The celebration will be as close an imitation of the genuine festival as. is possible in strange lands and away from the facilities Of the home. The centre of the feast is the Christ- mas Plum Pudding; and a scheme has been. devised by which every man of the three mil- lions will receive his plum pudding from home, wherever he may be-. .</p>
        <p>" Examiner " Fund To Assist Army Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>ONLY GUARANTEE.</head>
        <p>THE ONLY GUARANTEE.</p>
        <p>Past experience has proved that so many Christmas puddings sent out separately or by various organisations cannot be delivered. Readers have only to reflect to understand how.impossible it would be for the authorities to receive, carry and deliver 3,000,000 separ- ate Christmas puddings in a given period. The Army Council have •issued an official communique upon this point. In this they</p>
        <p>It will not be possible to guarantee de- livery of puddings sent privately through the Military Forwarding Officer addressed to units or individuals, and they would pnly be sent at the expense of other gifts which the troops will naturally prefer to receive, a ration of pudding being provided already by the Christmas Pudding Fund.</p>
        <p>The Christmas Pudding Fund is a fund that has been opened by the "Daily News" (Liberal) and the " Daily Telegraph "(Union- ist), at the invitation of the Army Council. The Council •</p>
        <p>hope that all who may wish- to contribute towards the, provision of puddings will fall in with these arrangements, in order that all efforts may be directed through the same channels. It is only in this way that it will be possible to make an equal distribution, and to avoid unnecessary duplication. ContributiOns should consist of money only; in order to make the supply uniform in quality, to simplify the method of pack- ing and inspection, and to facilitate transit. All puddings will be subject to official in- spection during the process of manufacture, and no puddings other than those provided by the Fund will be accepted for general distribution.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>When a man was exempted bY the Warring- ton Tribunal, on Wednesday, and told to join the v.T.c., Mr. A. T. Hallaway. (the clerk) remarked: "Seeing that he comes from the cold stores it would be appropriate if he joined Mr. Frost's company."</p>
        <p>Captain Mathams "I'm afraid that joke won't cut ice!"</p>
        <p>Mr. Frost, who is a member of the Tribunal, was not present, and I think it only fair to bring the Matter before his notice.</p>
        <p>What Mr. A. J. Jolley had described as the agricultural lamb and the ecclesiastical lion had been "at it" as usual at the meeting of the Guardians on Wednesday.</p>
        <p>Towards the end of the meeting .the with a twinkle in his eye and a glance towards the lamb, read out, formally and rapidly:—</p>
        <p>"County Asylum, Lancaster. Dear sir.—l be to inform you that .George Slather, admitted October 22, 1914, escaped on the 9th instant—T" Laughter suspended operations.</p>
        <p>"If," continued Mr. Bottomley, still quoting with evident relish, "you hear. of his where- abouts-"</p>
        <p>That was the end of the quotation to reach the Board. The members roared, ,Mr. George Mather smiled his blandest, and the Rev. A. Alf Mitchell almost slipped under the table, wiping the tears from his eyes and finding relief for his feelings by exclaiming " Oh, dear! Oh, dear ! Oh, dear! Oh, dear!"</p>
        <p>I wonder how many Warrington ambulance men are en active service at the present moment? They will doubtles be numbered by the hundreds.</p>
        <p>Last week I had a note from Mr. Spann, the secretary of the Headquatters Division of the local St. John Ambulance Brigade, who is in Salonika. This week I heard from Mr. T. Cawley, the capable officer or the Crosfield's Division, who is staff sergeant and doing valu- able work at a large military hospital in the North of Ireland.</p>
        <p>Staff Sergeant Cawley says he is fairly well; and, from the tone of his letter, it is clear his heart is in its right place yet.</p>
        <p>I shall always be pleased to hear from friends on service in order to let their other friends know how they are progressing.</p>
        <p>Corporal Frank Kay is convalescing at Cairo his complaint having been desert jadedness—if the doctors will allow me to give a name to one of the ailments of the war. He reports "Improving daily," which is the best of news.</p>
        <p>Councillors, Guardians and the like will enjoy another walk-over—and another year's work. In their case no war bonus is to be allowed.</p>
        <p>With his usual generosity the Mayor provided refreshments after the presentation ceremony, at the Town Hall on Tuesday evening. There was everything the varying tastes desired. Even the baby was catered for—it was given its dummy and smacked its lips with relish.</p>
        <p>A brawny Liverpudlian told. a Warrington policeman (P.C. Bartley) who apprehended him under the Military Service Act on Wednesday night: "I have worn a pair of boots out dodging policemen. The giime is up. lam spent up and fed up." Now he is in the Army he will have to stand up. He will also get some new boots.</p>
        <p>"Steve" Donoghue, the famous Warrington jockey, secured a triumph in being on the win- ning horse in the Cesarewitch on Wednesday. The last time I saw him was when he was taking the ivories out of the pockets for Tom Reece at the Liberal Club.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>FOR OUR WARRINGTON " BOYS."</head>
        <p>FOR OUR WARRINGTON " BOYS."</p>
        <p>"The Daily News" has asked "The War- rington Examiner" to co-operate in raising, the 'necessary £60,000 to supply each man at the Front with a Christmas Pudding on Christ- mas Day. We have the greatest pleasure in acceding to the request, and ask our readers to send us their contributions. For conveni- ence' sake the puddings will be sent out in bulk, but if a district such as, Warrington or Lymm. contributes enough to serve a battalion, the Commanding Officer of the local battalion •will be so informed, and the men will know that the money for their own particular pud- din&amp;s came from their own particular homes. We are not permitted to name battalions, but we all know the units under whose colours our local men are fighting. To these men we ask our readers to send a Christmas message in a seasonable form, and so make them happy rin the remembrance of home if they cannot wish them the familiar Merry Christmas ! Sixpence pays for one man;, 2s. Gd. for five; 10s. 6d. for twenty-five; £1 is. for fifty; £1 11s. 6d. for a platoon; £3 3s. for a squadron or flattery; £5 ss. for a company; £9 9s. for an artillery brigade; £l2 125. for a cavalry regiment; and £2l for an infantry battalion, £B4 for a brigade, £420 for a division, £B4O for an army corps.</p>
        <p>"THE EXAMINER" CHRISTMAS PUDDING FUND.</p>
        <p>We have pleasure in opening an Army Christmas Pudding Fund, and in asking our readers to Send us any sum they may feel dis- posed to send. If those who had intended to send out a plum pudding and find they can- not do so with any guarantee that it will be delivered, will send us the money they in- tended to spend they will be taking a step to- wards making the delivery of the 'pudding a certainty. We are authorised under the War Charities Act to make this appeal. • Unauthorised and unregistered collections are an infringement of the Act. No one, of course, wants to prevent, say, a family or a party of friends sending. their money in a lump sum to the fund. The whole object of the new Act is to prevent-unauthorised public ,1 appeals, with the risks that come from them, and the wastage that arises when "charity" performances are given of which the proceeds are swallowed up- in expenses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>WHAT TO DO.</head>
        <p>WHAT TO DO.</p>
        <p>Send or bring ANY SUM FROM ONE SHILLING UPWARDS to The Editor, " Examiner " Office, Warrington.</p>
        <p>All gifts will be acknowledged in "The Ex- aminer." Only prompt action can make the scheme a success, so send in yohr donations at once!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>DONATIONS RECEIVED.</head>
        <p>DONATIONS RECEIVED.</p>
        <p>To make a start two of the directors of "The Examiner" have sent donations as under:— Mr. C. E. Parker • £s 0 0 Sir Arthur Crostield, -Bart.) £1 1 0</p>
        <p>"The Examiner"</p>
        <p>£1 1 0</p>
        <p>Remember! We want 21 for each batta- lion to be served. The number of local batta- lions to have plum puddings from home depends upon the number of £2l's you send to us.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>THANKS FOR THE HARVEST.</head>
        <p>THANKS FOR THE HARVEST.</p>
        <p>ST. PAUL'S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The harvest festival services at St. Paul's Church were continued on Sunday, when large congregations assembled. The Rev. E. Gunner and the Rev. E. DOwnham were the preachers morning and evening, cespectively: The an- them "Let the heavens rejoice" was rendered by the choir, Mr. E. Wilkes being at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>ST. JOHN'S PRESBYTERIAN.</head>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S PRESBYTERIAN.</p>
        <p>The annual harvest thanksgiving services were held on Sunday last, the preacher for the day being the Rev. John Heap, of Manchester, who took for his morning lesson "In the morning sow thy seed, and in the evening withhold not thy hand," and for his evening subject "A basket of summer fruit." The sermons were most appropriate to the occasion, and the thoughts expressed were much appreciated. Special 'music was rendered by the choir at both .services. At the evening service -Madame Sanders was the soloist, and gave "The radiant morn" with beautiful expression. Miss Cotton accompanied ,an'tho piano and Mr. Lewsey offi- ciated at the organ. The church had been taste- fully decorated by the lady members, and the fruit and flowers were afterwards distributed to the local hospitals for wounded soldiers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>INTERNATIONAL ORDER OF GOOD</head>
        <p>INTERNATIONAL ORDER OF GOOD</p>
        <p>TEMPLARS.</p>
        <p>The quarterly session of the No. 1 Conven- tion, South-West Lancashire District, was held last Saturday, in the Emmanuel Church In- stitute, Knutsford-road, Warrington, under the auspices of the Temperance Mission Lodge, N. 1,321. Bro. Morris Jones, of Liverpool, the district secretary, occupied the chair and gave an inspiring address. All the Lodges in the Convention area were represented, including 'Widnes, St. Helens, Wigan and Earlestown. Early in the meeting a touching tribute was Paid to the late Convention Chief Templar, Bro. Roger Watson, who was in the chair at the last meeting, but has since passed away, and a vote of sympathy with the relatives was carried. The reports of the various officers, showing the work accomplished during the past quarter, were well received. After an adjournment for refreshments the 'session re-assembled and an excellent paper was read .by Bro. Atherton, of St. Helens, dealing with objections raised to Gopd Templary, .and an interesting discussion fol- lowed.. in the evening a Iree.concert, at which there was a good attendance, Was given by members and friends.</p>
        <p>Guild of Help Secretary. The new secretary of the Warrington Citizens' Guild of Help is Miss Ashworth, who comes to</p>
        <p>_ . us with very -good credentials and recommenda- tions. She was with Mr. Grundy in the early days of the Manchester Guild of Help move- ment, and for the last nine years she has been secretary of the Reading Guild of Help. Mr. Rowland, whom Miss Ashworth succeeds, is now engaged by the War Savings Committee in London.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <p>say anything he shouldn't. For the most part he talked about the pleasure of being home once more, and his thankfulness, that after twenty-one months' campaigning in Ffance he still throbs with energy and haenot been away from his battalion for sickness for an hour dur- ing the whole time. Since January, when he was promoted, he has not been exposed to con- tinual danger—excepting on excursions with food and kit he does not enter the front line trenches—but previously he was sergeant of the. machine gunners, and fought sternly in several engagements, and saw men falling by his side in groups. Some of the Germans he saw cap- tured on the Somme were very young and elderly, and a group of them admitted that through our excellent artilleri work they had been unable to get food for five days, their ration parties being prevented from making the usual journeys through the fire-swept course.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>CERTAIN VICTORY.</head>
        <p>THE CERTAIN VICTORY.</p>
        <p>Asking the Quarter-Master to have a cigarette led him to remark that in a German dug-oat 30 feet deep he found some cigars of quite a splendid quality. Apparently, he said, -cigars were part of the Germans' allowance.. For the rest the N.C.O. spoke of his local associations—the days when he was in charge of a Latchford tram and enjoyed a popularity un- rivalled, his friends, and the causes he cares for in Warrington. . "But I'll tell you this," he said, "if you saw the me* the gnus, and the shells we; have got out there ypu would feel as confident of victory as we soldiers do. The British Army is the finest in the world."</p>
        <p>While he was speaking his nine-year-old-or- thereabouts-daughter Phyllis came in from school. "Have you been caned?" her father asked playfully. "No, of course, not," she rejoined in a way which betokened that of a good girl. And she sat lovingly on his knee. Her,dad has gone again, and she has begun to write him a long, long letter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>Comforts Galore.</head>
        <p>Comforts Galore.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Association of The War Comforts for Soldiers (Official Branch) which is appealing for funds, has sent out during the last month 114 shirts to France. 154 mufflers to Egypt. 1,240 pairs of socks to France. 240 towels to France: 500 pairs of mittens to Mesopotamia. 110 muffrers to Salonica.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>Tube Worker's Claim.</head>
        <p>Tube Worker's Claim.</p>
        <p>At the County Court ,on Thursday, before Judge Barnard Lailey, K.C., Mr. Chapman, 'solicitor, Manchester, who appeared for the plaintiff in an action of Enoch Baker v. Monks, Hall and Co., said the parties had arrived at a settlement. His client, a tube worker, claimed compensation from respondents, a firm of tube makers in Warrington, in respect of two acci- dents which occurred on the 15th September, 1915, and 16th May, 1916. Both accidents caused injuries to his left knee. Applicant had been out of work since the date of the second .accident. Be had agreed to accept £45 and costs in full settlement of his claim. Mr. Healey. solicitor. Manchester, appeared for the respondents:- His Honour Mode the order sought, confirming the settlement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Sea Breezes at Wycliffe P.S.A.</head>
        <p>Sea Breezes at Wycliffe P.S.A.</p>
        <p>, There was a good meeting on Sunday last, presided over by Mr. B. Farrar. . The Rev. G. Curtis, of Hill Cliffe, was the speaker, and spoke upon "Being Faithful "—faithful to God, to our fellows, and to the work of God. A hearty welcome was given to Geo. Colley, who was over on a visit, also to Roy M. P. Conville. The latter's experiences on the sea have been considerable, and he was on two transport ships which were disabled. The brethren wished for him God-speed in his future sailings. A vote of synipathy was passed to Mrs. Parkes and family, of Bewsey-road, in the death of her husband, Joseph Parkes, Who was for many years a mem- ber of 'Wycliffe P.S.A. Miss E. Taylor sang, "Entreat. me not to leave Thee" and "Come unto Me" with great taste and feeling. Mr. W. H. Payton presided at the organ. Mr. Yearsle'y also took partin the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>Bank Quay Mission: Y.P.S.C.E.</head>
        <p>Bank Quay Mission: Y.P.S.C.E.</p>
        <p>- The young people's section of the Bank Quay Working Men's Mission Christian Endeavour, celebrated its twenty-second anniversary on Wedesday evening by a most enjoyable social gathering, at which about fifty guests were pre- sent. Mr. A. Gleave, president, was in the chair, and Mr, Thomas Dale, president of the Mission; Mr. George Dale, vice-president; and Mr. George Head, hon. secretary, were amongst those present. The arrangements were excel- lently carried out by the musical and . social committee, and the programme of solos, duets and recitations was full of good things. The following contributed in a marked degree to the success of the evening: The Misses Eva Tanner, E. Oakley, L. Rigby, D. Tillinh, Gleave and E. Millington, and Mr. S. Ray, humorist. Mr. T. Tanner was the accompanist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>SIDE-SLIP FOR SPEEDY SPEAKERS.</head>
        <p>SIDE-SLIP FOR SPEEDY SPEAKERS.</p>
        <p>suggestion!A simple Successfully -iii;-"She should surely see shows smart with smiles but no shockings, So secure some seats soon or "A Pair of Silk Stockings." Said safely? Send swiftly !—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 21, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 21, 1916.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE FINDLEY DALE.</p>
        <p>Survives The Explosion Of Two Bombs In lis Equipment.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Stephen Dale, 264, Manchester- road, have received news that their son, Private</p>
        <p>Findley Cecil Dale, has returned to duty. As we reported last week, he and five others were together when a high explosive shell burst a few yards from them. All were buried, two being killed and three wounded, Private Dale being the only one unwounded. "Some time after the charge," says Private Dale in a letter home, "the battalion were re- lieving another lot. Two lads•and I were be- hind the trench waiting to rush into it when a 'whiz-bang' dropped at our feet and exploded, hitting the other two (One a corporal, wfio has since died), and exploding two bombs in my equipment, blowing it off and part of my tunic, hut it never entered any part of my body. It ' threw the three of us some yards. After this I dropped into the trench while the other two were got away. Twenty-four hours later we. went forward into the front line, and after being there about twelve hours a passed over dropping sft. away, burying three of us, and. killing another one. The lads dragged us out. After that I had no nerve or spirit left, but I was not hurt in the least. I was sent down the line to hospital, but I was only in there four</p>
        <p>back on October 9th, and now lies in a hospital at Sheffield.</p>
        <p>Sapper Peacock, a former employe of the tele- graph department of the .London and North- Western Railway, is an old boy of Holy Trinity School, and 24 years of age. He enlisted a few days after the outbreak of war, and has been on active service for fourteen months. Mr. Thomas Peacock, a brother, who was wounded in April of last year, and subsequently dis- charged, belonged to the South Lancashire Regiment. His other brother; Driver Fred Peacock, is at present in training with the Mechanical Transport Section A.S.C.</p>
