      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>: • • " R L 1 I E •</head>
        <p>: • • " R L 1 I E •</p>
        <p>Additional Local Casualties in the Various War Areas.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE W. ROSE Killed.</p>
        <p>street, , concerning her husband, Private William Rose, South Lancashire Regiment, and stating (in part): "I am extremely sorry to have to say that Private Rose was killed in action on Sepiember 27th. .. . His comrades are very sorry to have lost him, and I sincerely hope that the fact that he was a most willing worker and good soldier will help you to feel that comfort and consolation which such know- ledge is calculated to give. In the recent successes of the allied forces your husband Played a fearless part, doing his duty fearlessly and well."</p>
        <p>Private Rose was a native of Runcorn,. but had lived in. Warrington for six years. His Wife and one child now live in Clegg-street, but formerly resided at 6, Birchall-street. Thirty- three years .of age, Private Rose. who enlisted on 28th December, 1914, and had .been at the Front fifteen months when he was killed, was employed on the whad by Messrs. Joseph Cros- field and Sons, Ltd. He was educated at the board School, Runcorn, and has a brother, David, serving with the forces at Gibraltar. lie has also four nephews in the Army.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE S. BENION. Killed.</p>
        <p>to you, and he said I was to give you his love." Driver Daniels was the only, son of the family, though he had seven sisters. He formerly at, tended SL Ann's School and Church.</p>
