      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>LIEUT. ARMSTRONG'S THRILLING</head>
        <p>LIEUT. ARMSTRONG'S THRILLING</p>
        <p>COURAGE.</p>
        <p>A gallant deed which the late Second-Lieut. Armstrong performed a day before his death in action is narrated by a. man of the same regi. ment, now lying wounded in the Lord' Derby 'War Hospital, Winwick. On the Sunday after- noon, he says, a volunteer was asked for to take out a bombing Party, owing to the bombing °facer being indisposed. Lieutenant Armstrong unmediately volunteered, and so keen was he that be left the trench with his men without his hat, shouting light-heartedly to a comrade. " Well, Bill, I'm off." The enemy trenches were taided, and all the party came, safely Liontenant Armstrong being the last man to do so.</p>
        <p>The informant did not knew of t:te fate of Lieutenant .Armstrong, and was greatly sur- prised to hear he wis killed the following day Although 14 had only been with us a aho he added, "lie was very :veil</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>CLERGYMAN AND GIRL.</head>
        <p>CLERGYMAN AND GIRL.</p>
        <p>Identity Of Defendant In Mysterious Case.</p>
        <p>There has been a zather startling develop- ment of the case . which was heard at the Warrington Borough Police Court last week; when a young female, whose name and address were not made public, and Basil Wilberforce Stothert, of Thrapston, Northants,. were charged with offences under the Aliens Reatric;.- tion Act, 1914. The female defendant .was- charged with having registered in a false mune, and address, while staying at the Lion Hotel on the 25th September, and also with regist-er- ing in a false name when staying at the Patt,ik Arms on August 24th. The male 'defendant was charged with aiding and abetting on each occa- sion. • It has since been made known that the mare defendant was a clergyman and that the Bishop of' Peterborough ha§ declared the :benefice o. Thrapston, Northants, vacant by the resigaa- tion of the Rev.„ Basil Wilberforce Stothert, a, former military chaplain. At the hearing of the case the Chief Con- stable explained that when the parties stayed at the Patten Arms it was not necessary to register their addresses, the names alone being sufficient. It appeared, he said, that the male defendant belonged to Thrapston, in Northants. He went to the Lion Hotel and eti,gaged roams for himself and wife and the young- girl at- tended on fiat 25th September represented herself as his wife. The registration forms were placed in front of them, and. the young woman apparently had no knowledge of filling in the form. She asked the male defendant how to fill it, and he suggested the names and address. She was a young woman of about 20 years of age, and had been engaged at Thrap- ston. It seemed that there was ix certain amount of familiarity between the parties, and she was removed from Northamptonshire. to Lancashire. Some time ago, in August, they came to Warrington, and went to the Patten Arms Hotel and stayed there approximately a week, and the same procedure as to registration took place there, only the address had not to be taken. The male defendant signed his name and put his nationality as British. The female defendant was with him at (the time, and was observed by the bookkeeper to fill in her name as Stothert. On the form at the Lion Hotel the address was filled in as Thrapston, Northants, and as far as the girl was concerned that was not correct. The ease in the eyes of the law was , an exceptionally serious one, for the reason that the girl was 20 years of age, and that the male defendant had apparently been following her. He came there and without his guidance or , without his help it would have been impossible for her to have committed the offence. The :male defendant was a married man with a family, and held a position in the place he came from which certainly ought to have made- him the • last possible individual to have acted in the 1 way he had done. Stotherts was fined .t2O or a month's imprison- ment, and the case against the girl was dis- missed; as she had been acting under the male • defendant's influence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>WAR BONUSES.</head>
        <p>WAR BONUSES.</p>
        <p>Why Men Get Bigger Grants Than Women.</p>
        <p>At the fortnightly meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians on Wednesday the Clerk (Mr. A. BOttomley) reported that the }louse Committee had adopted the Local Government Board rate of war bonus in respect of certain employes of the Board, who came under th-s?, designation of civil servants. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell drew attention to the fact that the bonus allowed for :women was only half the amount allowed to men. He asked why this should be so. The Chairman (Mr. A. J. Jolley): This is a Government scheme; I cannot answer that ques- tion.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: It means a very in, significant sum and the women are doing men's work.</p>
        <p>'The -Chairman : That is not a point for us ta argue.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell moved that a protest be sent to the Local Government Board against the arrangement whereby women would receive only one-half the war bonus received by men. Miss Broadbent seconded, and expressed the hope that other officers not named in this list would not, be passed over. The Clerk explained that in a case specifically mentioned the officer was not one of those whose salaries had to be sanctioned by the Local Government Board, and the Guardians could fix the bonus at whatever sum they pleased. The Chairman explained that there was a differentiation between men and women all over the country., A large number of the women were not the sole support .of the home as were the men. Whether the 'division was a wise one he was not disposed to discuss, but he would be Sorry if the Board sent up this protest to Lon- don. (Mr. Mather: "Hear, hear.") There was something to be said for the Treasury side of the question. The proposition was rejected, only the mover and seconder voting for it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>BISHOP'S MESSENGER.</head>
        <p>THE BISHOP'S MESSENGER.</p>
        <p>Talks To Women In St. James' Church, Latchford.</p>
        <p>The Bishop's Messenger visited St. James' Church, Latchford, yesterday, and delivered an address to women in connection with the National Mission of Repentance and Hope. Women, he said, were sent into the world to purify it, to bind up the broken-hearted, to tend and cherish, and to make the path of life smoother. This could not 1)e better accom- plished than in the home 'Ti fe. Where was there ai more beautiful, word in the English language than "home," the place where out of the turmoil and care of life we could find shelter and sympathy? Home life to-day, how- ever, was very much in danger. It was partly due to the sad state of life in which we were living. Homes were bereft of male workers, and women had been called out to do the work And they were doing ifwell. We could not say that women were wrong in coming forward to do the work; indeed, it was very self-sacrificing. on their part. But a word of warning must be uttered, for, though the women were giving themselves so plentifully, they could not afford to let the home life slip away. What of the ghild during these times? Boys and girls were at present utteiqy out of hand; and to say we had not got time 'during these days to study them-was a terrible thing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>MISSiNG.</head>
        <p>MISSiNG.</p>
        <p>SERGEANT APPLETON.</p>
        <p>Two Lower Walton Brothers</p>
        <p>Missing.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Samuel Appleton, of 72, Elles- mere-road, Lower Walton, have received a letter from a 'friend of their son, Sergeant Alfred Appleton, to the effect that he has been missing since an action on the Ist October. AccO,ding to the letter, Sergeant, Appleton, who was serving in France and attached to the went over the parapet wita his platoon, and was seen to be hit by a piece of shell and afterwards to run forward a short distance. This was the last seen of him by his friends. Up to now no official intimation has been received by the parents and naturally they are very anxious as to his welfare ar.d whereabouts, and any information will be greatly appreciated by them. Sergeant Apple- ton joined the K. 0.5.8. in the early stages of the war and has been on active service ,in kirance for about eighteen months. Nothing further has yet been heard of his brother, Private John Appleton, Cheshire Regi- ment, who has been wounded and missing since 2nd August. Another younger brother, Horace' Appleton, has joined the Navy and is at present stationed at a naval port. All three were connected with the Latchford Baptist Church, Alfred and John being Church Members. Prior to the war, John was a mem- ber of the Latchford Hockey CI 2b, and Alfred and Horace were prominent . members cf the Latchford Baptist Boys' Brigade.</p>
      </div>
