      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>DIED OF WOUNDS.</head>
        <p>DIED OF WOUNDS.</p>
        <p>DRIVER C. DANIELS.</p>
        <p>An Only Son's Unfulfilled</p>
        <p>Ambition.</p>
        <p>Driver .Charles Daniels, R.F.A., son of Mr. Wm. Daniels, 21, Cross-street, died of wounds in hospital in France on October Bth. He was only nineteen years of age, and about .five Months ago left his employment in the London and North-Western Railway Co.'s Locomotive Sheds at Warrington to join .the Royal Field 'Artillery in order to gratify an ambition to follow in the footsteps of his uncle, Sergeant T. C. Cullen, who is a gunner in the R.F.A. Writing to his mother a day, or two before, he was wounded, he said :—" I have been attached to a battery at last, for good. I was only attached last night about tea time. I was kept working till early this morning. And what a night! We were under fire all the time; it will be a long time before I forget it, I can tell you." He also said he was "dying for news, from home." Then came the news that. be had been. wounded, only three weeks after his arrival in France, and the assistant matron of the hospital wrote to say he had died. "Unfortunately,"• she said, writing to Mrs. Daniels, "his leg had to be amputated.' He passed away quite peacefully. He didn't realise he was dying. I told him I was writing.</p>
        <p>DRIVER C. DANIELS Died of Wounds.</p>
        <p>BRAVE R.A.M.C. MAN.</p>
        <p>Ho‘‘ Private W. E. Owner Met His Heath.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Chetter, " 42; ,Pierpoint-street, have received the following , letter .epneerning their son, ,Private William Edward Chetter, •R,A.31.C., who died -of wounds in the leg on September 17th._</p>
        <p>The letter, which was from Major O'Neill, commanding officer 3-.. d Field Ambulance :ran.: "*I have received your letter re the death of your son, Pte. W. E. Chetter, R.A.M.C. He was badly wounded by shell fire when bravely remov- ing w•ounded from the firing line. He died of the wounds received, at No. 5 Casualty Clearing Station, and is 'buried in the British Cemetery there. The officers and men Of this unit regret the loss of a gallant comrade, and offer you their deepest synipathy in your trouble." Private Chetter formerly worked at the Bewsey Wire Ropery, later going to the White- cross Co., pd. He also Was a \ former member of the Be wsey Boy Scouts, and attended St. Paul's Church Bible Class. . He had served in the Army two years, and at the Front fourteen months, being nineteen and a half years of age.</p>
        <p>Brilliant Scholar Leaves Study for Camp. A request from Mr. Leslie J. Witts, a Warring- ton Scholarship holder, for the suspension of the award of his scholarship during his period of military service, was acceded to by the Education Committee on Monday evening.</p>
        <p>--- SIDE-SLIP FOR SPEEDY SPEAKERS. A simple suggestion! Successfully say: She should surely see shows smart with smilcs but no shoekings, So secure some seats soon for "A Pair of Silk Stockings." Said safely? Send swiftly !—Advt.</p>
        <p>Wounded%</p>
        <p>eight weeks' service in France, enlisted in February of this year, and formerly worked for the St. Helens Cable and Rubber Co., Ltd. He is an "old boy" Of People's College, and is in his 20th year.</p>
