      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>SIDE-SLIP ,FOR SPEEDY SPEAKERS.</head>
        <p>SIDE-SLIP ,FOR SPEEDY SPEAKERS.</p>
        <p>A simple suggestion! SicePssfully say: She should 'surely see shows smart with smiles but no shockings, So secure some seats soon fOr "A Pair of Silk Stockings." Said safely? Send swiftly !—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>PRIVATE T. WILKINSON.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE T. WILKINSON.</p>
        <p>One Of Four Soldier-Sons Wounded In The Knee.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Wilkinson, -7, Dudley-street, have rcep,ved news_ from the' son, Set -eont Thomas Wflkinson, South .I.4_teashire Regi- ment, who has been wcunded in he right knee. Extracts from his three letters are reproe,ueed below</p>
        <p>Writing from a Canadian hospital on Sept. 29th, Sergeant Wilkinson said "Just a few lines to let you knovy I am in the pink. At present I am in hospital suffering from a shrapnel wound above the right knee, and with a bit of luck .will get to England _ _ _</p>
        <p>The second letter, from the -Same hoSpital, ran : KC • • • • I am getting on pretty fine and may soon be " again in 'dear, old Blighty.' " third ran: "Just a few more lines to let you know I have arrived in hospital at Manchester."</p>
        <p>Sergeant Wilkinson, who is the -third son, is 20 years of age. Formerly an employe of Win- stanley's, plumbers, he was a member of the Church Lads' Brigade at St. George's Mission. He was mobilised on the 'outbreak of war and went to the Front in February of last year. He has three brothers serving—Private Frank Wilkinson, with the R.A.M.C. in Mesopotamia; Corporal Herbert Wilkinson, with the R.A.M.C. in. France; and Private Harry Wilkinson, in training with the Scutb. Lancashire Regiment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>WOUNDED SOLDIERS.</head>
        <p>WOUNDED SOLDIERS.</p>
        <p>School Children Entertain</p>
        <p>Whitecross Hospital Patients.</p>
        <p>The opening concert of the winter season was given at Whitecross illlspital on Thursday week, in the dining haft It was arranged by Mr, W. H. Rigby? and was given by vne children of gvellSin--streei School', under the leadership of .Tiss Clarke; and the children of St, Ann's &amp;heel; in charge of Mr. Rigby. The young folk, to the number of sixty,, gave songs; and dunces- in costume to, the great enjoyment of the soldiers who crowded the room and joined in the choruses in a spirited: manner. A very popular item was the Irish jig by the Evelyn- street children,, which had, to; be repeated twice. Another effective item was the candle scene, " Toddle,;off to bye-bye, by the St. children. Great amusement was caused by a girl's recitations, "If I were a man" and "A charwoman's opinion of: holidays:" Mr. W.- 11, Rigby gave "Up from Zummerzet;" and was- loudly recalled. The soldiers- received a pleasant surprise when during the singing Of a song, the singer. walked among the audience, showering cigarettes and sweets among them. During a short interval the men were invited to join in singing popular choruses, and this they did, much to their delight. Much' amuse- ment was caused by their singing of "I want to go home.," Mr. A. Bottomley; who had acted as chair- man, proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. Rigby for arranging such an enjoyable evening,.and to the children who had danced and sung so well. This was carried with great acclamation by the soldiers. A memorable evening was brought to a close by children and soldiers singing popu- lar airs and national anthems of allies. The Misses Clarke, Chamberlain and Mr. Rigby. acted as accompanists. The cigarettes and sweets had been provided by the children of both schools.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>VARIETY ENTERTAINMENT.</head>
        <p>VARIETY ENTERTAINMENT.</p>
        <p>A concert was given at the Whitecross Mili- tary Hospital by the Pearce's Variety Enter- tainers (Manchester) on Wednesday evening. The various items were loudly applauded by the wounded soldiers and nursing staff. Encores were demanded right throughout the evening, and the deligheof the audience was unbounded. The programme opened up with a pianoforte overture by Miss S. Michael, who played point- lar choruses in brilliant style. Miss D. Stanley, in the songs, "It is not because your heart is mine" and "When you come home" (contralto) greatly pleased the " Tommies." Miss Connie Slater (Little Connie from the Blackpool Tower), dainty singer and dancer, sang "Taffy's got his Jenny in Glamorgan" and "Michigan," and concluded with a graceful dance, which de- lighted all. Phil Careless was very amusing in "Bow-wow." The brilliant soprano, Miss Nelly Hall, in the song, "I did not know until,' met with full appreciation. " J.W.P.," in stories and monologue, caused much laughter by his wit. Miss. Minnie Victoria (some comedienne)! was loudly 'applauded for her quaint rendering of ".Broken doll" and "Cecil, I shall have to call the guard." The man of much mystery, Mr. Edgar Coles, interested the " Tommies " and staff when showing his skill as entertainer and conjuror. Mr. Ernie Milton (comedian) in his songs, "Don't stick it out like that" and "Dance with your Uncle, Joe,' caused - much mirth with his patter. Miss Maude Forrest, the charming young soprano, delighted her hearers with the songs, "God sent you back to "The long, long trail" and "Annie Laurie." A concerted item, "Ken- tucky home," by four of the artistes, was also rendered amidst much laughter and applause. At the conclusion of the programme C.S.M. Blakeley (organiser). called for a vote of thanks to the party, and Mr. Pearce, acknowledging the vote, which was passed with acclamation, said that he and his party had looked forward to this second appearance at Whitecross Hos- pital with great pleasure.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>HIPPODROME ARTISTES AT THE</head>
        <p>HIPPODROME ARTISTES AT THE</p>
        <p>INFIRMAP,Y.</p>
        <p>A really tip-top concert arranged by Mr. W. B. Randall, was given to the wounded at the Infirmary on Wednesday evening, when (by the kind permission of Mr. W. A. Jackson, manager of the Hippodrome), the following artistes appeared :---Miss Pauline Travis (male imper- sonator), Fyne and Hurley, Warwick and Ciss, Edwin Adeler, Harry Brown, and Miss Hurley (dancer). They were assisted by some excel- lent local talent, including Miss Emily Taylor (contralto), Miss Mamie Blackburn (soprano), Mr. Fred Fieldhouse, Mr. Frank Lloyd (cornet) and Master Haddock. All the items were well rendered, the artistes being recalled several times. Mrs. Hurley and Mr. W. B. Randall I made first-class accompanists. A vote of thanks was passed to the artistes for the excellent manner in which they had entertained the "Tommies," and, in reply, Mr. Harley said it gave them unbounded pleasure to be there. Three real British cheers were then given for the artistes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>WHITECROSS HOSPITAL.</head>
        <p>WHITECROSS HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>To Take 500 Military Patients</p>
        <p>in The Future.</p>
        <p>Whitecross 'Military Hospital, which at pres- ent accommodates 300 soldier-patients, is to undertake the care of 500 patients in the near future.</p>
        <p>A letter from Colonel William Cotes, Chester, was read at the meeting of the Warrington Board of• Guardians on Wednesday, asking whether they could undertake to provide 200 additional beds for military cases. Mr. G. Mather proposed that the additional accommodation be provided, _</p>
        <p>. - The Rev. A. M. Mitchell seconded, agreeing that we mast make every possible sacrifice</p>
        <p>The proposal was carried.</p>
        <p>The -Chairman (Mr. A. J. Jolley) explained that the people who would feel the change most would be the sick and aged, who would have to be transferred. It Was necessary that they should be at a place accessible to the visits of friends. Mr. Brooks, Dr. Hodgkinson, Mr. Bottomley and he had inspected the house and thought that by the expenditure of not less than £3oo—which would be paid by the Government —upon some portions of the old part of the building, accommodation could be provided for the sick poor, who could be transferred to make .room for the 200 extra beds for the soldiers. There was the alternative of sending their in- mates to other , unions, where the workhouses were not required for military purposes, but long distances raised a doubtful point. The Master (Mr. W. Loxham), in answer to several members, Said there were now 279 sol- dier-patients in the hospital,' and 27 more were expected that morning. Up to date they had had 1,005 patients through the hospital. . It was mentioned that a suggestion had been •made that the children pier seven years of age at the Padgate Homes should be sent to Woolstou School for educational purposes. This would free a large room to be transformed into a hospital for the sick children of the union. On the motion of Mr. Geo. Mather, the com- mittee in charge of the War Hospital arrange- ments was empowered to carry out the neces- sary work to provide the accommodation re- quested and, to +l," 'arrangements for the transference of the inmates to other quarters.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>Crosfield's 400.</head>
        <p>Crosfield's 400.</p>
        <p>It is estimated that 400 of the employes of Messrs. Jos. Crosfield and Sons are on active service at the present moment. The employes' Works Council, of which Mr. W. E. Clegg is the chairman, have decided to send out a parcel to each of these 400? but they are in need of information as to the whereabouts of many of them. It will be seen from an advertisement that Mr. Clegg is asking their relatives and friends to supply him with the name, battalion and regimental number of the men, so that the work of isending out the paicels without risk of loss may be facilitated. Will all readers interested send the information to Mr. Clegg at the works, Bank Quay?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>SIDE-SLIP FOR SPEEDY SPEAKERS</head>
        <p>SIDE-SLIP FOR SPEEDY SPEAKERS</p>
        <p>A simple suggestion! Successfully say: She should surely see shows smart with smiles but no shockings, So secure some seats soon for "A Pair of Silk Stockings." Said safely? Send swiftly !—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>TRAFALGAR DAY.</head>
        <p>TRAFALGAR DAY.</p>
        <p>To-day's Anniversary Antici- pated By The Schools.</p>
        <p>ALDERMAN BENNETT'S PRIZE-WINNERS.</p>
        <p>Trafalgar Day is to-day (Saturday) and because it did not fall within school hours the event was anticipated by the schools and cele- brated yesterday. At all schools in the borough special lessons :on patriotism and the "Nelson touch" were :given, but the most interesting celebrations ,were where Alderman Arthur Bennett went to. :distribute:. his prizes in connection with essays :the scholars had written successfully at the :Secondary,. Holton and St James' (Latchford). :The subject set the Seceexlary School pupils was "Shakespeare, England, and the War," and that stipulated for the elementary schools "Patriotism, as illustrated by characters in Shakespeare's playa."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>JUBILATION. AT BOLTON COUNCIL</head>
        <p>JUBILATION. AT BOLTON COUNCIL</p>
        <p>Sc. e</p>
        <p>To Bolton Council. School west the elemen- tary school shield through the excellent essay written by Winifred Tilbrook, daughter of Mr. Frederick Tilbrook, of Latchfood- Naturally the was great jubilation in the girls' department at the Bolton Council School yesterday morning, when the Alderman ap- peared with the shield and severer books for the winner. Mr: W. Bibby presided- Among those present Were the Misses Stewart, Jones and Beardmore; Mrs. Tilbrook,. and Mr. Elliott (assistant director of education) and Mr. Tur- ton (headmaster of the boys' department). After the opening chorus, !` Now pray we for our country," which was most pleasingly sung by the children 4, Mr.. Bibby formally introduced Alderman Bennett to the girls—although he is well known to them—and the Alderman gave them a stirring Trafalgar Day address: He then presented the shield and a present of books to Winifred Tilbrook.</p>
        <p>Miss Place (the headmistress) suitably thanked the Alderman, and said that as he had at one time expressed a wfsh that the Bolton girls might be successful, he had now his wish fulfilled.</p>
        <p>A short musical programme follbwed, when the songs ‘‘ Patient- "Our Sailor &amp;c., were well rendered. Vacs of thanks and cheers for Alderman Bennett were/accorded.</p>
        <p>Mr. Turton, in proposing a vote of thanks to Mr. Bibby for his kindness in occupying the chair, expressed the satisfaction of the boys' department that the shield had once more come to the Bolton Council School.</p>
        <p>The proceedings closed with the singing of</p>
        <p>he National An-them</p>
        <p>The books handed to the winner were of extreme usefulness and • charm—Lamb's "Tales from Shakespeare," Sir Henry Newbon's "Book of the Blue Sea," Captain A. H. Atteridge's "The Army shown to children," " Warrington Volunteers," and Alderman Bennett's "Some plain words to the English People."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>PICTURESQUENESS AT ST. JAMES' (LATCHFORD).</head>
        <p>PICTURESQUENESS AT ST. JAMES' (LATCHFORD).</p>
        <p>For the presentation at St. James' (Latch- ford) School, the Headmaster (Mr. W. H. Stansfield) gathered the upper standard chil- dren into the large room, giving Thomas Littler, • the second prize winner, a corner seat. His haul of books was: "The Navy," "Lord Kitchener," Lamb's "Tales from Shakes- peare," and "Warrington Volunteers." With majestic portraits of Britain's heroes— from the Duke of Wellington and Lord Nelson to John Travers Cornwell, the boy V.C.—the scene was splendidly appropriate and inspiring, and the children, under the leadership of Mr. C. George, sang with sweetness and vigour songs of charm and patriotism, including "Blow, blow, thou winter wind," and "Rule, Britannia." A verse each of "Land of Hope and Glory" was sung with expression by Clara Clough and Bryan Dunbavand, and a John Oxenham's stirring poem, extolling Cornwell, was recited in praiseworthy fashion by Gladys Timmis and Eric Clifton, each taking the first and second portions respectively. The Rev. 11. B. Firth presided and &amp;dared that duty was founded upon religion and patriotism was really based upon belief in God. The address by Alderman Bennett proved of great interest. He congratulated the school upon having won the shield three times and the second prize a similar number. It always seemed curious to him, he said, that at that end of the town the children always did well with Trafalgal Day essays, while at others they did poorly. The only conclusion he could draw was that the teachers and scholars there were keener patriots. The Alderman, in that graphic and reminiscent style of his which is ' intensely interesting, went on to speak of Shakespeare, Nelson and Patriotism, and his I occasional pauses to ask questions of his hear- ers kept them at attention all the time. At the conclusion they cheered him heartily and accorded him a vote of thanks, on the proposi- tion of the Headmaster, seconded by Master John Mincell.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>SECONDARY SCHOOL PATRIOTISM.</head>
        <p>THE SECONDARY SCHOOL PATRIOTISM.</p>
        <p>A fine musical programme was arranged at the Secondary Schools by Mr. Baxter, and Alderman Bennett was given a rousing recep- tion by the scholars, who cheered him to the echo. The songs, which were splendidly ren- dered by the pupils, included "God bless our native land," "Ye Mariners of England," Kipling's Recessional hymn, and that stirring part-song "Admirals all." Master Greenough sang "Long live the King" in fine style,. and Lillie Weston's recitation from "Richard II." was given with grace and freedom, and the patriotic sentiments respecting our " demi- paradise " and the "isle in the silver sea " were loudly applauded'. It was a happy coin- cidence that Alderman Bennett had', in a very appropriate speech, actually referred to these words himself, when he hoped that every boy and girl would endeavour to make England a</p>
        <p>happier and better country in the -future. Alderman Bennett mentioned the difficulties of the subject which had been given for competi- tion, viz. "Shakespeare. England, and. the War." He paid Ethel White Johnson the com- pliment of reading her essay, which was indeed a highly creditable terse and pointeA composi- tion.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. S. Broome, Principal of the Schools, said that in days to come the Battle of Jutland would be more important in history than the Battle of Trafalgar, and perhaps some patriotic citizen would then be good enough to give a nrize for an essay on Jutland Day as Alderman Bennett was doing now for Trafalgar Day. The prize essayists were :—Girls' prize, Ethel W. Johnson; Boys' prize, Norman Tilling. Mr. Broome's prizes for the lower classes were awarded to :—Girls, Marion Boyle; boys, JarLIS Woodhead.</p>
        <p>Master Dien gave a very spirited re.cital of Henry V.'s Speech, and Master Greenough accompanied the vocalistic items on the piano. The proceeding closed with the singing of the ':ationul Anne ni</p>
        <p>,! 4 1 ' . ' ,1., °Si '</p>
        <p>,t 4</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>"NO INTERVENTION."</head>
        <p>"NO INTERVENTION."</p>
        <p>The Briton's pledge is given, His hand unto the plough; He wins to Hell or Heaven As fate the dice may throw. He• striveth and hath striven, And will not turn him now.</p>
        <p>Do ye mistake his meaning, Beholder from afar Who have no likesome leaning? Ye serve another star, And cannot get the gleaning, Nor know what Britons are!</p>
        <p>The vision that allureth —To know his fellow, Friend—, The concord it ensureth, His purpose comprehend. The kingdom that endureth He serveth to the end.</p>
        <p>He shall not be defeated - Who holdeth Honour dear And with no strength unmeted Pursues in motive clear. To see the task completed Ordain he find him cheer!</p>
      </div>
