      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>Should Present Applicants Be Made To I If I 4: Join V.T.C. And Former Ones Escape?</head>
        <p>Should Present Applicants Be Made To I If I 4: Join V.T.C. And Former Ones Escape?</p>
        <p>IS IT JUST?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>By S.D.M.</head>
        <p>By S.D.M.</p>
        <p>Are Tribunal methods just as regards send- ing applicants to the V.T.C.? This is one of the questions that everybody is asking,' and no one seems able to enlighten us. The fact is, they are not altogether just, but the members have no option. How is it that men who re- ceived total exemptions a few months ago were not called upon to join the V.T.C., while men of to-day have the condition attached to their exemption? The only explanation I can give is —at the time that these men who are free came came before the Tribunals, the Tribunals had no power to make this condition. Only a short time ago they were given the power to make a man do voluntary service. This power they have exercised in full; and ,in doing so they have done what ought to have been done months ago. They have rendered their country a ser- vice, for a man who takes the Volunteer's oath and gwears to bear true allegiance is no longer a civilian, but instantly become a Toldier and is entitled to all soldier's rights and privilegea. He becomes a combatant, and is entitled under the Hague Convention to bear arms. which Means that in the case of grave emergency he would be entitled to defend his hearth .and home against any enemy. Surely this is what every man would like to do " if" the Germans invaded England. But let me again remind your readers that unless this 'oath is taken now. if the worst comes to the worst, civilians will have to stand by and only do what the military authorities consider non-combatant service; What are you called upon to do when you have taken the oath? Well, you are asked to sacri- fice an .hour or two each week for the purpose of drilling, which is one of the best tonics a man can have. If you are feeling down in the dumps get an hour's good drill, and I can assure you that you will reap the benefit. Try it! It beats all the doctor's medicine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>Revision Necessary.</head>
        <p>Revision Necessary.</p>
        <p>But this is not a recruiting article, and I am beginning to get a little bit away from m,ir argument. What I should like to know is: When are these former exemption certificates going to be revised? Is it fair that one set should be allowed to do nothing while another set are forced to join the V.T.C.? That's the argument,. and those are the plain facts. If the Government would only pass a Bill to fore all exempted men to join either the V.T.C. or Special Police, or perform orderly work for the Red Cross, then we should get along. Until that is done no one will feel satisfied. I have often seen men come to the V.T.C. against their will, and their one grievance is that other exempted men, in some cases their own business pals, are not forced to join. I can easily under- stand their feelings. Simply because these other men had the good fortune to get exempted early in the day they have got off scot free. It is " not good enough," and if these men have any patriotism at all they will take the oath voluntarily and put themselves on, a level with their friends.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>MR. HUGHES WAS RIGHT!</head>
        <p>MR. HUGHES WAS RIGHT!</p>
        <p>"He has given to the nation just the tonic that was needed," said Mr. Bonar Law, on the occasion of the farewell banquet to Mr. Hughes, the now famous Australian Premier. Mr. Bonar Law was, of course, referring to the series of forceful and inspiring speeches which Mr. Hughes had delivered during his tour in this country. "Now a tonic," said the Minister for the Colonies, "though sometimes nasty is always useful." Most people will agree with that statement, and also that the best stomach and liver tonic is Mother Seigel's Syrup. This it has been proved to be by tens of thousands of one-time sufferers from diges- tive troubles. Indigestion, biliousness, flatu- lence, pains after eating, acidity, constipation, and the like are all signs that your stomach, liver and bowels are not doing their work efficiently. If you are not feeling up to the mark, lacking the snap and vim you once had, probably all you need is the help of a really efficient stomach and liver tonic, such as Mother Seigel's Syrup, to tone up and strengthen these organs to • healthy activity. For nearly fifty years this famous remedy has been used with wonderful success as a ready means of banishing and preventing the dis- tressing symptoms which arise, from a dis- ordered state of the principal organs of digestion. This is the secret of its long- standing, world-wide reputation!</p>
        <p>Not a True Dream. An American farmer, hearing a noise in the night, went to the back of his house,,anci.feund one of his black labourers in his fowl-house. "Joe, you rascal, what are you doing here?" he exclaimed. "It's all right, sah," replied J 0:.,; "it's owing to a dream that I am here, sah." "A dream, you black thief! What do you mean?" "Well, sah, I dreamt I was ,goin' to have chicken to-morrow foi. dinnah; but I'm 'fraid 'taint true, sah!"</p>
      </div>
