      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 21, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 21, 1916.</p>
        <p>a big difference. If you have got an ' Ex- aminer ' you might send it on, for I should like to know how things are at Lymm."</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal William Jackson.</p>
        <p>Lance-Corpl. Win. Jackson, who is sa comrade af Private Joseph Clare, and was wounded at the same time, is progressing favourably. A letter from the Chaplain received by his par- ents at Newfields yesterday, says that the gal- lant soldier is still seriously ill, but improving, though not yet fit to be removed. Both are members of the Cheshire Regiment.</p>
        <p>of this conversion, free from all class distinc- tion that has bound us in the past; the inner truth is only revealed to the childlike, for chil- dren have no class distinctions. We must begin by looking through the observant eyes of a child. Never again will we be satisfied with the old, easy-going Gospel, and we will pray in future with a new meaning and new spirit the Lord's own prayer, "Thy kingdom come." The following hymns ,!ve,re sung, accompanied by the choir and church organist, Mr. Wm. Cook :—Processional hymn, "Onward, Christian soldiers"; National Anthem, "God save the King"; "Oh, Lord, how joyful 'tis to see " ; "For all the saints who from their labours rest"; "Lord of our life, and God of our salva- tion"; and Psalm lxvii., "God be merciful unto us." The offertory was in aid of the nurses' fund and the sick and .poor fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>Is I:44T!"lcr pinyi, tj</head>
        <p>Is I:44T!"lcr pinyi, tj</p>
        <p>lIYMIVI</p>
        <p>SEND A CHRISTMAS PUDDING TO "TOMMY."</p>
        <p>In to-day's Examiner" you will see that the Editor has started a fund to support the effort that is being made to send every soldier on active service a pl,um pudding for Christmas Day.</p>
        <p>This is the only authorised feheme for send- ing Christmas puddings to the Front and has been undertaken. by official request to relieve the authorities of the impossible task of de- livering 3,000,000 separate puddings in the trenches.</p>
        <p>If you have a boy at the Front, or are a friend of a boy at the Front, or have a friend with a. boy at the Front, send him a Christmas pudding through our fund—the only way in which the War Office will guarantee its delivery. Send'your shillings to</p>
        <p>THE EDITOH, " Examiner " Office, Warrington.</p>
        <p>mission of a rose," Miss W. Williams; song, "Soldier's grave," Mr. J. Willett; song "Annie Laurie," Madame J. Armstrong; humorous Se- lection, "Simple country curate," Mr. W. W. Whittle; and a vast number of encores. Dur- ing the interval Mr. H. Sinclair (the- chair- man) stated that it was just about four years since they first opened their branch in Lymm. The first week's takings amounted to £29 175.; now they averaged £215 a week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>LOCAL FACTS AND FANCIES.</head>
        <p>LOCAL FACTS AND FANCIES.</p>
        <p>By CRITIC.</p>
        <p>All went well last Sunday. Everything went off Al. Everyone was satisfied.</p>
        <p>Although it was only a specially organised band for the day, the players acquitted them- selves admirably, and deserve to be compli- mented.</p>
        <p>Then I must not forget to compliment the officials of the Tradesmen's Association for the able way in which they conducted the proces- -don,</p>
        <p>The bugle band attached to the rleatley Troop of Boy. Scouts kept things going when the augmented band took a•rest.</p>
        <p>And to use a term uttered by one of the Volunteers, "We never lost a step all the time."</p>
        <p>Those who went to the service were well re- paid, for the sermon so ably rendered by Canon Bannister the Archbishop's messenger) was one of the finegt I have ever heard.</p>
        <p>Even his praypr for the Councils was up-to- date, and was very much noticed.</p>
        <p>He termed them "the local • governing body," but, of course, it means the same.</p>
        <p>I hear that one of our local wounded at Brookfield celebrated his twenty-first birthday last Saturday.</p>
        <p>Needless to say, his comrades rose to the occasion and presented him with a "most suit- able" present.</p>
        <p>I won't divulge any secrets as regards the form the presentation took," but it was some- thing we very often see but seldom posses.</p>
        <p>And liardly .ever covet.</p>
        <p>My advice to Min is: Put it at one side, it -may, save a billing some day. One never knows does one?</p>
        <p>What do-you_think about the appointment or officers for the Cheshire Volunteer Regiment?</p>
        <p>Platoon Commander G. R. Williamson, of our local platoon of Volunteers, was appointed to the rank of Lieutenant in last Monday's "Gazette."</p>
        <p>Don't you think it is about time they were given some recognition?</p>
        <p>I hear rumours that the appointment is going to be kept up by a hot-pot supper.</p>
        <p>District Medical Officer of Heaith.—Dr. Garstang, medical officer of health sto the Bucklow Board of Guardians, Buck Rural Council, and the F:rutsford Urban i3ouncil, has intimated .his intention of resigning at the end of the year. •</p>
        <p>Zepp. Time Bell—By arrangements with the churchwardengt of the Parish Church it has been decided to ring the church bell-for two or three minutes prior to . Zeplpelin time each evening to warn residents to draw their blinds and shade all lights.</p>
        <p>Railway Staff's "Adopted " Prisoner.—Pte. E. Worrall (from whom Mr. D. Daniels, Booths Hill, received a postcard last week) is the pris- oner of war in Germany to whom the railway staff at Lymm Station send a parcel of com- forts every, month. They " adopted " him at the latter end of 1914.</p>
        <p>Unshaded Lights.—Three Lymm residents were prosecuted at Wednesday's Sessions for failing to reduce or shade the lights in their shops or dwelling-houses on various dates. The following fines were inflicted: E. Ward, Bridge- water-street, 58.; Wm. Rouse, electrician, Rush Green, 10s.•' Temperance Tipping, shopkeeper, Lymm-lane Crossing, ss.</p>
        <p>Platoon=Commander G. R. Willialgson Gazet- ted.—The public will be interested to hear that Platoon-Commander George Royds Williamson, of the local Volunteers, has been gazetted and appointed to the rank of temporary lieutenant, attached to No. 1 Battalion of the Cheshire Volunteer Regiment. He has been commander of the local Volunteers since they were organised at the beginning of the war, and is exceed- ingly well liked by his men. He has worked his platoon up to the highest state of efficiency.</p>
        <p>Baptist Church, Higher-lane.—At the harvest thanksgiving services the Pastor (the Rev. Edward E. Lovell) was the preacher for .the day. Miss Bessie Wright, of Lymm, and Miss Snelling, of Warrington, were the soloists, and their services durirg the day were much ap- preciated. Gifts of flowers and fruit were plentiful, and the church was prettily decorated by Mrs. Lovell, Miss Dainttee, Mrs. Gibbon, Miss Lovell, Miss Brundrett, Mr. Ackerley, Mr. Moss, and various other members of the church and Sunday school. The collections taken up during the day *ere in aid of the Church Funds.</p>
        <p>Illegal Use of Railway Ticket.—At Lymin Petty Sessions on Wednesday, Mrs. Elizabeth Anne Owen, Warburton Green, Warburton, was prosecuted .by the London and North-Western Railway Company for having on the 18th August travelled- by-train between. Heatley and Broad- heath stations with a workman's ticket, for Which she had not paid, and which had been used in the early part of the week by her daughter.—:-Mr. C. W. Pride, solicitor, Euston, represented the company. It appeared that on the date in question defendant used the weekly ticket which had been issued for her daughter on the 14th August. When approached by the railway company's detective (Harry Ernest Hewitt) Mrs. Owen stated that she did use her daughter's ticket, but the latter had got a work- man's daily ticket on that occasion. She said she did not know she was &amp;ping wrong.—Defend- ant was fined ss. and alsO ordered to pay 10s. costs.</p>
        <p>Co.-op. Concert.—The annual concert given by the. Education Committee of the Warrington Co-operative Society took place at the Drill Hall on Thursday evening, and was enjoyed by a large attendance.. The chair as taken by Mr. H. Sinclair, who was assisted by Messrs. Geo. T. Rushton and F. Berry, and the following splendid programme was rendered: Duet, "The battle ere," Messrs. Pearce and Willett; song, " Soldier boys," Miss W. Williams; song, "Young Royalist," Mr. E. Pearce; song, " En- chantress," Madame J. Armstrong; 'song, "My Queen," Mr. J. Willett; humorous selection, "I do love my wife," Mr. W. W. Whittle; song, "Bugler," Mr. E. Pearce; song, "The</p>
        <p>merchant, of Bridgewater-street. His brothers, Privates Thomas and William Drinkwater, both of the South Lancs., are at present somewhere in France.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>PRIVATE GEORGE DRINKWATER.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE GEORGE DRINKWATER.</p>
        <p>" Nothing Worse Than A Wound In Left Thigh."</p>
        <p>I Private George Drinkwater, _writing to his wife at Sandy-lane from The Cottage Hospital, Haywards Heath, Sussex, says:—" You will be glad to know that I have landed in good old ` Blighty ' again. It's quite a treat to get in bed again after 13 months in France. I° am very sorry I could not get towards Manchester; but never mind, when I get better I shaill get a sick leave. It's the first time I have been in bed for 13 months, so you can picture what it is like. I got hit at , the place you hear so much about in the papers. It was murder. Only two days before I was going up to the trenches when one of our gun team got killed. He was walk- ing next to me, and the shell that hit him smashed the butt of my rifle •and broke my entrenching tool, so I think I am lucky to get out of it all with nothing worse than a wound in the left thigh." Private George Drinkwater,is a very popular local man. He enlisted in the South Lan-.%. Regiment in October, 1914, and was drafted out to France in September, 1915. He is 29 years of age, mar,ried, and has four children. He was educated at Lymm School and worshipped at the Parish Church. Prior to enlisting he was employed as a Carter by Mr. Wm. Hinton, coal</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>Private Harry Cadman.</head>
        <p>Private Harry Cadman.</p>
        <p>Private Harry Cadman, also of the Cheshire Regiment, reported by us as down with disease, has also landed ip England. Writing to some relatives at Brookfield Cottages from Birming- ham, the gallant soldier says: "I am in hos- pital again in 'Blighty,' which I know you will be glad to hear. Do not worry about me, for l am quite all right, only a little weak. I can- not tell you how long I shall be here, but I shall most likely go to Blackpool next, con- valescent. It is much better being here than out there, for it is murder on the Somme. That's where I came from. I can tell you lam 'glad to get away." His home address. is Booths Hill.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>FATAL ACCIDENT.</head>
        <p>FATAL ACCIDENT.</p>
        <p>Succumbs To Injuries Received At Work.•</p>
        <p>We regret to announce the death of Mr. Geo. Frederick Shakeshaft (31), of Rush Green, Lymm, which took 'place at the Manchester Royal Infirmary last Saturday morning. He was employed by the Partington Steel and Iron Company. Falling several feet from a ladder, he fractured his skull. He was immediately conveyed to the Manchester Royal Infirmary, hut, despite the best of attention, he succumbed to his injuries. The funeral took place at the Parish Church on Tuesday afternoon and was conducted by the Rev. G. B. Thurston, in the presence of a large number of relatives and friends. The principal mourners were his wife and mother, Messrs. Frank, Herbert, Jack, Harry, Tom, Isaac and William Shakeshaft (brothers), Miss Polly Shakeshaft (sister), Mr. and Mrs. Frank Jackson (brother-in-law and sister), Miss Edith Shakeshart (niece), Mr. Frank Shakeshaft (nephew), Mrs. Brierley (cousin), Mrs. H. Shakeshaft (sister-in-law), Mr. and Mrs. Cowsill (uncle and aunt), Mr. and Mrs. C. Booth (brother-in-law and sister), Mrs. Armstrong (sister-in-law), Mrs. Hadden (aunt); Mr. W. Wright, Mr; T. Kerfoot, and the Misses H. and C. Middleton..</p>
        <p>The bearers were Messrs. A. Hulme, William Southam, J. Elliott and Wi Wright.</p>
        <p>The funeral arrangements were conducted by Mr. Hugh Holt, Deceased, who was in his 31st year, leaves a wife and four children.</p>
        <p>A large number of floral tributes were re-</p>
        <p>LYMM VOLUNTEERS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>Platoon Orders.</head>
        <p>Platoon Orders.</p>
        <p>To-day (Saturday).—Battalion Parade, Lymm Station, 2.35 p.m. Tuesday', October 24th.-7.30 p.m., Recruits' Drill; 8 p.m., Platoon Drill. Thursday, October 26th.-7.30 p.m., Recruits' Drill; 8 p.m., Platoon Drill. Orderlies for the Week.—Sergeant Moston; Privates Moston and Hatton. G. R. WILLIAMSON, Platoon Commander.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>CITIZEN SUNDAY.</head>
        <p>CITIZEN SUNDAY.</p>
        <p>Many Citizens Attend Annual Service</p>
        <p>At The Parish Church.</p>
        <p>Despite the torrential rain that fell through- out-the early hours of Sunday morning, (mite a large crowd of citizens attended the annual service which takes place every Citizens' Sun- day at the Parish Church; As in former years a procession was formed at the Cross and marched to the church via. Eagle Brow. It was headed by a band specially organised for the occasion, and followed by., the Heatley Troop of Boy Scouts with their Bugle Band, under the command of Scoutmaster Theodore Nash; wounded soldiers from Brookfield; Lymm Platoon, No. 1 Battalion, Cheshire Volunteer Regiment, under the command of Lieut. G. R,. Williamson and Sergt. S. D. Moston ; Oughtring- ton Platoon, No. 1 Battalion, Cheshire Volun- teer Regiment, under the command of Capt. Hawkins, R.E., and Sergt. T. Peters; special police, under Leader W. G. Groves; firemen, under Capt. Wm. Rose; police, under Sergt. Davies; members of the Urban District Coun- cil; tradesmen; friendly societies; and various other individual citizens. Officials of the Trades- men's Association officiated as M.C.'s.</p>
        <p>The procession arrived at the church about 3 p.m., where special seating accommodation had been reserved for them, and a special service was held, conducted by Canon Bannister (the Arch- bishop's messenger, canon-residentiary of Here- ford Cathedral, and ararden of St. Katherine's Hospital, Ledbury), assisted by the Revs. G. B. Thurston, Horake Gray and E. C. Lovell. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>Canon on Changing Times.</head>
        <p>Canon on Changing Times.</p>
        <p>Addressing the congregation, which filled the entire church. the Canon explained the objects of the mission, the Mission of Repentance and Hope. He asked God to place His blessings on our local governing board and make them good citizens. Never in history had. the well-to-do class been brought more in contact with the working class than during the last two years in Flanders. Rich and poor had fought side by side, and in many cases paid the supreme sacri- fice together. The social feeling that existed before fbewar had been changed. out there, and how-The change would come about at home de- pended entirely on us. Everyone must be pre- pa*d to *make a real 'sacrifice, and the Church could show the way as no other agent could. We had been a wasteful nation, but the worst of all things had been the waste of religion. "What can we do as individuals?" the Canon proceeded. "Except a man be born again" (said the Lord) "he cannot see the Kingdom-of God." Some of us who are possibly good Christians are in need</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>NATIONAL MISSION INAUGURATED</head>
        <p>THE NATIONAL MISSION INAUGURATED</p>
        <p>The citizens' service had been preceded by a public meeting on Saturday to inaugurate the National Mission in Lymm, Oughtrington, Warburton, Dunham Massey, Bollington and High Legh parishes. The meeting took place in the Drill Hall, after a procession, which started from the Cross. The Rector presided over a large attendance, and was supported by Canon Bannister, Canon Wainwright, and the Rev. G. S. Tudor-Evans, the Rev. Horace Gray, the Rev. J. A. D. Surridge (High Legh) and Mrs. Ridgway. "Don't let us have the clamour we used to have about the rights of property and the rights of working men," exclaimed the Messenger in the course of his address, "Let us think about the responsibility of property, the responsibility of the working men, and filen we shall find that the rights will look after themselN es. If this Mission gets hold of the nation's soul we shall entirely lose the bitterness of class feeling and take in a- sense of fellowship and brotherhood—a feeling that we ,are one nation with common interests and the common worship of one God." Mrs. Ridgway said: "God did not send us into a finished world to enjoy it, but into an unfinished world to 'redeem it, and it is in our own homes, in the midst of our own people and daily duties that we have to redeem our mission. Each one of us is a missioner, and from our individual experience we must teach others the beautiful message of brotherhood. An opportunity has come to us, and shall we not respond to this great National Mission of Repentance and Hope? Shall we not in deadly earnest ask for God's forgiveness and dedicate ourselves to Him?"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>CIIESIIIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT.</p>
        <p>Grappenhall Detachment.</p>
        <p>Orders for week ending Saturday, Oct. 28th: Mon.day.—Musketry at-range, 7.30 p.m. Range Officer, Pte. J. F. Fairhurst.</p>
        <p>Tuesday.—Musketry instruction. Fall in at headquarters, 7.45 p.m. Thursday.—Squad drill at headquarters, 7.45 Pm-</p>
        <p>Friday.—Musketry at range for recruits, 7.30 p.m. Names of those required to attend will be posted at Headquarters. Range Officer, Platoon- Sergeant C. G. Hutchings. - Saturday.—Company drill. Fall in at head- quarters at 3 p.m. Orderlies.—Sec.-Commander W. Trantora, Pte. J. Scott. By order, F. A. FROST (Lieutenant) O.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>WIN WICK.</head>
        <p>WIN WICK.</p>
        <p>KEEN ON FRUIT.</p>
        <p>Youths Raid Mr. J. W. Knowles' Orchard.</p>
        <p>Fines of 10s. each were meted out at the War- rington Petty Sessions on Wednesday to five Vulcan youths named James Roberts, Richard Miler, Wm. BrockleY, Samuel' Brockley and Jos. Dearden, who were charged with stealing 101 b, of apples and pears, valued 25., from an orchard at Winwick on October 4th.</p>
        <p>Inspector Clare said that on Saturday after- noon Sergeant Walker and P.C. Gorse were on duty near Mr. J. W. Knowles' orchard, The Towers, Middleton, when they saw the defend- ants onne out of the orchard. The policemen gave chase, and each caught one of the defend- ants, who had apples and pears in their pockets. They gave the 'names and addresses of those who had run away, and on the following Sunday all the defendants went to the Padgate Police Station and brought the stolen fruit with them. Continuing, the Inspector said they had had many complaints of this nature, and that was the fifth gang Mr. Knowles had had in hrs orchard that day. Early in the afternoon another constable had ,seen a gang of four young men near the Rector of Winwiek's orchard, but he (the Inspector) was not prepared to state whether the defendants had anything to do with it. Such occurrences were serious matters.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Detachment.</p>
        <p>Orders for week commencing 22nd October : Sunday.—Route March postponed for a week. Monday.—Company and Recruit Drill, Head- quarters, 7.45. . • Thursday.—Recinits' Drill, Headquarters, 7.45 Sergt.-Major Stirrup. Signalling Class. Lamp Practice, 7.45, Headquarters; Sergt. Fergusson. Friday.—Swedish Drill, Headquarters, 7.45; Sergt.-Instructor Mottram. Sunday (29th).—Route March, Delamere; fall in Victoria-square, 9.45. Dismiss, 5.45; one ration to be carried and great coats rolled. FRINIC WARBURTON, Officer Commanding.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXI.</p>
        <p>THE LION'S BRIDE</p>
        <p>A. PICTURISED ROMANTIC NOVEL BY ROY tivicOWELL ADAPTED FROM THE SENSATIONAL -FLYING LISEIIAL FILM' BY ALEC J. BRAID..</p>
        <p>[Characters in this story are purely imaginary, living</p>
        <p>From the scene of his crime Jack Williams drove the horse and rickety old waggon madly. Ever before him in the darkness was the contorted face of a •dead man, a man whose life he could have saved but for greed. Dangling at. his breast was the jewel for which he had bartered his soul. Driven recklessly, the horse stumbled as a wheel struck a boulder. The waggon went over, and the man was flung out head first on the road. Cursing the deed he had done, and the trumpery he had done it for, the bill-poster roused himself and wiped the blood that ran into his eyes from a deep cut in his forehead, held a handkerchief to the aching wound, and limped off into the night, but still holding fast to the diamond. Marmaduke Smythe also fled from the fallen tree where he found the man, and ran, forgetting his own fears and misery at the haunting memory of this phantom of the Dimly revealed, were the grey roof and dull white walls of the ranch- house by the bee-yard. Towards it Alarmaduke ran wildly and hammered furiously with both fists on the door. The rancher came down to the door in answer to the clamouring summons. Behind him his frightened wife held high a lamp. Frayed and tattered, more like a tramp than a prim man of the law, the half-hysterical Englishman told his broken story of a dead man lying beneath a burn- ing tree; and then, as though he would put the horrid occurrence behind him, the strange messen- ger turned and fled again. Yet, for all his fright and all his panic, he clung instinctively to the deer •head that had been his impedimenta, beloved and cherished since he was safe at Stanleigh Hall. How long ago that was, whether days or weeks or months, liarma duke Smythe could not• have told. The worst of Marmaduke's hardships in the dark continent were over. At a distant ranch he pre- vailed upon a farmer to drive him to *the city. Smythe and his precious deer head rode twenty miles in more or less comfort, and arrived dusty, tattered and torn at the towii. At the straggling outskirts he plucked up courage again, and then when they drove up a business street, to the sign "King George Hotel" above a somewhat middle- class hostelry' to have so high sounding a name, Smythe clutched the old rancher by the arm. "If you will stop here I will alight, thank you," he said.</p>
        <p>The farmer stopped his horse at the kerb. The day clerk, loitering at the doorway, smiled at the eccentric figure before him. "I have been lost in the wilderness and suffered untold hardships for a fortnight," stammered Smythe, "Could I secure a room and bath?" "Sure," said the matter-of-fact hotel clerk. "You can have two rooms and two baths if you pay for them." Marmaduke took off his hat, pressed it in a reverential manner to his breast, gazed at the sign at the doorway of the hotel, and cried fervently, "God save the King.",</p>
        <p>The Stanleigh spirit, of determination glowed in Esther's heart. Until she had seen Arthur and spoken to him she would not leave Los Angeles, and she called at the mansion until the manservant, prompted by Vivian, told her that "Mr. Powell" had been taken into the cour try. Blair Stanleigh was chafing under the domineer- ing attitude of Durand. The coming of the "King of Diamonds" annoyed him, and in ,the office of the Fair Hope Oil Company he complained to Vivian of her friendship for the schemer.</p>
        <p>"I hate this*way of winning the diamond. This method of dragging his manhood out of him with drugs is not my way."</p>
        <p>"Perhaps not. but managing this business and reaping its benefits appeals to you, doesn't it?" asked Vivian.</p>
        <p>Blair would have retorted nastily had not a clerk brought in a telegram from Mrs. Randolph Burton appealing to him to return to his mother, who was dying of paralysis. "I must go back. Vivian," said the young man. "My mother needs me, she must not die before I see her."</p>
        <p>"Do not leave me here. Blair," cried the girl. "If you are away Durand and De Vaux will do me out of everything. What good can you do if you do go home?" she asked, flinging her arms around his neck.</p>
        <p>Once again Blair's better nature was conquered by the fascination of the woman who allowed nothing to stand in the way of her desires. Mrs. Stanleigh could die for all she cared.</p>
        <p>"The big blow off" of Santley's Stupendous Circus, as its proprietor -would,say, was undoubt- edly La Belle, the Lady of Lions. Splinters, the clown, was a big hit, too. But La Belle's act with the great fierce Nubian lion Lancelot was the " blow:off." It was the last act on the bill, and *sent the crowds away thrilled and satisfied. Of herself, La Belle was new to the show this season, and Splinters was her husband. She was bold and handsome, and Splinters, the husband, was tor- mented with many a bitter pang of jealousy through her coquetries—to describe it mildly. Sam Santley, proprietor of Santley's Stupendous Circus, was what was known as a working boss. Through devious ways—luck at gambling and by the exercise of sharp practices—he had gained his way from card shark to ownership of a show that bore his name. Santley personally supervised his own show from top to bottom. He acted as ring- master and announcer when the star acts were on. When there was anything to be done, Sam Sant- ley did it himself. As for the ladies—" Janes," he called them—Mr. Santley also monopolised the fair. Woe to any of his circus people who transgressed the bounds of strict morality with his show. But with himself it was another matter, and Sam Sant- ley looked with longing eyes on La Belle, the Lady of Lions. In the performers' entrance she had given him a smile. Splinters had seen it, and his face was contorted in jealous hatred under his make-up. _Then Sam Santley had gone to the " privilege " tent, wh4re his advertising men and the followers of the circus generally met for social converse.</p>
        <p>At the entrance to the " privilege " tent Sam</p>
        <p>and have no connection with or relation to any person.]</p>
        <p>Santley, in his ringmaster's costume, was accosted by a grimy,, shambling, shaking man in a stained and tattered suit of paste-marked-Overalls. "What's the matter with you? Get out of the way," growled Santley. "1 am Williams, boss—Jack Williams. I was out with the billposting outfit, don't you remem- ber?"</p>
        <p>"Why aren't you out with it now?" snarled Santley. "Got drunk, I suppose, and quit the job cold. Get off the lot before you're kicked off. As for any money, you don't get a cent. Beat it." "But, listen, boss! I wasn't drunk. I didn't quit. I walked 100 miles like a crazy man. A tree fell on the waggon in the woods way back in the interior and killed Ben Burk and the horse, and smashed the waggon-"</p>
        <p>"Come in and tell me," interrupted Santley, for the loss of a horse and billposting waggon was something to worry about. That Burk lit ad been killed did not matter much. Billposters cost noth- ing. It was different with horses and waggons. Then seeing Williams was shaking as if with ague, Santley ordered a drink of whisky from his private stock for him. Williams gulped it down, and told his story as best suited him. But he said no word regarding the diamond, which had been the real cause of the death of Burk.</p>
        <p>The men in the tent were playing a game with dice, ind watching them Santley mentally calcu- lated that a hundred dollars would cover his loss. Then the cynical idea occurred to him that he could recoup himself by winning. Throwing down a hun- dred dollar bill he shouted, "Have a stake worth Dlaying for."</p>
        <p>Ragged, unkempt, without a dollar in his pocket, and realising Santley would absolve, himself from any wages that might be due to him, owing to the loss of the horse and waggon, the miserable bill- poster eyed the hundred dollar bank note hungrilY. Instinctively his hand searched his clothes, and, as if drawn by fate, the wretched man brought forth The Diamond from the Sky. , Santley eyed the chain and curiously. Ile thought how, it would adorn the fair 'throat of the coquettish La Belle, the Lady of Lions. "I shoot a hundred dollars against the junk," he growled, and tossed the dice to Williams. The necklace with the great diamond clattered to the table. Williams seized the dice with trembling hand and rolled them, but Santley, his ck in, lu cast higher and won. That night La Belle wnreha t the jewel. disregarding her hus'band's threat t he would kill her and Santley if she didn't plaY fair.</p>
        <p>The arrival of the circus in Los Angeles attracted all classes. Rich and poor alike flocked to the travelling shows, and the fame of Santley's fit-out did not entirely depend upon the gorgeousness of the posters he plastered town and country with. Quabba's tastes were in that direction, but dust now he was seeking information. Esther did not believe that Arthur had been .moved from his home' and her faithful protector sought information from a fellow-countryman, the gardener at the mansion. "No," said he, "the boss is sick, but has not gone away. The nurse told me they all• go to the circus to-day."</p>
        <p>Physically Arthur was improving rapidly, but the drug habit was growing upon him as Durand hoird it would. Gradually he was falling under its dire influence. In this way the plotters were successful, although they failed to drive the memory of Esther from his mind.</p>
        <p>"Vivian, you nursed me back from death; nov; tell me, where is Esther? Wasn't she here?”_.</p>
        <p>Durand, overhearing the question, replied: “your terrible injuries cause these hallucinations. It „i! a bad sign when you think you see this girl. Figr" against it, my boy." "Yes, my work .„,as nurse is finished," replie4 Vivian, to a furtlflier question. "You are well enough now, but no 'business, no excitement. Jus' amusement is what you want, and this afternoon we are all going to the circus."</p>
        <p>- - The circus band had leaded a procession through the town. Lancelot the lion, the bears, and the tigers in their cages had thrilled the 'young people; now, with the band blaring music outside the great tent, people were flocking into the theatre. Among them, pushing his way forward, was Quabba, Who had purchased a couple of tickets for the show. Esther had said to him, "We are,not going awal until I speak to him, and not then if he wishes we to stay here with him. We will go to the circus, and I may have an opportunity of speaking t° him."</p>
        <p>His wife false to him. Splinters the clown was resolved upon a dreadful revenge. While the clowns were fooling and the riap.,; showing their prowess, the Stanleigh party took their seats la the private box at the end of the aisle. A little while later Esther and Quabba wdlked 'past and occupied seats near at hand. All eyes were turned upon the arena, where the proprietor was announcing the thrilling and sense' tional act to follow: "La Belle, the Lady of Lionel and Lancelot, the superb, untamed Nubian King el Beasts." At that moment Esther caught sight Arthur, and was creeping down to speak to hire when suddenly all in the box rose to' their feet with a gasp of astonishment, for on the breast of Vie, woman who had entered the cage was The Diamona from the Sky. Then a loud cry of himor rose, and the vas audience heaved as a wave heaves, for the lion had sprung at the woman ere her smirk could changl to a look of fear, and had struck her down, sae with his cruel claws rent her fair bosom and tare away the gleaming jewel there. ,</p>
        <p>, Santley shrieked and sprang forward, and 3 •ust then the clown Splinter, standing behind hiim drew a pistol from his pierrot garb and shot hira, immediately turning the pistol upon himself. But Esther had no eyes for the dreadful scene-, She called to Arthur, who, turning with a 'ell °I " There's Esther," stretched out his arms towarcls her. "Wait for me," he shouted above the din but the crowd, surging in panic, pushed her avial' and once again the lovers were separated. (To be continued.)</p>
        <p>. - -144-4,1:- 4-44. = - - • _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>PRIVATE PERCY CLARE.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE PERCY CLARE.</p>
        <p>Writing home to Booth's Hill, Private Percy Clare says : "Thanks for parcel -received to-day. I .am sorry you have not had such good news about poor Joe and William (meaning Private Joseph Clare and Lance-Corporal William Jackson). but I hope and pray that you will have better news next time you hear from them. I have not much to say this time, so Good-night and God bless you. I am living in hopes of being with you once again before long and still trust- ing in God to pull us through. Give my love to all at home and tell Joe's mother I thank her for the cigarettes she sent. Tell her to be brave and try to look on the bright side of things and trust in God to pull Joe through, Which I think He will. If he can only get over this he will be about finished with this war. I think he has done his share, and done it bravely and Private Percy Clare is well known to "Exam- iner" readers. „.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>NEWS OF THE WOUNDED.</head>
        <p>NEWS OF THE WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>Private Jos. Clare Laughs Now He Is In "Blighty."</p>
        <p>It gives us the greatest of pleasure to say that Pte. Joseph Clare, who was so seriously wounded some time ago, has arrived in this country. Writing -home to Booths Hill from the V.A.D. Hospital, Perth, Scotland, the gal- lant soldier says: I don't want you to expect a letter every day, for I am lying flat on my back, and I have not to do much writing. lam sorry I have landed so far away, but don't worry, for everytking is done to make us corn- fortable. We hay 7 ,no need to' be in want of anything, for they are so kind to us. I am going on as well as can be expected. At the time I was at Rouen I was in awful agony, so bad that almost every minute of the day I was asking them to take my leg off, but since landed here I have been as different again. I am laughing now, for coming to 'Blighty' makes</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>69, LONDON ROAD, STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>69, LONDON ROAD, STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <p>00 .00 4, 0 0.0• 440 ••••••• + .0.0 0 0 46)0.4 • • 4. 4. • • 0 0 +OOOOOOOOO 00000+0+00 00000+0000 40 000 000 0</p>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>only just pulled down in time, while Brookes ran over after good forward work, but was pre- vented from grounding.</p>
        <p>(Lancashire Section.)</p>
        <p>A huge kick by J. Jolley relieved the situa- tion, but Warrington were soon back again, the forwards, who were a much heavier set than Runcorn, giving the baeks -numerous oppor- tunities. Good work by Butterworth forced Warrington back, and J. Jolley only just failed with a: long penalty shot. Rupcorn pressed and Flanagan was kept out with difficulty, after having picked up smartly. Banner brought Ben Jolley down near the line after the visit- ing custodian had beaten man after man. Dol- ing some stiff play near the Warrington posts, Walker snapped up a pass intended for all opponent, and scored a good try near the posts, J. Jolley giving his side the lead amidst applause. Warrington now pressed, and Hr- rocks went over, but was brought back for an alleged forward pass. Wairington kept up the pressure, and eventually Douglas went over for a good try, B. Jolley failing at goal. Warring- ton pressed until the interval, the arrival of which found the score: Warrington, two tries, 6 points; Runcorn, one goal, one try, 5 points.</p>
        <p>Upon resuming. Stevenson broke away for the home team, but Brookes brought him down In fine style. J. Jolley and Walker passed well, but Tranter , touched down from the former. player's kick. The visitors then attacked, and Potter missed a fine opportunity of scoring. Horrocks now exchanged positions with Jolley, and cleared well from Richardson. Flanagan was pushed into touch after a (good combined movement, and a moment later Douglas was tackled when attempting to break through. Warrington were driven back to the quarter flag, where a scrimmage was formed. From this Brookes received, and, breaking away smartly, gave to Cunliffe. The forward ran splendidly, and outpacing all his opponents, scored a grand try, which Jolley converted. Soon afterwards Warrington were awarded a penalty, and Jolley kicked a good goal. Run- corn were • now always on the defence, and nearing the finish Tranter scored Warrington's fourth try, Jolley kicking the goal.</p>
        <p>. , Warrington held a strong advantage in the forward line, and Tranter, Brookes and Doug- las were clever backs. For the home team none did better than Walker, Richardson, Spruce aid Hambleton.</p>
        <p>Result: Warrington; three goals, four tries, 18 points; Runcorn, one goal, one try, 5 points.</p>
        <p>The teams were :</p>
        <p>Runcorn: D. Butterworth, back; Jolley. (J.), Flanagan, Walker and Okell. threequarters; Harn- bleton and Banner, half-backs; Richardson, Spruce, Shields, Stevenson, Nickson and Booth, forwards. Warrington: Jolley (B.), back; Griffiths, Tranter, Potter and Horrocks, threequarters; Brookes and Douglas, half-backs- Harmer, Cunliffe, Matthewa, Glegg, Ward and Jones. forwards. Referee, Mr. C. Jameson (Wigan).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>18 POINTS TO 5.</head>
        <p>18 POINTS TO 5.</p>
        <p>Warrington Trounce Runcorn</p>
        <p>On Own Ground.</p>
        <p>Having trounced the " Linnets " at Wilders- pool in the third match of the season, by twenty points to nil, Warrington journeyed to R.uncorn on Saturday to play the return match, and were again successful by eighteen points to five. The visitors again had Jolley at back, while Brookes partnered Douglas at the base of the scrum, Young having joined the Army during the week.</p>
        <p>A late start was made before a poor "gate." Runcorn had a strong wind in their favour. The first incident of note was a. good kick and fellow-up by the Warrington custodian, but the ball rolled into touch. Following the scrim- mage, Brookes and Potter handled well' for Warrington, but the movement was neatly checked. The Warrington forwards made r combined rush, but Butterworth cleared, and a few minutes later at the other end Jolley was forced to touch down from a rush by Shields and Hambleton. The latter was smart at the base of the scrum for Runcorn, and initiated several clever movements, but he was not sup- ported as he might have been.</p>
        <p>With the breeze in their favour, Runcorn pressed for- some time, but Douglas and Cunliffe .Changed the venue, following which Tranter made a fine run and gave to Griffiths, who ran over for a try. Jolley failed at goal. The visi- tors kept up the pressure, and Horrocks was</p>
        <p>FR</p>
        <p>'PLE</p>
        <p>nd your name and</p>
        <p>ress and 3 penny stamps for postage of trial bottle FREE. Wholesale Depot: 86 Clerkenwell Road, London.</p>
