      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>OTHER RESULTS.</head>
        <p>OTHER RESULTS.</p>
        <p>G. T. r. G. T. P. Salford Leigh Broughton R. .. 0 3 9 St. Helens ..... 1 2 8 Widnes ..... 1 1 5 St. Helens R.— 0 0 0 Wakefield T. ..... 1 2 8 Rochdale H. .. 0 0 0 Bramley Hull</p>
        <p>Hull Kingston 1 5 17 Huddersfield ... 0 0 0 Bradford Batley Dewsbury</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS AT WILDERSPOOL TO-DAY. Warrington are at home to-day to St. Helens, and should record another victory. St. Helens were beaten at home las) ,week by nine points to eight, Broughton Rangers being their vanquishers. The home team will be selected from :—Jolley; Horrocks, Tranter, Potter, Griffiths, and Mills; Brookes, Young, and Douglas; Green, Harmer, Cunliffe, Glegg, Matthews, Ward, Jones. and Dumbell. The match to-day is the last St. Helens is playing this season, the committee having decided to dis- band the team owing to the lack of home support. Last week. when Broughton Rangers were the at- traction, they only drew .£7. A ship cannot float in such low water.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON INTERMEDIATE LEAGUE. Result of Match played last Saturday. Heathside, 6 points; St. Thomas', nil. Match for To-day. Rylands's v. Beamont. At Rylands's, kick-off 3.30. Referee, Mr. Bibby.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <p>continued to attack, but so well did Royle, Marsh and Fearn defend their goal that twenty minutes elapsed before- Cooper scored the first goal for the visitors. From the centre kick the home forwards paid, a visit to the visitors' goal, but were easily re- pulsed. by Brandwood, and James, securing the ball. mced along the touch-line and scored the second goal. Following these successes Green scored a third and Heesom a fourth goal without any reply from the home team. Halt-time: Recs., 4 goals; Widnes, nil. The second half opened very keenly, and each end was visited without any result. Brandwood and Willcock were playing so well that Lockhart had only a few'long shots to deal with. After a spell of midfield play the visitors' forwards broke away, and Heesom scored their fifth goal. Following these reverses the home team were seen to better advan- tage and scored their first goal with a long shot, which hit the under side of the cross-bar and went through the g0a1... An accident to Trainor caused his retirement for the remainder of the game, and the visitors, having to continue with ten players, were forced to play on the defensive. The home team scored again before the call of time, the final result being: Latchford Recs., 5 goals; Widnes Juniors, 2 goals. Willcock, Brandwood, Green and Heesom were the best on the day's play for the visiting team.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
