      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>VOLUNTEER SHOOTING.</head>
        <p>VOLUNTEER SHOOTING.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Detachment Outdoor Competition.</p>
        <p>The Stockton Heath Detachment of the 6th (Knutsford) Battalion of the Cheshire Volunteer Regiment held their final outdoor competitions at the Appleton Range last Saturday. A' good attendance of members made the event a 'seccess. There were four competitions, and all were shot with military open sights, and no slings were used.</p>
        <p>Competition 1 ("The Rowson ").-Five shots and a sighter at a six inch bull target and five shots at a disappearing figure target at 100 yards; possible scores 25 and 5.-1, C. Lambert, bull 25, figure 2, handicap 41, total 311; 2, A. S. Wright, 19, 5,5, total 29; 3, A. Shepherd, 25, 4. ,scr., total 29; 4, G. Lowe, 22, 2,5, total 29; 5, E. C. White, 22, 0,6, total 28; 6, A. Worrall, 20, 4,3, total 27; 7, R. M. Swinton, 23, 3; 1, _total 26; 8. T. I. Fletcher, 22, 3, total 251. Competition 2 (" The Warburton ").-Ten shots and up to five shots as sighters. deliberate at a. two inch bull target with a one inch centre (10) at 25 yards, possible score 100.-1, W. A. Thompson, 94, handicap 7, 'total 100 (tie shoot 96); 2, J. H. Teare,.94, 6, total 100 (tie shoot 95); 3, E. F. Cliffe, 93, 9, total 100 (tie shoot 90); 4, G. Lowe, 89, 10, total 99; 5, C. W. Hallows. 91, 8, total 99; 6, B. W. Clough, 89, 9, total 98; 7, A. Shepherd, 98, scr.. total 98; 8, C. Worrall. 88, 9, total 97. Competition 3 (" The Sharpshooter ").-Shooting for one minute at black bulls on a green target at 25 yards, hits on the bull to count 1 point.-1, J. T. Shepherd, hits 7, handicap 3. total 10; 2, E. F. Cliffe, 7,3, total 10; 3, A. S. Wright, 5,4, total 9; 4, R. M. Swinton, 9, scr., total 9; 5, A. Shepherd, 9. scr., total 9; 6, A. Millar, 4,4, total 8; 7, C. W. Hallows, 3,3, total 6; 8, H. Bibby, 2,4, total 6. Competition 4 (" The Aggregate ").-The aggre- gate scores of Competitions 1,2, 3, with handicap additions; prizes given by Messrs. Smith and Walker.-E. F. Cliffe, Competition 1. 241; 2, 102; 3, 10; total 1361. Next best scores: A. Shepherd, 29, 98, 9, total 136; G. Lowe, 29, 99, 5, total 133; R. M. Swinton, 261, 96, 9, total 1311; A. -S. Wright. 29, 91, 9, total 129; J. H. Fletcher, 261, 96, 5, total 1271; E. C. White, 28, 94, 5, total 127; W. H. Thompson, 201. 101. 5, total 1261; C. Worrall, 241, 97, 5, total 1261; C. W. Hallows, 20, 99, 6, total 125. •</p>
        <p>The officials were Commander F. Warburton; range Officers, Sergt.-Musketry-instructor A. Shep- herd and Pte. R. M. Swinton; statistics officers, Sergt. J. F. Fergusson and Pte. J. H. Fletcher; range stewards, Sergt. C. J. Smith, Sergt. E. G. White, Corpl. A. S. Wright and Pte. L. R. Rowson.</p>
        <p>FLETCHER, RUSSELL'S v. APPLETON R.C. The first match of the seasdn took place at the range of the former. The conditions were: 10 rounds deliberate on 5 bull targets at 25 yards, 10 to shoot, 8 best .to count. The interest was keen, which, no doubt, accounted for some of the scores, but on the whole the shooting of both teams was very uniform. Appleton ultimately ran out winners by a margin of 2 points. Scores:—</p>
        <p>Fletcher, Russell's.—G. Legge 96. J. Buchanan 96, R. Turner 95, J. .F. Fergusson 94, F. Hare 94, J. Phillips 94. H. Whiteley 92, J. Towers (capt.) 92, 3. McDermid 89,'J. Colley 83-925. Deduct 2 lowest 172; total 753. Appleton R.C.—F. C. Bennion 97, F. J. Grocott 96, F. Black (capt.) 96, R. M. Swinton 95, C. Shep- ley 94, J. H. Fletcher 94. J. W. Elliott 92, N. Shep- herd 91, P. Harrison 91, M. A. Piercey 91-937. Deduct 2 lowest 182; total 755.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>Before and After.</head>
        <p>Before and After.</p>
        <p>Two ladies were exchanging observations on human nature within the ambit of their own experience. "How marriage changes a man," said one, in a reverie. "Doesn't it?" acquiesced her friend. "Take my lufsband—he used to offer me a penny for my thoughts. and now he offers sme a sovcreign to shut up."</p>
        <p>11161 Lowsomiewimimososiiiwrionommo. _ _</p>
        <p>1,77:77f</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>FOOTBALL FIELD.</head>
        <p>THE FOOTBALL FIELD.</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>Tr,-.:E TOWN CLUB DRAWS AGAINST WALKDEN.</p>
        <p>The game at Hood-lane last Saturday between the Town and Walkden Central was greatly in- terfered with by a strong winthwhich blew from goal to goal, and it was difficult for the players to control the ball, and each team had many well-intended shots blown away from the vicinity of the goal. At one period of the game it appeared as if the visitors' goal was charmed, as the cross-bar was hit three times in quick succession, but apart from this the home for- wards' final efforts lacked decision and sting, •and the opportunities they had ought to have resulted in nhore goals being scored.</p>
        <p>The visitors opened the second half very briskly, and were soon trying to find their range with some long shots. Marsden. and Berry were effective in their clearances, and after Kite had neatly saved a long, low shot from Flaherty, the latter managed to steer the ball safely out of Kite's lengthy reach, and a few minutes later Coid equalised with a splendid shot. The clos- ing stages saw the home team make a deter- mined effort to regain the lead, but the visitors' defenders Droved too good for them, and 'thus the Hood-laners dropped a point as compared with the corresponding match last season. '</p>
        <p>All the Juniors •were engaged last. week in search of points, and perhaps one of the keenest games was contested on the Highfield Tannery ground, where die- Juniors and the Cadets were ,in .opposition to each other. Previous to this game neither of these teams had gained a point, and both were very anxious to get away from the bottom of the League chart. The game was keenly contested and very- mu&amp; enjoyed by the spectatcirs. on the day's play a division of points was a creditable performance to both teams. Bell, Wishlade, Ford and Hankey did well for the Juniors, as also did Thornhill in goal. Mayers, for the Cadets, was their most prominent player, and scored both their goals.</p>
        <p>Grappenhall opened ,their fixtures at Thelwall- lane, and had Penketh Juniors as visitors. The home club could not have wished for a better team to test the abilities of their players. The Juniors were successful in piercing the home defence on three occasions in the first half. The second half was evenly contested for a while, but in the last twenty minutes the home team completely turned the tables on their opponents, and. ran out winners by four goals against three.</p>
        <p>Thornton, Kenwright, Willoughby and Ash- croft showed promising form, and the visitors were best represented by Thomassoh, Pearson and Glover.</p>
        <p>• Watson made his re-appearance in the visit- ors' ranks since his accident a few weeks ago on the Rec.</p>
        <p>Latchford Recs. journeyed to Widnes to open the Juniors' League fixtures, and accomplished a very smart performance by scoring five goals against two by the home team. Owing to the strong wind, which blew straight up the field of play, one set of goals were blown down and the cross-bar was broken, and not having another to replace the broken one, the referee had the unusual task of judging when goals were scored or when the ball was shot too high for a goal to be allowed.</p>
        <p>Appleton Hornets journeyed to town to open their fixtures against the Orfortl euniors. The first 'half was pointless, and the second half was well advanced before the home team succeeded in piercing their defence. A second goal was obtained a few minutes before the end of hos- tilities, the result being two goals against none in favour of the Juniors. The home team were best represented by Clare, Buckley, Gibson and Bothwell, and the defenders were most promi- nent for the visitors; it was owing to their fine play that their defeat was not more severe.</p>
        <p>Howley 'United, the leaders of the League, were opposed by the Bewsey Adults. The United proved, superior both in defence, and attack, and sciired. 'twice in. each half. • 714 Adults' only time when they were really dan- gerous was when they Were awarded a penalty kick. Hassan took, the kick, but Walters brought off a splendid save, for which he was loudly cheered.</p>
        <p>Miller and Hind were ever in the thick of the game, and Rowlinson and Foster were very re- sourceful at back. '</p>
        <p>Hassall and Roylance showed up well in the forwards, but were badly supported, and Stephens did well in goal.</p>
        <p>Widnes St. Mary's paid their first visit to the Steel-street ground, and were very lucky to come away with a point from the Saints. Mercer, for the visitors, proved a capable custodian, and saved many good shots, while on several occa- ions the cross-bar was hit by, shots from Eng- "land and Holden. Mercer's goalkeeping was one of the best features of the match.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN v. WALKDEN.</p>
        <p>The first of the season's fixtures between the above clubs was played last week on the Hood-lane ground in very boisterous weather and before 600 spectators, and the clubs were represented by the following players:—</p>
        <p>Warrington: Kite; Leah, Ifillward; Siddall, Whit- field, Ditchfield; Illidge, Stephens, Aspinall, Light- owler and Banner.</p>
        <p>Walkden: Cousins; Berry, Marsden; Cookson, Coid, Cordwell; Miller, Meehan, Flaherty, Brooks and Hayes.</p>
        <p>Referee, Mr. Robe'tts, Wigan</p>
        <p>The home captain won the toss, and Flaherty set the ball in motion against a very strong wind. The opening exchanges were in midfield, the players having great difficulty in controlling the ball. The home team gradually settled down to their game, and advanced towards the visitors' goal, when Berry checked Stephens when dangerously placed. Then Miller led the way on the home citadel, and Kite had to kick away, and Leah transferred to mid- field. Illidge, gaining possession, landed the ball on the top of the net. After a brief visit to the- home half a free kick was awaided the home team just outside the penalty area. Aspinall shot over the bar. Miller and Meehan were prominent on the visitor? right,. but Ditchfield in dose attend- ance and did not allow them to become dangerous, and the ball was transferred to Banner, for the latter to centre nicely in front of goal, where Stephens headed against the cross-bar, and in 'the next minute Banner hit the bar with a long shot, the visitors' goal narrowly escaping capture. At this stage the visitors' defence was being severely tested, and Cousins, Berry, Marsden and Cold played a grand game for the visitors. Aspinall tried to break through, but was checked by Berry, and then Whitfield hit the cross-bar with a good shot. The visitors' forwards now gained possession, and Flaherty and Meehan caused trouble to the home defence, and Kite was called upon to handle, and from his clearance good combination was shown by the home forwards, and play was quickly trans- ferred to the opposite end, Aspinall sending a dan- gerous centre, which Cousins failed to clear, and the ball went into the net. From the centre kick the visitors made a strong effort to equalise. Brooks, with a hard drive, gained a corner kick; this was eventually cleared by Ditchfield, and Aspinall ob- tained the ball, broke away, and beat the opposing backs and scored the second goal for the home team. After this reverse both ends were visited without result. During a* scrimmage near the visitors' goal Stephens missed by inches. Half-time score: War- rington, 2 goals; Viralkden, nil. The becond half opened very briskly, and the visitors were soon trying to find their range by long shots. A neat pass by Siddall to Banner en- abled the latter to get away on the extreme left; but when getting dangerous he was smartly robbed bv Marsden, and in .the next minute Flaherty tested Kite with a long ground shot, which was neatly fielded and cleared away. A long shot by Aspinall was well cleared by Berry, and then off-side against the visitors lost 'them a good opening. From the free kick a fine centre by Banner was not made the best use of, and the leather was quickly returned to the home end, where Brooks shot outside, and later Kite was called upon to save several good shots, but at last Flaherty beat him with a beauty. Encouraged by this success the visitors now played keenly for the equaliser, but met stout opposition from Millward and Leah, who succeeded in check- ing their advance, and Aspinall sent 'the ball out to Banner, but the latter was smartly checked by Berry. For a few minutes play was confined to a series of throws-in, but these ended in favour of the visitors, who gradually gained ground, and Meehan nut in a grand centre, which was not accepted by his colleagues. Off-side against the home forwards put them on the defensive again, and following some keen exchanges in the home Ralf Coid suc- ceeded in making the scores equal with gt, grand shot that completely beat Kite. The home team now strove hard to regain the lead, but -the visitors'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <p>defence held out, and the ball was returned to mid- field, when time was called, the result being: War- rington, 2 goals; Walkden, 2 goals. The visitors were best served by Berry, .Marsden, Coid, Flaherty, Miller and Meehan. The home team were best represented by Kite, Millward, Ditchfield and Whitfield.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION. Results to Date.</p>
        <p>GOALS.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. F. A. P. Tranmere Rover Hurst South Liverpool Warrington Town Stalybridge Celtic Barrow Walkden Central Altrincharn Eccles Borough Atherton Hyde</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>LAST SATURDAY'S RESULTS</head>
        <p>LAST SATURDAY'S RESULTS</p>
        <p>Hurst , 4 Atherton Tranmere Rovers ... , 5 Barrow Eccles Borough Walkden Central .... 2 Warrington Town South Liverpool</p>
        <p>ItARRINGTON AND DISTRICT</p>
        <p>JUNIOR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>GOALS. P. W. L. D. F. A. P. Howley St. Elphin's Orford Juniors Latchford Recs. Penketh Juniors Bewsey A.S Grappenhall Widnes St. Mary's Highfield J. Latchford Cadets Widnes Juniors Appleton Hornets</p>
        <p>HIGHFIELD JUNIORS v. LATCHFORD CADETS</p>
        <p>On the ground of the former.</p>
        <p>H ighfield : Thornhill; Worrall, Bell; Wishlade, Hankey, Barnes; Highfield, Moulsdale, Peattie; Ford and Tonalinson.</p>
        <p>Latchford Cadets: Miller; Sankey, Williams; Jones, Plumb, Porter; Bradshaw, Gibson, Mayers, Davenport and Massey. Referee, Mr. S. C. Essenhigh. The game opened very keenly. Both goals were attacked, and Thornhill had difficulty in saving a shot from Mayers. Alter half an hour's play the Cadets succeeded in opening their score with a shot from Mayers. The home team now made an effort to equalise, but were easily driven back. Another advance by the Cadets was checked by Bell, and Sankey also cleared from Peattie. The Cadets re- turned to the attack, and Mayers, fastening on a neat centre, increased the Cadets' lead. Half-time: Cadets, 2 goals; Highfield, nil</p>
        <p>_ • The home team opened very strongllf, and Ford scored their first goal within five minutes. The Cadets retaliated very strongly and Thornhill and Bell defended well. Some keen play followed in the Cadets half, and Ford, Wishlade and Tomlinson each tried to gain the equalising point but failed to control the ball properly. Aq time was drawing near the home team made a determined effort to get on equal terms, and in spite of the fine defence of the Cadets, Peattie was successful in placing the ball between the uprights. Result: Highfield Juniors, 2 goals; Latchford Cadets, 2 goals.</p>
        <p>HOWLEY UNITED V. BEWSEY A.S.</p>
        <p>In Victoria Park.</p>
        <p>Howley: Walters; Rowlinson, Foster; Hoarty, Miller, Hind; Hibbert, Fitzgerald, Davies, Wood- house and Humphries.</p>
        <p>Bewsey: Stephens; Robinson, Main; Perks, Rent, Atkinson; Hassell, Worrall, Roylance, Bradburn and Schofield.</p>
        <p>Referee, Mr. H. Berry- • Both teams were at full strength, Hibbert appear- ing on the extreme right in place of Boyle for the United. After fifteen minutes' play Davies opened the score with a shot that completely beat Stephens. Occasional attacks by the Adults were easily re- pulsed by Rowlinson and Foster, and from another attack by the United,-Woodhouse scored the second goal. The Adults gained a penalty kick. Hassall was entrusted with the kick and Walters brought off a splendid save. The second half saw the Adults to better advantage, but they were unable to get past the defence of Miller, Rowlinson and Foster, the former paying special attention to Hamall. Woodhouse, receiving the ball in midfield, made a grand individual effort, and Stephens was unable to clear his final shot. DOies was well up and notched the •third -goal. The same player 'added a fourth a few minutes later. Result: Howley United, 4 goals; Bewsey A.S., nil.</p>
        <p>ST. ELPHIN'S v. WIDNES ST. MARY'S.</p>
        <p>On the _Steel-street ground.</p>
        <p>St. Elphin's: Povey; Tunstall, Furness; Cooper, Miller, Gleave; Watson, England, Lee, A. Jones and Holden.</p>
        <p>Widnes St. Mary's: J. Mercer; Evans, Gahon; Crewe, Cooper, Thompson; Bickers, Bennett, McAlister, Mellor and Gregory. Referee, Mr. J. Wright.</p>
        <p>The game opened very fast, and Lee sending out some judicious passes from the centre, the home team were quickly attacking, and were unfortunate in failing to score, Holden hitting the cross-bar three times in the first five minutes. England hit the bar wtih a fast shot, and immediately after Holden scored for the Saints. From the centre kick the Saints continued to ' attack, and England was again unfortunate with a well-directed shot. Mercer was keeping a good goal for the visitors, and from a good clearance the visitors began to improve, Gregory being very prominent. From a free kick Povey neatly tipped the ball over the bar. Several other attacks by the visitors follolved, and PoveY saved well from McAlister. Half-time: St. Elphin's, 1 goal; Widnes, nil.</p>
        <p>There were no signs of slackening the speed in the second half, and Mercer proved safe and cleared good shots from Jones and Lee. The visitors now had more of the game, and Gregory and Tickers (the extreme wingers) were the most dangerous. Play now deteriorated in quality, and at times was very scrappy. A penalty kick to the visitors was entrusted to Gregory, and Povey stopped the shot but failed to get the\ ball away, and a scrimmage ensued in the goalmouth, and the referee awarded the visitors a goal, he judging the ball to have been over the line. Holden sent in a fine shot, but Mercer was equal to the occasion. Result: St. Elphin's, 1 goal; Widnes St. Mary's, 1 goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL v. PENKETH JUNIORS.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL v. PENKETH JUNIORS.</p>
        <p>Grappenhall: Hadkiss; Knight, Rigby; Tomlin- son, Jones, Thornton; W. Smith, Willoughby, Aikenhead, Ashcroft and Kenwright. Penketh Juniors: Finch; Moss, Thomasson ; Swift, Sankey, Winstanley ; Hughes, Ainsworth; Glover, Pearson and Watson. Referee, Mr. C. Gosling. The visitors made ,a strong attack on the home goal, and mis-kicking by Knight enabled Glover to open their score. The visitors had the best of the game and forced the home,ciefence to concede several corner kicks, which resulted in Glover adding a second goal. Even play followed for a while, then owing to weakness by the home half-backs Pearson increased the visitors' lead. Half-time: Penketh Juniors, 3 goals; Grappenhall, In the second half after even play the home team took up the aggressive and severely tested the visitors' defence. Good play between Thornton, Kenwright and Ashcroft, resulted in Kenwright f;coring the home team's first goal. From the centre kick each goal was visited, and then the home for- wards showing good combination, Kenwright scored the second goal. Continuing to have the best of the exchanges, the home team forced a corner kick, aud this was well placed by Kenwright. Finch failed to clear and the ball passed through the goal, making the scores equal. The game was now keenly contested, but the home team proved too good for the visitors ,and Ashcroft secured the lead. Result: Grappenhall, 4 goals; Penketh Juniors, 3 goals.</p>
        <p>ORFORD JUNIORS v. APPLETON HORNETS.</p>
        <p>On the Lovely-lane ground</p>
        <p>Orford Juniors: Conley; Gibson, Doughty; Wit- ton, Austin, Bothwell; Hurst, Clare, Belshaw, Buckley and Griffiths.</p>
        <p>Appleton Hornets: Millar; Howard, Fozard; John- son, Broval., Robrts; Woolley, Green, Young, Bar- clay 4nd Tegg• _ _ _</p>
        <p>Referee, 1111. P. Gosling. The home forwards made the first advance, and when getting into a dangerous position Buckley was neatly robbed by Johnson, and the ball forced into touch. The visitors made an attack, but their final shots lacked sting and were ea qilv got away. There was a clear score sheet at: hof-time. On resuming Johnson was prominent in good passing movements and supplied his forwards with many openings, which they failed to convert by bad shooting. There was even play until the closing stages of the game, when the home team pulled themselves together and made a strong effort to score, but were repulsed. Buckley, by a splendid individual effort, succeeded in scoring the first goal. , This success acted splen- didly on the home team, and their inside forwards put plenty of dash into, their play, and Griffiths added the second goal. Score: Orford Juniors, 2 goals; Appleton Hornets, 'nil.</p>
        <p>WIDNES JUNIORS V. LATCHFORD RECS.</p>
        <p>On the ground of the former.</p>
        <p>Widnes- Juniors: Boyle; Marsh, Fearn; Cox, Brown, McConnell; Martin, Mooney... Magee, Welsh and Gallagher. Latchford Recs.: Lockhart; Willcock, Brandwood; Bancroft, Cooper, Trainor; Heesom, Fairbrother, Green, Dutton and James. Referee, Mr. G. Marsh, The visitors made the first attack, and Boyle was early called upon to save from Heesom. The Recs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>IN THE COURTS.</head>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>LIGHTING OFFENDERS.</p>
        <p>At Tuesday's sitting of the Boraugir Magis- trates, the following were fined ss. for offences under the Lighting Order: Ernest Smith, 24, Ash Grove; James Mason, 3, Winwick-road ; William Gates, 11, Scotland-road; Frederick Woodcock, 39, Wainwright's-yard; and Peter Knight, 76, Buttermarket-street, was fined 7s. 6d.</p>
        <p>On Thursday the Park Picture Palace pro- prietor was fined V, and Messrs. W. Hodgkin- son, Ltd., 10s. STRUCK HORSE WITH SPADE.</p>
        <p>"We s'aw him,, strike the horse on the head four or five times with a spade," declared Inspector Bonham, of the R.S.P.C.A., and P.C. Bartlett, at the Borough Police Court 'on Tues- day, when proving a charge of cruelty to a horse by George Blanton, 74, Hume-street. The incident took place on the 15th instant on Rowley tip. When the officers asked the de- fendant why he acted like that he replied that he lost his temper. Fined 20s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>DOG ATTACKS POLICE.</head>
        <p>DOG ATTACKS POLICE.</p>
        <p>When a charge of allowing a ferocioia dog to be at large was preferred against James Baskitter, 141, Liverpool-road, at the Borough Police Court on Wednesday, defendant stated that the dog's mouth was so ,shaped that it could not bite. This statement, however, Detective Anderson contradicted, for, he said, when on duty in Wellfield-street on Monda3, the 9th inst., a cross-bred Irish terrier ran at him and bit his leg. He turned round and the dog again attacked him, and he was forced to draw his staff to defend himself. When wit- ness drew defendant's wite's attention to the matter she said she wasn't surprised, as they had had complaints about the dog before. She had asked her husband to destroy it, but he had refused, as he thought the reports about the dog were exaggerated.—Fine 20s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>BOY STRIKES TRAM CONDUCTRESS.</head>
        <p>BOY STRIKES TRAM CONDUCTRESS.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Wednesday, on a charge of disorderly conduct in Knuts- ford-road by throwing a stick at a tram con- ductress, a boy of twelve was bound over and ordered to pay the costs (55.), in addition to two witnesses' fees of 2s. 6d. each-10s. in all. It was stated that numerous complaints of this character had been received. The defendant ran behind the car and the walking stick he was carrying became en- tangled in the rail on the car, and the conductress took it off him. On the return journey the boy asked the conductress for the stick, and when she refused to give it him, he threw another stick at her, this striking her on the nose and cutting it. She had on a pre- vious occasion received a black eye through a similar action.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable thought the tramcar drivers and conductors must be protected, Rspecially at the present time, when they were so shorthanded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>"PITCH AND TOSS" AT SANKEY BRIDGES.</head>
        <p>"PITCH AND TOSS" AT SANKEY BRIDGES.</p>
        <p>Seven young men, all hailing from the Bank Quay district, named William Spencer, Edward Spencer, Thomas Clare, Arthur Sankey, Robert Williams, John Hatton, and Ben Huxley, were charged at the Warrington County Petty Sessions on Wednesday with gaming with coins at Sankey Bridges on the Bth instant. P.C. Phillips said he watched the defendants playing pitch and toss for about half an hour, during which time money frequently changed hands.</p>
        <p>Inspector Forster said he hoped the Bench would make an example of these young men, who should know better. Numerous complaints had been received respecting Sunday afternoon gambling. People passing along the canal bank from Penk.eth to Warrington and to places of worship were much annoyed. The police had to go out in all sorts of disguises to catch the offenders. Fines,: 10s. each.</p>
      </div>
