      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 21, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 21, 1916.</p>
        <p>Property for Sale. vOR SALE, Block 6 Freehold Cot- tages, all let good tenants, producing £BO per year; email outgoings.—Particulars of G.- Dimes, Clarence-road. Fleet. •Hants.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>DISTRICT NEWS.</head>
        <p>DISTRICT NEWS.</p>
        <p>Continued from Page 6.</p>
        <p>PADG_ATE.</p>
        <p>Death of Miss Hindley.—We regret to an- nounce the death of Miss 11. 4indley, daugh- ter of Mrs. T. Hindley, of rearnhead-lane, Fearnhead, which occurred at her residence on Tuesday morning. Miss' Hindley had for many yeare been a Day *School teacher at the Risley Presbyterian School. She was well- known and highly respected in' the district, and her loss will be greatly felt by a large circle of .friends.</p>
        <p>Padgate Cadets.—Owing to the kindness of Miss Turner, of the Vicarage, Padgate, a hot- pot supper was given to the members of the C.L.B. Cadets on Saturday evening, in the Church House. After supper a concert was held, items' being given by several members of the company, including Corporal Gilbert and Private Wheatcroft. A hearty welcome was given to the late captain of the company, Mr. R. Dickinson, who was over for 9. few days' holiday. He spoke of the good work done by some of the members and asked all to work together in concord for the benefit of the corps, and in so doing they would secure for themselves a long and useful career. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>PRIESTHOOD OF WOMEN.</head>
        <p>PRIESTHOOD OF WOMEN.</p>
        <p>Rev. A. M. Mitchell's Suggestion 1.1 Wesley Guild.</p>
        <p>There was a good gathering of members at the Wesley Guild meeting on Thursday, when the Rev. A. M. Mitchell, vicar of Burtonwood, gave a very interesting address, the subject of which was "Women in our Churches." At the outset he stated that woman, from the beginning, had always been in -hot water. It had been said that women were at the bottom of all mischief, but, from his.limited 'experi- ence, if this were true he felt sure they .must be at the bottom of all good. Speaking of the olden times, and the time of the Teutons, he mentioned that women had always been con- sidered inferior to men, bht the emancipation of women began .with Christianity. Speaking of the work of women in the Churches, he mentioned that Nonconformists had more free- dom than Church of England ladies. As an illustration he spoke of the terrific storm which had been raised at the suggestion made by. the Bishop of London that women might speak in the churches- whilst the National, Mission was in progress. Why should unedu- cated men and . pale-faced curates have the privilege, of speaking whilst educated women of deep piety should be denied this privilege? He read a list of what women •might do out- side the Church, but they must hold their tongue inside, except for the singing of the hymn and a whispered "Amen." Sex equality in the Church should have the support of every minister of religion. Men were jealous, but all jealousy and prejudice must be put aside, and, therefore, they should accord to women an equal opportunity. Why should there not be a. priesthood of women? The ordination of women had not been unknown .in the history of the Church. In Psalm cxliv. we• read "That our daughters may be as corner-stones polished after the similitude of a palace." Votes of thanks were proposed by Mr. W. E. Plinston and Mr. H. Monks and heartily supported by Mr. A. Ward (Davenham) and Mr. E. England. The Rev. A. J. Morton. presided, and Mrs. Cottrell was the accompanist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>Our Village Warriors.—Leading Stoker Wm. Higham and Seaman Thomas Hayes are both home for a well-earned spell after doing. their share in fighting and patrolling around our island home.</p>
        <p>Private John Alfred'Pusill is at present in hospital at Malta, suffering from dysentery. Private Arthur Anstock, late chauffeur to Mr. C. E. Parker, is doing his bit in helping to manipulate a tank against the enemy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>HEADMASTER FINED.</head>
        <p>HEADMASTER FINED.</p>
        <p>Regulation Broken At Friends' Boarding Scjiool.</p>
        <p>Mr. William Edward Brown, principal of the Friends' Boarding School, Penketh, was fined ss. at the Warrington County Petty Sessions on Wednesday for failing to have posted on ot about his premises a list of the names of male employes between the ages of eighteen and forty- one, as required by the Defence of the Realm Regulations. Defendant pleaded not guilty.</p>
        <p>Inspector Forster said tliat Detective-In- spector Davidson visited defendant's premises In company with Sergeant Jowett, on the 28th September. He saw the defendant and asked him if he had a list of the names of men of military age posted on his premises, to which question defendant replied, "No." Ile admitted that he was responsible for the school and the employes, but said he had not heard the particulars were required before. He had tried to excuse himself by saying that the Chairman of the Directors was the employer and not he. He had three school masters on the premises between the ages of eighteen and forty-one, viz. Messrs. Baker (25), Bailey (24), and Sheldon (34). In his defence, defendant said he told In- spector Davidson he was responsible for what went on outside the house, but he was not the employer. He had no idea that the lists were required to be put up, and immediately he knew he posted one up.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>PARISH COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>PARISH COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The October Statutory Meeting of the Parish Council was held on Friday of last week in the Council School. The members present were: Messrs. J. G. Withinshaw _(chairman), R. Routledge (vice-chairman), J. Woodward, W. Woods, • Miss Tinsley, and Mr. E. Foster (clerk). Dr. Murray's retirement from the Earles- town Education Committee was ,announced, and Mr. J. B. Aitken accepted the position. In answer to a letter complaining about the state of the main road through the village, the County Surveyor wrote :—I am sorry that at the moment, owing to previous appoint- ments, I cannot suggest a date and ,time for a meeting of the deputation mentioned, but will do so at the earliest moment." Lighting.</p>
        <p>The Chairman complained that the gas pressure was very poor, and suggested that a letter be sent to the Warrington Gas Com- mittee, to the effect that the whole village was greatly inconvenienced in consequenee.—ln seconding, Councillor R. Routledge said that the committee ought to be asked to notify Consumers of gas for business purposes as to what precaution should be taken to nullify the effect Of shortage of pressure and to afford facilities.—The proposition was carried. The following letter was read from the Wid- nes Police Superintendent:—" I respectfully recommend that the lamps be not lighted, at least for the present, darkness being the great- est defence against Zeppelin raids. If you consider, it advisable later on for a lamp or two at important crossings to be lighted, I shall be glad to meet you."—This was agreed to, with a recommendation from Councillor J. Woodward that the Corporation be asked to paint the lamp standards white or partlY white at a reasonable height, from the ground, as they were very dangerous on a dark night. The London and North-Western Railway Company were also to be asked to deal with their lamps in Ferry-lane in a similar man- ner.</p>
        <p>A circular letter from the Local Government Board stated that the Council elections would be postponed for another year.</p>
        <p>Reduced Rates.</p>
        <p>The Chairman reported that he had attended a meeting of the Overseers, and he was very pleased to be able to state that there would be a reduction in the rates of 2d. in the C for the next half-year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>SIDE-SLIP FOR SPEEDY SPEAKERS.</head>
        <p>SIDE-SLIP FOR SPEEDY SPEAKERS.</p>
        <p>A simple suggestion! Successfully say: She should surely see shows smart with smiles but no shockings, So secure some seats soon for "A Pair of Silk Stockings." Said safely? Send swiftly !—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>BURTONWOOD.</head>
        <p>BURTONWOOD.</p>
        <p>Happy Burtonwood.—The Burtonwood poor rate of 2s. 6d. was signed by the magistrates sitting at the Warrington County Petty Sessions on Wednesday. The rate is 4d. less than last year, and 2d. less than that in April.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>BRAVE BROTHERS.</head>
        <p>BRAVE BROTHERS.</p>
        <p>Certificates For Gallantry In Rescue</p>
        <p>From Pond:</p>
        <p>A pleasant ceremony took place at the War- rington County Petty Sessions on Wednesday, when Mr. S. R. Armitage (chairman of the Bench) presented the Royal Humane Society% certificate and vellum to two brothers, _Thos. and Arthur Fairclough, of 43, Mersey-street, Burtonwood, for gallant actions at Burtonwood on August 2nd. In asking Mr. Armitage to present the cer- tificates, Inspector Forster said that on the date in question some small boys were fishing with nets in a pond in Back-lane, Burtonwood. little lad named Bertie Taylor borrowed a net from one of the other boys and climbed along a tree with it. While reaching with the net into the water he overbalanced and fell into the pit, which is a very deep one. Thomas Fairclough came on the scene and without a moment's hesi. tation dived into the water. He found the boy Taylor at the bottom of the pit, and took hold of him. The drowning boy, however. seized Fairclough in such a manner that he could not strike out to land, and they both went to the bottom of the pit, clenched together. Fair- dough eventually got away from Taylor, and reached the surface. Arthur Fairclough then came on the scene and saw his brother just about drowning. He dived into the water and brought his brother to the side, then repeatedly dived and tried to find the boy Taylor. He found the boy's fishing net, but not the boy, who was later discovered by the police. The Coroner, Mr. Brighouse, had placed the matter before the Society. In handing the certificate to Thomas Fair- dough, Mr. Armitage said he had most certainly performed a very brave deed, and his people should be very proud of him. Addressing Arthur, Mr. Armitage said: "You showed wonderful pluck and determination. We are all very grateful to you. It is true English character. You ought to be very proud of this certificate all your life."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>TRAP-DOOR ACCIDENT.</head>
        <p>TRAP-DOOR ACCIDENT.</p>
        <p>Sad Death Of A Corporation</p>
        <p>Workman.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday afternoon, William Henry Connor, aged 53 years, wire-jointei's mate, died in the Infirmary from injuries caused by fall- ing through a trap-door in premises in King- street.. The man had resided at 17, Dialstreet, and was employed by the Electricity Depart- ment of the Corporation. Mr. Samuel Brighouse, coroner, held an in- quest yesterday, when the widow and family were represented by Mr. T. S. Steel. Harry Bosco°, 18, Marbury2street, wire- jointer, employed by the Corporation, stated that on Tuesday afternoon Connor was assist- ing him on a job in .King-street, where an electrical installation was being put in at pre- mises occupied by Mr. Hulme, cabinet-maker and joiner. They were working fixing electrical. wire in the ground floor, where there was a trap-door leading. to a cellar. The trap-door was opened to give light to some men who were working in the cellar. Witness was cutting the wire with his pliers, and Connor, who was standing behind him and about 15 or 16 inches from the edge of the bole, was coiling the wire round his hand. Suddenly, witness heard a voice exclaim " Oh!" and one of the men called out: "Bill is down there." Witness ran down and carried him up. He asked him what had happened, but he did not answer. The distance to the floor of the cellar was over nine feet and the cellar was concreted. The Jury returned a verdict of "Accidental death," and added that a fence should be put around the trap-door.</p>
        <p>Don't be content too easily. Many a man is labelled " contented " who should he labelled " lost ambition."</p>
        <p>SIDE-SLIP FOR SPEEDY SPEAKERS.</p>
        <p>A simple suggestion! Successfully say: She should surely see shows smart with, smiles hut no shockings. So secure some seats soon for "A Pair of Silk Stockings." • Said safely? Send swiftly !—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>"OUR DAY,"</head>
        <p>"OUR DAY,"</p>
        <p>Nearly £5O Raised At Cafe</p>
        <p>Chantant.</p>
        <p>Under a panoply of flags of the Allied nations, a cafe chantaht held in the Parr Rail on Thurs- day.resulted in nearly 04.250 being raised for the " Our Day" fund. With those two vigorous and beneficent organisations—the B.R.C.S. and the Order of St. John of Jerusalem—in charge, and giving of their best, tice affair coUld not fail to ,be a success. Wellnigh two hundred helpers were concerned, most of them being gentlewomen act- ing as waitresses to the thousands of people who seated themselves at the fancy tables to enjoy food and music simultaneously. There were also heavily-laden and tastefully-arranged stalls —that for fancy articles being in charge of Mrs. Henry Greenall; the provisions, Mrs.' E. Osborne; and fruit and lowers, Mrs. Brereton FairclOugh. Raffles, organised by Mr. Harold Clarke, and competitions, requiring varying ability, from the ptVling of a golliworfs skirts for fortunes, to the naming Of celebr ties, were due to the initiative of Mr. F. S. Haucock. In- teresting Niles of the Lusitania and a wrecked Zeppelin were also sold. During the afternoon and evening a splendid musical programme was submitted, having been arranged by Mr. W. H. Payton. The Band of the 2nd South Lancashire Regiment, under Mr. R. E. Cahill, was extremely popular, and their contributions were interspersed by songs by Miss May Hockenhall, Miss Eva Cook, Madame Jennie Armstrong, Miss Elsie Hewitt, Madame Sephton ; Messrs. T. Crowe, J. H. Sephton, W. IL Dutton, A. W. Pearce, J. Babbitt; Pte. Grime; and an elocutionary effort by Mr. H. M. Ashton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>OPENING CEREMONY.</head>
        <p>THE OPENING CEREMONY.</p>
        <p>The Countess of Stamford attended for the opening ceremony, which was presided over by the Mayor. With him on the platform were the Mayoress, Mrs. Henry Greenall, Mrs. E. A. Fox, Miss Sylvia Broadbent, and Mr. W. P. Rylands. The Mayor said they were very fortunate that day :in having the Countess of Stamford with them. Her presence reminded him very much of the past; for about two hundred years ago the Earl of Warrington had his town house in Bridge-street, on a site somewhere near Sutton's shop. The Mayor also expressed indebtedness to the following for their help in connection with the cafe chantant, in particular to Mr. Ralph Johnson for the decorations and the furniture of the lounge; to Messrs. Peter Walker and Son for the decorations and stalls in the hall; to Messrs. Hodgkinson's, to Mr. Breward, St. Paul's Church, St. John's Church, Mrs. Hall (Sankey-street),. the band and the artistes, and Mr. W. H. Payton for arranging the musical programme. To all who in any way helped they were greatly obliged. (Applause.) The Countess said they must all 'feel inspired that day, because it was Our Day," and was being celebrated throughout the whole Empire, wherever the British flag was flying. It was very pleasing to come there that ,day to help Warrington in any, small way. At Dunham Massey, where they resided, she always recog- nised the connection between themselves and Warrington, recollecting that it was the last Lord Warrington who was the last peer to be Chancellor of the Exchequer. In being there that afternoon she felt that with her ancestors she was aoing a little bit for the people of War- rington. (Applause.) For the cafe chantant Miss Jolley was the hon. secretary and Mr. E. Roberts hon. treasurer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>"OUR DAY CONTRIBUTORS.</head>
        <p>"OUR DAY CONTRIBUTORS.</p>
        <p>The list of donors to the "Our Day" appeal is not yet completed, but will be published as soon as possible.</p>
        <p>Death of Mr. William Blackshaw. We regret to state that Mr. William Black- shaw, of Golborne-street, passed away at the Infirmary on Friday of last week, soon after he had been admitted for the purpose of being operated upon. He was taken ill the previous day, suffering from severe pains in the stomach, and died before an operation could be per- formed. The funeral took place at the Ceme- tery on Wednesday afternoon. Mr. Blatkshaw, who was a widower, had spent 26 of his 43 years as an employe of Messrs. Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd., and was highly respected as a workman and friend.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>Funeral of Mr. R. B. Woods.</head>
        <p>Funeral of Mr. R. B. Woods.</p>
        <p>The funeral of Mr. R. B. Woods, whose death was announced in our last issue, took place on Saturday at the Hill Cliffe Burial Ground. The interment was preceded by a service held in the Independent Methodist Church at Stockton Heath, and the large and reverent assembly testified to the deep affec- tion in which Mr. Woods was held by his own Church and colleagts. Mr. Richard Lee, of Wigan, presided, and addresses were given by Mr. E. Ralphs, of Westhoughton, and Mr. John W. Hancock, of Stretford. , Councillor R. Henshall and Mr. John Crumblehulme, of Southport, also took part in a very impressive service. At the graveside the committal ser- vice was read by Mr. E. Embleton, of Sunder- land, the president of the Independent Metho- dist Connexion, and a brief address was given by Alderman Hunter, of .Leigh, who voiced the affection which was universally felt towards the one who had passed from their midst. The Connesional Secretary, Treasurer, Editor, and several departmental secretaries were present, and representatives Eitte.nded from the churches at Manchester, Liverpool, Southport, Bolton, Wigan, Crewe, Urmston, Nelson, Colne; Blackpool, Leigh, Lowton, and Sunder- land, as well as many members of local churches.</p>
        <p>Among the mourners present were: Mr. R. W. Woods and Mr. Ernest Woods (sons), Miss Florence E. Woods (daughter), Mr. J. W. Woods, Birmingham (brother), Mrs. gounfield, Mrs. Millington, Mrs. Clegg (sisters), Mr. R. Brimelow, Mr. A. Mounfield (nephews), Mrs. J. W. Woods, Mrs. Griffiths, Mr. W. Clegg, Mr. and Mrs. J. W. Brayshaw, Mr. and Mrs. W. H. Grounds, Mrs. ,R. W. Woods, Mrs. E. Woods, Miss Clegg, Councillor and Mrs. R. Henshall.</p>
        <p>Messrs. J. Mounfield and Sons carried out the funeral arrangements.</p>
        <p>STAGE AND KINEMA.</p>
        <p>Royal Court Theatre.</p>
        <p>When being introduced to Mr. C. W. Somer- set, whose company is playing "The Silver King" at the Royal Court Theatre to crowded houses this week, I happened to have my jacket buttoned, and he remarked: "It is all right, old fellow, you can unbutton it. I shall not pinch your watch and chain. I am only 'The Spider,' the gentleman cracksman, on the stage."</p>
        <p>So I unbuttoned, and, would you believe it? before long he had my coat off. It happened in this way: Mr. Somerset is an old friend of the' late George Augustus Sala, the Lion of "The Daily Telegraph," and after showing me Mr. Sala's gold fob—a beautiful affair—he men- tioned that the silk dinner jacket he was wear- ing glso belonged to Sala, that gentleman being one of the most famous, journalists of the Victorian era, I, as one of his cubs, must needs, for sentimental and hero worship rea- sons, see if it fit me. It did, but "The Spider" wanted it back for the next act. •</p>
        <p>Mr. Somerset is an actor with a distinguished present, and past, Bohemian and literary in his temperament. A long career on the stage has been fraught with conspicuous success. All his time has not been devoted to its practice and study, for, as becomes his present charac- ter, he has written many short stories, and a very interesting book on Criminology, the fruit of witnessing many of the great trials at the Old Bailey.</p>
        <p>In the theatrical world his greatest success in London was his Dr. Marshall in "The Advocate." For that Clement Scott gave him a column of almost sycophantic adulation. An- other success was his Dr. Rylott in "The Speckled -Band." "The Daily Telegraph" said of his performance in "The Rivals" that his was the Pnest Sir Antony Absolute they had ever seen. He was with Lewis Waller at the time.</p>
        <p>Mr. Somerset is a brilliant conversationalist, without -much of the ego, and one had difficulty in getting him to, talk about himself and his company. "The play's the thing," I kept on saying, but still he went on with reminiscences of Sala and great actors. At last he came to it. "The obstacles in obtaining a company to play 'The Silver King' were enormous. Ever so many men are required," he said, "but they are all* right now. Several of my company are at the Front. I, too, have fought on French soil, having been with our Ally's forces in the Franco-German War of 1870. . I was then going in for the Army as an Artilleryman, but joined the Foreign Legion and was all through the Defence of Paris. In securing Walter Gay as 'The Silver King' I have been most fortunate (Mr. Gay acted as Jarvis in "Grumpy" .11 the Royal Court last year). I. don't know of any better Silver King.' You see, I have played the part myself, and 1 never realised how bad I was until I saw how good he is. When the people saw him play The Silver King' they remarked: `Oh, the darling; I hope they won't catch him ! ' but when I played it they said 'Haven't they caught him ye?' " Mr. Somerset had good words to say of all his company and scenery; also of the audiences which have assembled. Everything in I-lie gar- den was lovely!</p>
        <p>The attraction at the Royal Court Theatre next week will be, "A Pair of Silk Stockings." This delightful coMedy will be presented by Mr. Charles Steuart, who is bringing with „him a most excellent company. "A Pair of Silk Stock- ings" is one of the most original and .attrac- live plays. The whole thing is so smart; in fact, brilliant. It is rarely that one has the opportunity to see a play like this—full of humour and genuine spontaneous mirth, and yet having an intensely interesting story, which is never forgotten. It is a production beauti- fully staged and delightfully acted. Next month the Harrison-Trewin Opera Com- pany is due with some of the best operas. Mr. Raynor is coming again in December.</p>
        <p>AI</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>Palace and Hippodrome.</head>
        <p>Palace and Hippodrome.</p>
        <p>Pauline Tra-Vis, as a male impersonator, bids fair to rival Vesta Tilley. She looks capital in male attire, her clothes are cut in the fashion, and her songs are sprightly and catching. Edwin Meier tells some humorous stories, and Fyne and Hurley, in a novel burlesque entitled "Oliver Twist Twisted," worked in some local ' allusions. There are other remarkable "pairs" on the programme. Billy Howard and Kathryn Harris appear in an astonishing rough and tumble act. and Warwick and Ciss, in their burlesque, "The Artiste and the Stage Hand," provide a vast amount of genuine fun. Mr. and Miss de Brean, with their shadow show and comedy silhouettes, present an unusual line of entertainment.</p>
        <p>Next week we are promised an attractive revue, "Horn, Soit," but, as usual, the title has little to do with the performance except that the phrase suggests -something about the Order of the darter.. The piece has established a repu- tation for fun and frolic . which it would be hard to beat. The chief' comedian, Mr. pip Powell, is the centre of this concoction, and is amazingly funny in. his numerous disguises There is amusing burlesque of old-time melo- drama; and other opportunities for clever work are afforded tO this most versatile 'actor. 'Mr. Paul Clive and'Miss Lilian Christine, in leading parts, are inimitable, and have the advantage of long acquaintance with the frivolity stage. Miss. Evelyn Layer another member of the com- pany, is a charming and delightful actress, and special dancing by the Eight Sunrays and Miss Dora Delan.e adds much to the attractiveness of a gay and enlivening production. " Honi Soit " should not be missed.</p>
        <p>The Kinemas. At the Star Kinema House, Church-street, that fine film, "The Butterfly on the Wheel," adopted from the play, is now being screened. The chief pictures for next week are "The Second Mrs. Tanqueray " during the first half and "Out of the Drifts" during the latter.</p>
        <p>"Soul Market" is the title of a fine drama now being shown at the Grand, Bridge Foot, and next week we are to have that well-known pie-</p>
        <p>L.</p>
        <p>N V</p>
