        <p>troublous period through which we are pass- ing, and, although he hinted that he found some time in which to attend to his private business affairs, it is a -fact that he is prac- tically a whole-time Mayor, and that his public 'duties call upon him to make the fullest sacri- fices. The most glowing tribute of the even- ing was paid by Councillor, HEINSHALL, who emphasised the good the MAY'OR does privately, and, in this respect, quoted Lowell's I portrayal Of Christ: , He that giiires himself with his gifts feeds three— Himself, his hungry neighbour, and Me.</p>
        <p>TRIO ON LEAVE. TIE Warrington And Them a'ack To The Trenches.. LUCKY FELLOWS'. • -Until last week it was many months sine any soldier detrained at Warringtbru jiisti on illigiday leave from the trenches, and Company- (i)tiarter-MaSter-Sergeant Edward. John Vilidbeks, of Knutsfor.d-road; Private Appleton, of Nicholson-street; and Private, Devine, of Sharpe-street, all admit that when the.* com- rades bade them au revoir for tam days they sighed, "Lucky fellows!" But they are now back again at the Front. They returned on Wednesday,. midnight. To them their "Home on Leame‘" has been a greater success than the play of that name just produced in London. In times past the Quarter-Master has not- in- frequently written to Councillor R. Irenshati graphic letters of trench life, which have found their way into "The Examiner."' To wish him "God-speed and a safe return" our repre- sentative called at his home on Tuesday after- noon and got him out of his dug-out—a cosy ,sofa—for topical ccuiverse. The Quarter-Master is just the fellow to send on leave; he does not</p>
