        <p>same regiment, enlisted a month after the out- break of war, and has served alongside his brother. When Sam was reported missing Joseph made several special journeys up the line, only to find that he-could obtain no trace of him.</p>
        <p>WOUNDED. CORPORAL G.. EVANS. Three Brothers Who Have Been Wounded. Corporal George Evans, of the South Lanca- shire Regiment, has been wounded in the arms and right leg, and has written from a Glasgow hospital to his wife, who resides at 14, Robson- street, informing -her that he is progressing favourably. Corporal Evans, who is thirty=two years or age, and a former Rylands Bros.' employe, !joined the Army in November, 1914, and has been at the Front fourteen months. Two other soldier brothers have both been wounded - LANCE-CORPL. W. H. GRIFFITIIS. His Left Arm Has Had To Bt. Amputated. Mrs. Griffiths, 22, Wakefield-street, has been informed that her son, Lance-Corporal W. H. Griffiths, Cameron Highlanders, has been wounded for a second time. When wounded before he was on sentry duty, and was shot in the head by a sniper. This time he has, been wounded in the left) arm, which has had to be amputated. Enlisting in January of last year, and going to France a few months later, Lance-Corporal Griffiths was formerly employed by the War- rington Co-operative Society, and is an old boy of Heathside School. He is 24 years of age, and was attached to the Bible Class at St. Paul's Church. RIFLEMAN F. STOCKTON.' Hit By " Whiz-Bang " _While Helping Wounded Officer. • "It is grand to be back in ' Blighty ' aftet being in the Battle of the Somme for two and a half months." This brief note came from Rifleman Frederick Stockton, King's Liverpool Regiment, who has been wounded in the leg, stomach and shoulder, and is now lying in a Leeds hospital. He was cleaning his rifle, when his officer Came up and told him to act as stretcher-bearer. The officer was the first to bu ,</p>
