        <p>• RIFLEMAN FRED STOCKTON. Wounded.</p>
        <p>hit, and .while helping to carry him away,- Rifle- man Stockton was hit by a " whlz-bang.." Formerly employed in Melia's grocery shop, Pinner's Brow, Rifleman Stockton was well- known in Warrington. He is 19 years of age. PRIVATE L. MURRAY. Wounded In The Right Arm By Shrapnel Private- Leonard Murray, South Lancashire Regiment, whose home is with his parents, Mr. and Mrs. Murray; at 49, Gladstone-street, was wounded by shrapnel in the right -arm on the 27th September. A former employe .of the Longford Wire Works, Private Murray was educated at Silver- street School, and is in his 20th year. He was called up at the outbreak of war, and has been in France eighteen -months. PRIVATE H. DAINTITH. Ambulance Driver And Policeman - . Carry The News. Mr. and Mrs. Daintith, 79, St. Mary-street, Latchford, have been informed that their son, Private Harold Daintith, Royal Fusiliers, has been wounded. In-the evening ef October 12th Private Daintith, whilst passing through, the town on an ambulance to Winwick, handed a note to the driver of the car, who handed it to a policeman, who after Wards passed it on to Daintith's brother. and in that way the family eventually became acquainted with his where- abouts. This was also the • first news of his wounding that had reached his home. Private Daintith; whO was wounded after only</p>
        <p>PRIVATE F. C. DALE. Buried Alive. days when I was sent out again to our trans- pore line." Private Dale, who is twenty years of age, was, before enlisting, employed as a junior clerk to the, Warrington Board of Guardians. He has been in the Army fourteen months, and went to the Front early last March. He has taken part in much fierce fighting during the "big push," and has had some narrow escapes. On one occasion a part of his kilt was torn off by a shell ,plinter. PRIVATE Hi COOPER. Grenadier Guardsman Wounded In Left Arm. Private Herbert Cooper, Grenadier Guards, and who formerly resided with his sister at Harson-street, is lying in a base hospital suffer- in., from a wound in the left arm, near the Enlisting in January of last year, and going to France in October, Private Cooper was a former employe of the •Richmond Gas Stove nnd Meter Co., Ltd. His age is 20 year. Re was educated at the National Schools, Church- street. Private J. R.. etioper, -serving with tn.. South Lancashire Regiment; is his brother. SAPPER V. PEACOCK. Member Of Royal Engineers Wounded In The Back. Mn: and Mrs. Oswald Peacock,. 6, Wilson. Patten-street, who have two sons now serving hand another who has been discharged, have been informed that the eldest, Sapper William Peacock, Royal Engineers, was wounded in tha</p>
