        <p>AT a Court Function in the days of Edward 111. a lady dropped her garter. The reigning Monarch gallantly handed it to her with the words "Honi soit qui mal y pense," which being translated mean " Evil be to him who evil thinks." From this incident was formed the Noble Order of the Garter, whose Knights adopted the expression as their motto.</p>
        <p>"HONI SOIT" is the title of the maddest, merriest, jolliest and wittiest Revue on tour--direct from the London Pavilion, where it ran twelve months. The exact production as staged •at the Hippodrome, Manchester, three weeks ago. " A feast of fashionable frocks, Of lacy frills, and silken socks, Of dances in a maddening whirl, • Of saucy face, and pretty girl Of witty jest, and joyous chaff, Designed to make the saddest laugh, With frolic, fun, and festive quip From POWELL, whose other name is PIP. One short week only is the visit Of' HONI SOIT'—YOU MUSTN'T MISS IT!" Hippodrome, Warrington, Mon., Oct. 23rd. MATINE E, EONIAY, ait 2.45.</p>
